en directions, so a line through a single new point can be chosen with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). The other \\(n\\) lines are kept as in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\). Thus we increase the sunny count by one. Repeating this replacement up to \\(n+1\\) times yields configurations with any number of sunny lines between the original \\(k\\) and \\(k+(n+1-n)=k+1\\).  \n\n  By starting from the base case and applying the above two operations (adding a non‑sunny line, then optionally turning it sunny), we can reach **every** integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1\\). Hence the induction is complete.\n\nTherefore, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_3\\) of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nTake the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=3 .\n\\]\n\nAll three are non‑sunny, so they give the case \\(k=0\\).  \nTo obtain a sunny line, replace any of the above lines by a line through the same point with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny and still covers that point. By replacing none, one, two or all three of the original lines we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines respectively. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following statement \\(\\mathcal P(n)\\) holds:\n\n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le