ntain all points of \\(S\\) whose sum equals \\(c\\).  \n\n   Because the sums \\(a+b\\) range from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\) without repetition, a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can cover **exactly one** point of \\(S\\).  \n   Hence the only way a non\u2011sunny line can cover more than one point of \\(S\\) is by being horizontal or vertical.\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   Suppose \\(k\\ge n-1\\). Then at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  \n   *If the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)), it can cover at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{1}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\), which is strictly less than \\(n\\) for any admissible \\(c\\).  \n   *If it is vertical* (\\(x=c\\)), a symmetric argument applies.  \n   *If it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*, it covers exactly one point of \\(S\\).  \n\n   In every case the total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the \\(n\\) lines is at most\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot 1 + 1\\cdot (n-1) = n,\n   \\]\n   but equality would require that each sunny line actually covers a distinct point of \\(S\\) and that the single non\u2011sunny line covers the remaining point.  \n   However, the distinctness of the points forces the non\u2011sunny line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (otherwise it would share a coordinate with a sunny line, contradicting the injectivity of the sum on sunny lines).  \n   A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can cover only one point, leaving at least one point of \\(S\\) uncovered\u2014a contradiction to the covering condition.\n\nThus the assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible, establishing the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one element of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\); namely \\((1,s-1), (2,s-2),\\dots\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|=n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then the map \\((a,b)\\mapsto a+b\\) is injective on the set \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) are distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) with \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\).  \nThen the vector \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) is a non\u2011zero integer vector lying on \\(\\ell\\) and satisfying \\((a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=0\\). Hence the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(S\\) having a given sum \\(a+b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nImmediate from Lemma\u202f1, because all points of \\(S\\) with the same sum lie on the same line \\(x+y=c\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) forces \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). For each admissible \\(c\\) there is precisely one lattice point of \\(S\\) with sum \\(c\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (resp. vertical line \\(x=c\\)) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n+1-c\\rfloor\\) (resp. \\(\\lfloor n+1-c\\rfloor\\)) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(y=c\\), then points of \\(S\\) on this line satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b=c\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Since \\(a\\ge1\\), there are at most \\(n+1-c\\) possibilities for \\(a\\). The vertical case is symmetric. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument (Proof by Contradiction)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and that exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-1\n\\]\n\nof them are sunny. Let \\(\\mathcal S\\subset\\mathcal L\\) be the set of sunny lines and \\(\\mathcal N=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\mathcal S\\) the set of non\u2011sunny lines; thus \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\le1\\).\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f2, each sunny line can cover **at most one** point of \\(S\\). Hence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|=0\\) then the \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\), each covering a distinct point. This would force every point of \\(S\\) to lie on a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). In particular, the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) have sums \\(2\\) and \\(3\\); the line through them has slope \\(1\\), which is allowed, but the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) is vertical, contradicting the assumption that all lines are sunny. Hence \\(|\\mathcal N|\\neq0\\).\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\); denote the unique non\u2011sunny line by \\(\\ell\\). There are three possibilities.\n\n1. **\\(\\ell\\) parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).**  \n   By Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\ell\\) covers exactly one point of \\(S\\). Consequently the sunny lines must cover the remaining \\(n-1\\) points. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point, we would need \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines, which we have. However, the point covered by \\(\\ell\\) has a sum \\(c\\). Any sunny line covering a point with the same sum would be forced to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (Lemma\u202f1), contradicting its sunniness. Hence the sunny lines cannot cover any point whose sum equals \\(c\\); that point would remain uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\n2. **\\(\\ell\\) horizontal** (\\(y=c\\)).  \n   By Lemma\u202f4, \\(\\ell\\) covers