,\\,b_{2}-b_{1})\\) satisfies \\((a_{2}-a_{1})+(b_{2}-b_{1})=0\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting sunniness. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\) having a given sum \\(a+b\\). Since the sums of points of \\(S\\) are all distinct (they run through \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)), a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\) altogether. Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nNow consider the (possible) single non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal N\\).\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), then \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) and, by the definition of \\(S\\), contains **exactly one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then a point \\((a,c)\\in S\\) must satisfy \\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n* The vertical case \\(x=c\\) is symmetric and also yields at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nIn every possible case the total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{from sunny lines})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{from a non‑sunny line parallel to }x+y=0)\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nk+(n-1)\\;(\\text{from a horizontal/vertical line}).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge n-1\\), both expressions are **strictly less than** \\(|S|=n\\). This contradicts the requirement that every point of \\(S\\) lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence the assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible, and we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Realisation of every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1 Two non‑sunny “base’’ lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}: y=1\\qquad\\text{(horizontal)} ,\\qquad\n\\ell_{v}: x=1\\qquad\\text{(vertical)} .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny.  \nThe set of points of \\(S\\) can be partitioned as  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and \\(b=1\\) we are on \\(\\ell_{h}\\); otherwise \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces one of the coordinates to be \\(1\\) when the other exceeds \\(1\\)). Consequently every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the two lines \\(\\ell_{h},\\ell_{v}\\).\n\n#### 3.2 Adding the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines  \n\nWe need \\(n-2\\) further distinct lines. Choose any \\(n-2\\) distinct points of \\(S\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,1)\\quad (i=2,3,\\dots ,n) .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) draw a line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passing through \\(P_{i}\\) and through the fixed point \\((1,1)\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = \\frac{1-1}{1-i}(x- i)=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is the same as \\(\\ell_{h}\\) when \\(i=1\\); for \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{1-1}{1-i}=0\\) only when \\(i=1\\), so for \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{1-1}{1-i}=0\\) – actually this gives a horizontal line again, which would not be distinct.  \nTo obtain distinct lines we modify the construction: for each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{1}{i-1}(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,1)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{1}{i-1}\\).  \nSince the slopes \\(\\frac{1}{i-1}\\) are all different, the lines \\(\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) are distinct and also distinct from \\(\\ell_{h}\\) and \\(\\ell_{v}\\).\n\nNow we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\\[\n\\{\\ell_{h},\\ell_{v},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3 Controlling the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is sunny iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n* \\(\\ell_{h}\\) (slope \\(0\\)) and \\(\\ell_{v}\\) (vertical) are non‑sunny.  \n* For each \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope of \\(\\ell_{i}\\) equals \\(\\frac{1}{i-1}\\).  \n  This slope equals \\(-1\\) only when \\(i-1=-1\\), i.e. \\(i=0\\), which never occurs; it equals \\(0\\) only when \\(i=1\\), also impossible; it is never infinite.  \n  Hence **all** lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus the above construction yields a configuration with **\\(k=n-2\\)** sunny lines.\n\nTo obtain a configuration with a smaller prescribed number \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)), we simply replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) by additional non‑sunny lines (for instance, extra horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots\\) or extra vertical lines \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\)).  \nBecause the covering condition is already satisfied by \\(\\ell_{h}\\) and \\(\\ell_{v}\\), these replacements do not affect the covering property, and the total number of sunny lines becomes exactly \\(k\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\) (Section 2) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Section 3), we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
