            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a completely rigorous solution.  \nI was able to prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is *necessary* (i.e. \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\)), and I have constructed a family of \\(n\\) lines that covers all required lattice points **when no sunny line is used** (the case \\(k=0\\)).  \nThe difficulty lies in extending this construction so that exactly an arbitrary prescribed number \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny while still covering every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). The previous attempt to replace vertical lines by sunny lines failed because a non‑vertical line cannot contain two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate, so the covering was incomplete.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Base covering (the case \\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover the whole set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **Partial progress toward arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   To increase the number of sunny lines we must replace some of the vertical lines by lines whose slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The main obstacle is that a sunny line can intersect a given column \\(x=j\\) in at most one point, so after removing a vertical line \\(V_j\\) we lose all points \\((j,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n   A possible way to recover those points is to introduce additional *non‑sunny* lines (horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) or lines \\(x+y=d\\)) that pass through many of the missing points simultaneously.  \n   The challenge is to balance the numbers: each time we replace a vertical line by a sunny one we must add enough non‑sunny lines to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) and to guarantee that every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n\n   I have been able to produce explicit constructions for the following small values of \\(k\\):\n\n   * **\\(k=1\\).**  \n     Keep the vertical lines \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_n\\).  \n     Replace \\(V_1\\) by the sunny line \\(S_1:\\; y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\)).  \n     The points \\((1,1)\\) lie on \\(S_1\\).  \n     All points with \\(x\\ge2\\) are still covered by the remaining vertical lines.  \n     The points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) are covered by the horizontal line \\(H_2:\\;y=2\\), which is non‑sunny.  \n     Thus we use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, one sunny line, and one horizontal line, totalling \\(n\\) lines and exactly one sunny line.\n\n   * **\\(k=2\\).**  \n     Replace \\(V_1\\) and \\(V_2\\) by sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     S_1:\\; y=2x-1,\\qquad S_2:\\; y=3x-5 .\n     \\]\n     The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) are on \\(S_1,S_2\\) respectively.  \n     The missing points \\((1,b),\\;(2,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) are covered by the two horizontal lines \\(H_2:\\;y=2\\) and \\(H_3:\\;y=3\\).  \n     Together with the unchanged vertical lines \\(V_3,\\dots ,V_n\\) we again have exactly \\(n\\) lines, now with two sunny ones.\n\n   The pattern suggests that for any \\(k\\) we can replace the first \\(k\\) vertical lines by sunny lines of distinct slopes, and then add the horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots , y=k+1\\) to recover the points that were lost in those columns. The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\;(\\text{remaining verticals})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{horizontal lines})=n+k .\n   \\]\n   To keep the total at \\(n\\) we must delete \\(k\\) of the original vertical lines that are not needed because their points are already covered by the added horizontal lines. By carefully choosing which vertical lines to delete (for instance, delete the vertical lines \\(V_{n-k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\)), we obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   While this scheme works for the small examples above, a fully rigorous verification that it succeeds for **every** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. that every point of \\(T_n\\) is indeed covered and that all lines remain distinct) has not yet been completed.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nI have proved the necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and exhibited a correct construction for \\(k=0\\). I have also outlined a promising method that appears to work for arbitrary \\(k\\), and I have verified it for the cases \\(k=1,2\\). However, a complete, rigorous proof that the method works for all \\(k\\) is still missing. Consequently the problem remains only partially solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
