------------------------------------\n### 2.  Families of lines\n\nDefine three families of lines.\n\n* **Sunny family** (indexed by \\(s\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\))  \n\n  \\[\n  S_s:\\;y=sx-(s-1).\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of \\(S_s\\) equals \\(s\\); consequently \\(S_s\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(s\\neq0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (finite slope), and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(s\\neq-1\\)). Hence every \\(S_s\\) is sunny.\n\n* **Horizontal family** (indexed by \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\))\n\n  \\[\n  H_c:\\;y=c .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(H_c\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.\n\n* **Diagonal family** (indexed by \\(d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\))\n\n  \\[\n  D_d:\\;x+y=d .\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(D_d\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), hence non‑sunny.\n\nAll three families consist of distinct lines, and a line from one family never coincides with a line from another family because they have different slopes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsider the following set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k}_{k\\text{ sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{H_{2},H_{3},\\dots ,H_{k+1}}_{k\\text{ horizontal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}}_{n-k\\text{ diagonal}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk+k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the first block consists of sunny lines, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Proof that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 1,\\qquad b\\ge 1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWe distinguish three mutually exclusive cases.\n\n#### Case 1.