) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=a\\cdot a-(a-1)=a^{2}-a+1=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\le n\\) and we have the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) are covered by a sunny line.  \n   For \\(a>k\\) we have \\(a\\ge k+1\\); then the diagonal line \\(D_{a+1}\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{D_{k+1},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) because \\(a+1\\ge k+1\\) and \\(a+1\\le n+1\\) by (1)) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=a+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a,1)\\in D_{a+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point with \\(b=1\\) is covered.\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   For any such point the sum \\(s=a+b\\) satisfies \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) by (1).  \n   If \\(s\\ge k+1\\) then the diagonal line \\(D_s\\) is present in our family (its index lies in \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\)) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   If \\(s\\le k\\) then \\(b\\le k-1\\). In this case \\(a\\le s- b\\le k-b\\le k-1\\), so \\(a\\le k-1\\). Consequently \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) imply \\(a\\le k-1\\) and \\(b\\le k-1\\). For such a pair we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (a-1)\\cdot\\frac{b-1}{a-1}+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a-1\\) and \\(b-1\\) are positive integers with \\((a-1)+(b-1)=s-2\\le k-2\\), the quotient \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k-1\\). Denote this integer by \\(t\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   b = t a-(t-1),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\) (recall (2)). Since \\(1\\le t\\le k-1\\), this sunny line belongs to our collection. Thus every point with \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le k\\) is also covered.\n\n   Combining the two sub\u2011cases, every lattice point satisfying (1) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D_{k+1},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and together they cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   The obvious necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) (we cannot have more sunny lines than the total number of lines) therefore is also sufficient.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]"

