tfrac12 x+1,\\;y= -\\tfrac13 x+3\\), each passing through a different point of \\(T_3\\).  \n* \\(k=2\\): take two sunny lines covering \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) and a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) covering \\((1,1)\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): take the sunny line \\(y=2x\\) covering \\((1,2)\\), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covering \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible set is \\(\\{1,2,3\\}=\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**IH**) For the set \\(T_n\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n> \\[\n> \\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}.\n> \\]\n\nWe shall prove the analogous statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Lower bound for \\(n+1\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{*}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(L_{*}\\). Since \\(L_{*}\\) is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\), it **cannot** be sunny. Consequently at least one line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) that is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions must contain a point of \\(L_{*}\\); otherwise the points of \\(L_{*}\\) would be uncovered.\n\nRemove \\(\\ell\\) from the family and delete all points of \\(L_{*}\\) from the set of required points. The remaining points are precisely \\(T_n\\) (because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) together with the removal of the layer \\(a+b=n+1\\) leaves the inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\)). Hence the remaining \\(n\\) lines \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. Since \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny, the original family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)+0 = (n+1)-2\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. Thus any admissible family for \\(n+1\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\ns(\\mathcal L)\\ge (n+1)-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Constructions attaining the three extremal values\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{(n+1)-2,\\;(n+1)-1,\\;n+1\\}\\), a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 6.1  Construction for \\(k=n+1\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nTake the construction for \\(k=n\\) given by the inductive hypothesis for \\(T_n\\); it consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\). Add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{*}: x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny, but we replace it by a **sunny** line that still covers the points of the layer \\(a+b=n+1\\). For example, for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) the point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) lies on the layer. Choose a sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) that is not parallel to any forbidden direction, e.g.  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac12 x + \\frac32 + n .\n\\]\n\nThis line meets the layer in at least one point and, because its slope is \\(-\\frac12\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), it is sunny. Together with the original \\(n\\) sunny lines we now have \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(T_{n+1}\\). (Distinctness is guaranteed because the new line does not coincide with any of the previous ones; its slope is different from the slopes of the former lines, which were all distinct by the inductive construction.)\n\nThus \\(k=n+1\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 6.2  Construction for \\(k=n\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny line)\n\nStart from the configuration for \\(T_n\\) that uses exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line (which exists by the inductive hypothesis). Add a new line that covers the whole new layer \\(a+b=n+1\\). Choose the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{*}: x+y=n+1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the forbidden direction and therefore non\u2011sunny. The added line is distinct from all previous ones because its intercept differs. The total number of sunny lines remains \\(n-1\\), i.e. \\(k=n\\) for the case \\(n+1\\).  \n\nHence \\(k=n\\) is realizable.\n\n#### 6.3  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nTake the configuration for \\(T_n\\) that has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines and two non\u2011sunny lines (again guaranteed by the inductive hypothesis). Add a third non\u2011sunny line covering the new layer. As in the previous step we may use the line \\(x+y=n+1\\). The resulting family has  \n\n\\[\n(n-2) \\text{ sunny lines } + 0 = (n+1)-2\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\nThus all three values are attainable for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Completion of the induction\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The lower bound (1) holds for \\(n+1\\); therefore any admissible family must have at least \\((n+1)-2\\) sunny lines.\n* The three constructions above produce families with exactly \\((n+1)-2\\), \\((n+1)-1\\) and \\((n+1)\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of possible numbers of sunny lines for \\(n+1\\) is precisely \\(\\{(n+1)-2,\\;(n+1)-1,\\;n+1\\}\\).\n\nSince the statement is true for the base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step is valid, the claim holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Final answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
