le n+1\\) together with the positivity of \\(a\\) and \\(b_{0}\\) implies that at most two integer values of \\(a\\) satisfy the bound (the extreme values \\(a=1\\) and \\(a=n+1-b_{0}\\)).  \nHence at most two points of \\(S\\) lie on a horizontal line.\n\nThe same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\) (by symmetry) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIndeed, on \\(x+y=c\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\); the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots\\).  \nSince \\(c\\ge 2\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\), there are at most two such solutions that also satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Main Argument (Proof by Contradiction)\n\nAssume, for a fixed \\(n\\ge 3\\), that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **at least three sunny lines**.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S\\subseteq\\mathcal L\n\\]\n\nbe the set of sunny lines; by assumption \\(|\\mathcal S|\\ge 3\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 each line in \\(\\mathcal S\\) covers at most one point of \\(S\\); therefore the three sunny lines together cover at most three points of \\(S\\).  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\mathcal S\n\\]\n\nbe the set of non‑sunny lines; \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-|\\mathcal S|\\le n-3\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) covers at most two points of \\(S\\).  \nConsequently the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\;+\\;2\\,(n-3)=2n-3.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHowever, by (1) the set \\(S\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>2n-3,\n\\]\n\nbecause  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-\\bigl(2n-3\\bigr)=\\frac{n^{2}-n-4n+6}{2}\n=\\frac{n^{2}-5n+6}{2}\n=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) would be unable to cover all points of \\(S\\), contradic