            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Counting observation (pigeon‑hole)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is *not* sunny, then it is of one of the three forms  \n\n* \\(y=c\\) (horizontal);\n* \\(x=c\\) (vertical);\n* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nFor a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) on it are \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line.  \nFor a line \\(x+y=c\\) the points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points occur (the maximum is attained for \\(c=n+1\\)).  \n\nThus any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nConsequently, if a family of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines covers \\(S_n\\), then  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_n|}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince we are allowed to use \\(n\\) lines, condition (1) imposes no restriction on the number of sunny lines; it only guarantees that a covering is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_s\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\); there are \\(s-1\\) such points.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they cover all of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains at least one non‑sunny line \\(L\\).  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) (different from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)) such that  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is needed, there exists a point  \n\n\\[\nP\\in S_n\\cap L\\quad\\text{that lies on no line of }\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nChoose any point \\(Q\\in S_n\\) with \\(Q\\neq P\\) (such a point exists because \\(|S_n|>1\\)).  \nConsider the line \\(L' = PQ\\).\n\nIf the slope of \\(L'\\) were \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), then \\(Q\\) would lie on a line parallel to one of the forbidden directions. That line would be a non‑sunny line distinct from \\(L\\) (since it contains \\(Q\\neq P\\)), contradicting (2) because \\(P\\) would then be covered by two lines. Hence the slope of \\(L'\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); \\(L'\\) is sunny.\n\n\\(L'\\) is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\): it contains the point \\(P\\) which, by (2), belongs to no other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Therefore no line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can coincide with \\(L'\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered:  \n* \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(L'\\);  \n* every other point was already covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\) (including \\(Q\\), which is still covered because it lies on \\(L'\\)). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number of sunny lines\n\nWe start with the non‑sunny covering \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) of step 2, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply the Replacement Lemma to any non‑sunny line of the current family; each application replaces one non‑sunny line by a sunny line while preserving a covering of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nAfter performing the replacement \\(k\\) times we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S_n\\) and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince the lemma can be applied at most \\(n\\) times (there are only \\(n\\) lines), we obtain configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nNo configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.  \nThe constructions above show that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive can be realized.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
