ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=c\\}\\), i.e. the points of \\(T_n\\) whose\ncoordinates sum to \\(c\\).  The sets for different \\(c\\) are disjoint and\ntheir union is precisely \\(T_n\\).  Each \\(D_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\),\nhence non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a diagonal by one sunny line).\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) and choose a point\n\\((a_0,b_0)\\in D_t\\) (so \\(a_0+b_0=t\\) and \\(1\\le a_0\\le t-1\\)).\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y-b_0=m(x-a_0),\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) is an irrational number.  Define  \n\n\\[\nH_{b_0}:\\;y=b_0,\\qquad V_{a_0}:\\;x=a_0 .\n\\]\n\nThen the three lines \\(\\{S_t,H_{b_0},V_{a_0}\\}\\) are distinct, exactly one\nof them (\\(S_t\\)) is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(D_t\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}\\bigr)\\subseteq\n\\bigl(S_t\\cup H_{b_0}\\cup V_{a_0}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(m\\) is irrational, the line \\(S_t\\) contains no lattice point\nother than \\((a_0,b_0)\\); indeed, if \\((x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) satisfied\n\\(y-b_0=m(x-a_0)\\) with irrational \\(m\\), then \\(x=a_0\\) and consequently\n\\(y=b_0\\).  Hence \\(S_t\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}=\\{(a_0,b_0)\\}\\).\n\nEvery other point of \\(D_t\\) has either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as\n\\((a_0,b_0)\\) or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate as \\((a_0,b_0)\\); therefore it\nlies on \\(V_{a_0}\\) or on \\(H_{b_0}\\), respectively.  The three lines are\nclearly distinct, and \\(S_t\\) is not parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions, so it is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Increasing the number of sunny lines).\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  If \\(\\mathcal L\\) still\ncontains a diagonal \\(D_t\\) from the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) of Lemma 1, then we\ncan construct a new family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 2 to the diagonal \\(D_t\\).  Remove \\(D_t\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\) and\nadd the three lines \\(\\{S_t,H_{b_0},V_{a_0}\\}\\).  Because \\(H_{b_0}\\) and\n\\(V_{a_0}\\) already belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) only if they coincide with a\npreviously used horizontal or vertical line, we choose the point\n\\((a_0,b_0)\\) so that neither \\(x=a_0\\) nor \\(y=b_0\\) has appeared before;\nthis is always possible while at most \\(k\\le n-3\\) diagonals have been\nreplaced.  Consequently the three added lines are all new, the total\nnumber of lines remains \\(n\\), and exactly one new sunny line (\\(S_t\\))\nhas been introduced, raising the count from \\(k\\) to \\(k+1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition (Achievable values of \\(k\\)).\n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions\nof the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the family \\(\\mathcal D\\) of Lemma 1, which has \\(k=0\\).  \nRepeatedly apply Lemma 3 to replace a diagonal by the three‑line\nconstruction of Lemma 2.  Each application increases the number of sunny\nlines by one while preserving the total of \\(n\\) lines and the covering\nproperty.  The process can be performed at most \\(n-2\\) times, because after\n\\(n-2\\) replacements only two original diagonals remain; those two are\nnecessary to cover the extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) and cannot be\neliminated without losing distinctness of the lines.  Hence we obtain\nconfigurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every\n\\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nWe have proved rigorously that all integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2}\n\\]\n\nare attainable.  The existence (or impossibility) of configurations with\n\\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains an open question."
