m \\(k\\) to \\(k+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition (Attainable values of \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions\nof the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the family \\(\\mathcal D\\) of Lemma\u202f1, which has \\(k=0\\).\nRepeatedly apply Lemma\u202f3 to a diagonal that has not yet been replaced.\nEach application increases the number of sunny lines by one while keeping\nthe total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) and preserving the covering of\n\\(T_n\\).  The process can be performed at most \\(n-2\\) times: after\n\\(n-2\\) replacements only two original diagonals remain, and those two\nare necessary to cover the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) (they lie on\ndifferent diagonals and cannot both be covered by the horizontal and\nvertical lines introduced in the last replacement without duplicating a\nline).  Hence we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for\nevery \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nAll non\u2011negative integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2}\n\\]\n\nare realizable as the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  The existence of configurations with\n\\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains open."
