onsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_s\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\); there are \\(s-1\\) such points.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they cover all of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma (rigorous)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\). Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) not belonging to \\(\\mathcal L\\) such that  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Existence of a uniquely covered point.**  \n   Because \\(L\\) is part of a covering, if every point of \\(S_n\\cap L\\) were also on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\), then \\(L\\) could be removed without destroying the covering, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non\u2011sunny line that we intend to replace. Hence there exists  \n\n   \\[\n   P\\in S_n\\cap L\\quad\\text{such that }P\\notin\\bigcup_{M\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}}M .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Choosing a point outside \\(L\\).**  \n   From the counting observation, a non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Since \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), the set \\(S_n\\setminus L\\) is non\u2011empty. Moreover it contains at least one point different from \\(P\\). Choose  \n\n   \\[\n   Q\\in S_n\\setminus L,\\qquad Q\\neq P .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The line \\(L'=PQ\\) is sunny.**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(L'\\) had slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Then \\(L'\\) would be a non\u2011sunny line. Because \\(Q\\in L'\\) and \\(Q\\notin L\\), this non\u2011sunny line would be distinct f