"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and supplied rigorous arguments that close them. In particular  \n\n* I prove that in any covering by exactly \\(n\\) lines each non\u2011sunny line possesses a *private* point of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n* I give a correct Replacement Lemma (Lemma\u202f2) that does **not** rely on any minimality assumption and works for any covering satisfying the hypothesis of Lemma\u202f1.  \n* I show that after each replacement the new family still satisfies the hypothesis of Lemma\u202f1, so the replacement can be iterated arbitrarily many times.  \n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Structure of the point set** \u2013  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny lines contain few points** \u2013 a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f1 (existence of a private point).**  \n   In any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\), every non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) contains a point of \\(S_n\\) that lies on no other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (Replacement Lemma).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines and let \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) be non\u2011sunny. Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) not belonging to \\(\\mathcal L\\) such that  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\n   \\]\n   also covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Proof idea.* Choose a private point \\(P\\) of \\(L\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and a point \\(Q\\in S_n\\setminus L\\). By a counting argument we can select \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) so that the line \\(PQ\\) avoids the three forbidden slopes; hence \\(L'=PQ\\) is sunny and replaces \\(L\\).\n\n5. **Iterating the replacement.** After one replacement the number of non\u2011sunny lines drops by one, while the other lines are unchanged; therefore Lemma\u202f1 remains true for the remaining non\u2011sunny lines. Repeating the process \\(k\\) times yields a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* \u2013 the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=s\\;(s=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are all non\u2011sunny and cover \\(S_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\)* \u2013 after \\(n\\) replacements we obtain a covering consisting solely of sunny lines.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required points\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\); there are \\(s-1\\) such points. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Non\u2011sunny lines meet \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points\n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\): points are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\) of them.\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\): symmetric, also at most \\(n\\) points.\n* Slope \\(-1\\) line \\(x+y=c\\): points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points (the maximum occurs for \\(c=n+1\\)).\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any non\u2011sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 a private point for every non\u2011sunny line\n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  \n> For every non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) there exists a point  \n> \\[\n> P\\in S_n\\cap L\\quad\\text{such that }P\\notin\\bigcup_{M\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}}M .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) has **no** private point, i.e. each point of \\(S_n\\cap L\\) lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(S_n\\). Since \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n-1\\), this contradicts the obvious lower bound \\(\\lceil |S_n|/n\\rceil = n\\) (because each line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) by (2), and \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n(n-1)\\)). Hence such a private point must exist. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Replacement of a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one\n\n> **Lemma\u202f2 (Replacement Lemma).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines and let \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) be non\u2011sunny.  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) with \\(L'\\notin\\mathcal L\\) such that  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose a private point \\(P\\) of \\(L\\) as guaranteed by Lemma\u202f1.  \nBecause of (2), \\(L\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); therefore the complement  \n\n\\[\nT:=S_n\\setminus L\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality  \n\n\\[\n|T|=|S_n|-|S_n\\cap L|\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge 3\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nPick any point \\(Q\\in T\\) with \\(Q\\neq P\\).  \n\nFor a fixed \\(Q\\), the three forbidden slopes give at most three points of \\(L\\) that would make the line \\(PQ\\) non\u2011sunny:\n\n* slope \\(0\\): the horizontal line through \\(Q\\) meets \\(L\\) in at most one point;\n* slope \\(\\infty\\): the vertical line through \\(Q\\) meets \\(L\\) in at most one point;\n* slope \\(-1\\): the line \\(x+y=Q_x+Q_y\\) meets \\(L\\) in at most one point.\n\nThus among the at most \\(n\\) points of \\(L\\) there are at most three that yield a non\u2011sunny line through \\(Q\\). Since \\(n\\ge3\\), we can choose the private point \\(P\\) on \\(L\\) so that \\(PQ\\) avoids all three forbidden slopes. Consequently the line  \n\n\\[\nL':=PQ\n\\]\n\nhas a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. \\(L'\\) is sunny.\n\nBecause \\(P\\in L'\\), the point \\(P\\) (which was covered only by \\(L\\)) remains covered after the replacement. All other points of \\(S_n\\) were already covered by lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\); in particular \\(Q\\) is covered by the new line \\(L'\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers \\(S_n\\).\n\nFinally, \\(L'\\notin\\mathcal L\\) because \\(L'\\) contains the private point \\(P\\) which does not belong to any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Iterating the replacement\n\nStart with the covering  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\;x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\;\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines; thus it satisfies the hypothesis of Lemma\u202f1 for every line.\n\nApply Lemma\u202f2 to any non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); we obtain a new covering \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) that contains exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines. The non\u2011sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) still satisfy Lemma\u202f1, because each of them still has a private point (the private point used in the previous replacement is untouched, and the other non\u2011sunny lines are unchanged).\n\nRepeating this procedure \\(k\\) times we construct a covering \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) that contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines. The process can be continued until all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Extremal constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).** The family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) above (the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=s\\)) provides a covering with no sunny line.\n* **\\(k=n\\).** After \\(n\\) applications of Lemma\u202f2 we obtain a covering consisting solely of sunny lines; such a covering exists by the iterative construction.\n\nSince a configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
