M\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}}M .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a non‑sunny line \\(L\\) has **no** private point; i.e. each point of \\(S_n\\cap L\\) lies on at least one other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Counting incidences (pairs \\((\\text{point},\\text{line})\\) with the point on the line) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{total incidences}\\;\\ge\\;2|S_n| = n(n+1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, by (2) each line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences, so\n\n\\[\n\\text{total incidences}\\;\\le\\; n\\cdot n = n^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n(n+1)>n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge1\\), (4) and (5) cannot both hold. The contradiction shows that every non‑sunny line must possess a private point, establishing (3). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma 2 – replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one\n\n> **Lemma 2 (Replacement Lemma).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines and let \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) be non‑sunny.  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) with \\(L'\\notin\\mathcal L\\) such that  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose a private point \\(P\\) of \\(L\\) guaranteed by Lemma 1.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nT:=S_n\\setminus L .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) we have \\(|S_n\\cap L|\\le n\\); using (1),\n\n\\[\n|T|=|S_n|-|S_n\\cap L|\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nFor a fixed point \\(P\\) the three forbidden slopes (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) determine three lines through \\(P\\). Each of these three lines meets \\(T\\) in at most one point, because any two distinct points of \\(T\\) lie on a line of slope different from the three forbidden values (otherwise that line would be non‑sunny and would contain at least two points of \\(T\\), contradicting (2)). Consequently at most three points of \\(T\\) are *bad* for the purpose of keeping the new line sunny.\n\nSince \\(|T|\\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge 6\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\), there exists a point  \n\n\\[\nQ\\in T\\setminus\\{\\text{the at most three bad points}\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(L':=PQ\\). By construction the slope of \\(L'\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L'\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe point \\(P\\) (which was covered only by \\(L\\)) now lies on \\(L'\\), and \\(Q\\) (which was not on \\(L\\)) also lies on \\(L'\\). All other points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered by the unchanged lines \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers \\(S_n\\). Moreover \\(L'\\notin\\mathcal L\\) because it contains the private point \\(P\\) that belongs to no line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). One possible covering by three lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\,y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=3\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the line \\(y=1\\) is non‑sunny. Its private point is \\((2,1)\\). Choose \\(Q=(1,2)\\in S_3\\setminus y=1\\). The line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), so we do not use it. Instead we replace \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (the latter point is not in \\(S_3\\) but the line is defined). Explicitly, take  \n\n\\[\nL' :\\; y = x-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and passes through the private point \\((2,1)\\). The new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x+y=3,\\;y=x-1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all three points of \\(S_3\\) and contains exactly one sunny line. Hence the replacement lemma holds for \\(n=3\\) as well.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Iterating the replacement\n\nStart with the covering  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\;x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\;\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines; thus it satisfies the hypothesis of Lemma 1 for every line.\n\nApply Lemma 2 to any non‑sunny line of