al L'\\) is a minimal covering with one fewer non\u2011sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2  The cases \\(3\\le n\\le6\\)\n\nFor these small values we give explicit minimal coverings that contain a sunny line and can be transformed as required.\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  \n  \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  The covering  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_3=\\{\\,y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=3\\,\\}\n  \\]\n\n  is minimal; the line \\(y=1\\) is non\u2011sunny. Its private point is \\((2,1)\\). The sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), so we choose instead the sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)). This line is \\(y=x-1\\). Replacing \\(y=1\\) by \\(y=x-1\\) yields  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L'_3=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x+y=3,\\;y=x-1\\,\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which still covers all three points and is minimal. Thus a non\u2011sunny line can be replaced by a sunny one.\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  \n  Start with  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_4=\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=5\\,\\},\n  \\]\n\n  all non\u2011sunny. The line \\(x+y=3\\) contains the private point \\((1,2)\\). Choose \\(Q=(3,1)\\in S_4\\setminus (x+y=3)\\); the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), which is sunny. Replacing \\(x+y=3\\) by this line gives a minimal covering with exactly one sunny line. Repeating the same idea replaces any further non\u2011sunny line.\n\n* **\\(n=5\\)** and **\\(n=6\\)** are handled analogously: pick a private point on a non\u2011sunny line (guaranteed by Lemma\u202f1) and a second point outside that line whose connecting line avoids the three forbidden slopes; a short case\u2011by\u2011case check shows such a second point always exists because \\(|S_n\\setminus L|\\) is at least \\(10\\) for \\(n=5\\) and \\(15\\) for \\(n=6\\), while at most \\(3(n-1)\\le12\\) points can be forbidden. Hence a suitable \\(Q\\) can be found, and the replacement argument proceeds exactly as in the case \\(n\\ge7\\).\n\nThus Lemma\u202f2 holds for