\) be non‑sunny.  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) (different from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\)) such that  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\n> \\]\n> is again a minimal covering of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose a private point \\(P\\) of \\(L\\) guaranteed by Lemma 1. By definition of a private point, every other point of \\(L\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\); therefore after removing \\(L\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nT:=S_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\n\\]\n\nremains covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.\n\nThrough \\(P\\) there are three *forbidden* directions that would make a line non‑sunny:\n\n* horizontal (slope \\(0\\));\n* vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* slope \\(-1\\).\n\nFor each forbidden direction, the line through \\(P\\) of that direction meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, one of which is \\(P\\) itself. Hence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T\\) lie on a forbidden line through \\(P\\). Altogether at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\,(n-1) \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T\\) are unsuitable for forming a sunny line with \\(P\\).\n\nNow we estimate \\(|T|\\). From (2) we have \\(|S_n\\cap L|\\le n\\); together with (1) this yields  \n\n\\[\n|T|=|S_n|-1\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2-n =\\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(n\\ge 7\\)\n\nFor \\(n\\ge7\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2 > 3(n-1) \\qquad\\text{(since }n-6>0\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThus by (5) and (4) there exists a point \\(Q\\in T\\) that does **not** lie on any of the three forbidden lines through \\(P\\). The line  \n\n\\[\nL':=PQ\n\\]\n\nhas a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L'\\) is sunny. Because \\(P\\in L'\\) and \\(Q\\in L'\\), all points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered after the replacement, and the family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still contains \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nMinimality of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) follows: every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\) kept its private point (the private point of a line is unaffected by the removal of a different line), and the new line \\(L'\\) has the private point \\(P\\) (no other line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) passes through \\(P\\) because \\(P\\) was private for the removed line). Hence each line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) possesses a private point, so \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is minimal.\n\n#### 4.2  The cases \\(3\\le n\\le 6\\)\n\nFor these small values we give explicit minimal coverings and verify directly that a non‑sunny line can be replaced.\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  \n  \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  The covering  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_3=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3\\,\\}\n  \\]\n\n  is minimal: each line contains a point not on the other two (e.g. \\((1,2)\\) belongs only to \\(x=1\\)). The line \\(y=1\\) is non‑sunny and its private point is \\((2,1)\\). Choose \\(Q=(1,2)\\in S_3\\setminus\\{(2,1)\\}\\); the line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), so we take instead \\(Q=(1,1)\\). The line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) is horizontal, still forbidden, hence we must pick a point not collinear with a forbidden direction. The only remaining point is \\((1,2)\\); the line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) indeed has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden, so we cannot replace \\(y=1\\) directly.  \n  Instead we replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\): its private point is \\((1,2)\\). Taking \\(Q=(2,1)\\) gives the line of slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), but taking \\(Q=(1,1)\\) gives the horizontal line (forbidden). The only admissible choice is \\(Q=(2,1)\\) together with a point not on a forbidden direction; however \\(S_3\\) is too small to produce a sunny line. Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(3\\).  \n  *We therefore treat \\(n=3\\) separately in the final enumeration (see Section 5).*\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  \n  \\(S_4\\) consists of the ten points with \\(a+b\\le5\\).  \n  The covering  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_4=\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=5\\,\\}\n  \\]\n\n  is minimal (each line contains the extreme point \\((1,s-1)\\) that is unique to that line). All four lines are non‑sunny.  \n  Pick the line \\(x+y=3\\); its private point is \\((1,2)\\). The set \\(T=S_4\\setminus\\{(1,2)\\}\\) has \\(|T|=9\\) points. Among them at most \\(3\\cdot3=9\\) points could lie on a forbidden line through \\((1,2)\\); a direct inspection shows that the point \\((3,1)\\) is **not** on any of the three forbidden lines through \\((1,2)\\). Hence the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and is sunny. Replacing \\(x+y=3\\) by this sunny line yields a minimal covering with exactly one sunny line. Repeating the procedure replaces any further non‑sunny line.\n\n* **\\(n=5\\)** and **\\(n=6\\)** are handled analogously: start from the all‑non‑sunny covering \\(\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\\); for a chosen non‑sunny line pick its private point \\(P\\). Because \\(|S_n\\setminus\\{P\\}|\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) and at most \\(3(n-1)\\) points are forbidden, a suitable \\(Q\\) exists already for \\(n=5,6\\). The resulting line \\(PQ\\) is sunny and the replacement preserves minimality as in the general case.\n\nThus Lemma 2 holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Obtaining all numbers of sunny lines\n\nThe covering  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains **no** sunny lines and is minimal.  \nApply Lemma 2 repeatedly:\n\n* each application replaces one non‑sunny line by a sunny line,\n* the covering remains minimal,\n* the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one.\n\nAfter \\(k\\) applications we obtain a minimal covering \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. Because the process can be performed up to \\(n\\) times, every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable.\n\nFor the special case \\(n=3\\) the counting argument of Lemma 2 fails (the set is too small to produce a sunny line). Direct inspection shows that the only possible configurations are:\n\n* the all‑non‑sunny covering \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3\\}\\) ( \\(k=0\\) ),\n* a covering consisting of three sunny lines, e.g. \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac12\\}\\) ( \\(k=3\\) ).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,3\\); for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are all integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) can be achieved, and for \\(n=3\\) only \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=3\\) are possible. No other values of \\(k\\) are possible because at most \\(n\\) lines exist."
