onal line \\(\\ell_c\\) (i.e. a line of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line contained two distinct points of the same \\(\\ell_c\\), the segment joining those two points would have slope \\(-1\\); consequently the whole line would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\nConsequently a sunny line can replace at most **two** of the diagonal lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\): the two diagonal lines that contain the (at most) two points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the sunny line.  \n\nLet us start from the full diagonal family \\(\\mathcal D\\) (which already covers \\(T_n\\)).  \nIf we introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines, we must delete at least \\(2k\\) diagonal lines in order to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\). Hence at least \\(n-2k\\) diagonal lines must remain. Because the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nn-2k\\ge 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 3.1.  Replacing pairs of diagonal lines by sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \nLet \\(\\sigma_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  \nA direct computation gives\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\; y-1 = \\frac{1-i}{i-1}\\,(x-i)\\qquad (i\\ge 2),\n\\]\nand for \\(i=1\\) we obtain the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIn every case the slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe two diagonal lines that contain \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}= \\{x+y=i+1\\},\\qquad \\ell_{i}'=\\{x+y=i+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nRemove \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(\\ell_i'\\) from the family \\(\\mathcal D\\) and insert \\(