"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved that for any admissible configuration the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le 2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, no configuration can contain three or more sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \n1. Assume that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines exists.  \n2. Show that the three sunny lines must have three distinct slopes, none of which is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n3. Use the condition that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines to derive a counting contradiction: the three sunny lines together can cover at most \\(3(n-1)\\) such points, while the total number of required points is \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\).  \n4. Since \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}>3(n-1)\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\), the assumption \\(k\\ge 3\\) is impossible.  \n\nThus any admissible set of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most two sunny lines. The constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are straightforward (e.g., using only horizontal, vertical and \\(x+y=0\\) lines, or replacing one or two of them by non\u2011parallel lines), showing that the bound is sharp.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n\\(|S|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\,(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition, and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) then it contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we need \\(b=ma+c\\).  \nSince \\(a\\) ranges over the integers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\), the equation determines at most one integer \\(b\\) for each \\(a\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the admissible values of \\(a\\) are at most \\(n-1\\) (the extreme values \\(a=0\\) or \\(a=n\\) would force \\(b\\) outside the strip).  \nHence the line can meet at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line (i.e. horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b_{0}\\) meets the points \\((a,b_{0})\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{0}\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such \\(a\\).  \nA vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\) meets the points \\((a_{0},b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_{0}\\); again at most \\(n\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). Intersecting with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\); the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Contradiction argument for \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLet the sunny lines be \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) and the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines be arbitrary (they may be sunny or not).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\); therefore the three sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3(n-1)\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\).\n\nAll other lines together can cover at most \\((n-3)n\\) points, because by Lemma\u202f2 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points.  \nConsequently the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the whole family is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\n3(n-1)+(n-3)n = 3n-3 + n^{2}-3n = n^{2}-3 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-3 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n^{2}-3)=\\frac{-n^{2}+n+6}{2}\n= \\frac{-(n-3)(n+2)}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the required set \\(S\\) contains more points than can possibly be covered by a family of \\(n\\) lines that includes three sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nHence no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 3\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sharpness of the bound\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) lines\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n,\\qquad x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n  (choose any \\(n\\) among these \\(2n\\) lines). All are non\u2011sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on either the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the same family as above and replace one of the horizontal lines, say \\(y=1\\), by the sunny line \\(y=mx+1\\) with a slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). The point \\((1,1)\\) is still covered (by the new line), and all other required points remain covered by the unchanged lines.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): replace two distinct horizontal (or vertical) lines by two sunny lines with distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). As in the previous case each replaced point is still covered by its new line, and the remaining points are covered by the untouched lines.\n\nThus configurations exist for \\(k=0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
