frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is horizontal, vertical, or has slope \\(-1\\) then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=b_{0}\\): admissible points are \\((a,b_{0})\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{0}\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such \\(a\\).  \n* Vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\): admissible points are \\((a_{0},b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_{0}\\); again at most \\(n\\).  \n* Line of slope \\(-1\\): it has equation \\(x+y=c\\). Intersecting with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\). The integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Assume \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines exist\n\nLet the three sunny lines be \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each of them covers at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\); therefore the three together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are arbitrary; by Lemma 2 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\). Hence they cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)n\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nAdding (1) and (2) we obtain an overall upper bound for the number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered:\n\n\\[\nN_{\\max}=3\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-3)n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Comparison with the required number of points\n\nThe set \\(S\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Bigl[3\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-3)n\\Bigr]\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2n^{2}+6n-6\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}+7n-6\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), so\n\n\\[\n-n^{2}+7n-6\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\ge -n^{2}+7n-3(n+1)= -n^{2}+4n-3\n= -(n-1)(n-3) >0\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge4\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|>N_{\\max},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the hypothesis that the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\},\n\\qquad |S|=6 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can contain at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{4}{2}\\big\\rfloor=2\\) points of \\(S\\) (the only possible slopes are \\(1\\) or any other non‑forbidden value, and the condition \\(a+b\\le4\\) limits the number of integer solutions).  \nThus three sunny lines can cover at most \\(3\\cdot2=6\\) points, and this would require each of them to contain exactly two points and the three lines to be pairwise disjoint on \\(S\\). A direct inspection shows that no three distinct sunny lines can achieve this configuration (any two sunny lines with distinct slopes intersect, and the intersection point would belong to \\(S\\), forcing a double count). Hence \\(k\\ge3\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\) as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion of the contradiction\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the assumption \\(k\\ge3\\) leads to a contradiction. Therefore any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at most two sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sharpness: constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Use only non‑sunny lines. For example, take the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  (all horizontal). Every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \