            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points of the right‑angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((0)\\), vertical \\((\\infty)\\) and slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering all points off the main diagonal with sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nThere exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus D\\subseteq \\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\ell_i,\n\\qquad \nD:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_i=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nso that each \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) we shall select the intercept \\(b_i\\) so that the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=m_i x+b_i\n\\]\n\npasses through a prescribed set of points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nConsider the matrix \\(M\\) whose rows are the vectors \\((1,m_i)\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the rows are linearly independent; hence the linear system  \n\n\\[\nM\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}= \\begin{pmatrix}y_1\\\\ \\vdots \\\\ y_k\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nhas a unique solution for any prescribed right‑hand side.  \n\nNow enumerate the points of \\(T_n\\setminus D\\) as \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_{|T_n|-n}\\}\\).  \nAssign the first \\(k\\) of them arbitrarily to the \\(k\\) lines: for each \\(i\\) require \\(\\ell_i\\) to pass through \\(P_i\\).  \nBecause the system determining the intercepts \\(b_i\\) is solvable (the matrix \\(M\\) is invertible), we obtain a choice of \\(b_i\\) that makes each \\(\\ell_i\\) contain its assigned point.  \n\nFor any remaining point \\(P\\in T_n\\setminus D\\) we may adjust the intercept of one of the lines (say \\(\\ell_1\\)) without destroying the already satisfied incidences, because the set of admissible intercepts for a fixed slope is infinite. Repeating this procedure we can ensure that **every** point of \\(T_n\\setminus D\\) lies on at least one of the \\(\\ell_i\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and such that **all** \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) with maximal sum \\(a+b=n+1\\) form the set  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a set of \\(n\\) collinear points lying on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(-1\\).  \n\nSince each point of \\(D\\) must belong to some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), by the pigeon‑hole principle at least two of these points share a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Any line containing two distinct points of \\(D\\) coincides with \\(\\ell_{-1}\\). Consequently \\(\\ell_{-1}\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\nBut \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny, contradicting the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   By Lemma 1 we can choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) that together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except possibly those of the diagonal \\(D\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Add the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{-1}:x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which contains the whole diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k-1\\) lines are taken to be the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c\\in C\\,\\},\n   \\qquad \n   C:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\ell_i\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By construction each \\(c\\in C\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\) that does **not** occur as the sum of coordinates of a point already covered by the sunny backbone; therefore the line \\(x+y=c\\) does not duplicate any of the sunny lines.  \n   All these lines have slope \\(-1\\), hence they are non‑sunny.  \n   The cardinality of \\(C\\) is exactly \\(n-k-1\\) because the \\(k\\) sunny lines together account for \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(a+b\\) (each sunny line, having a slope different from \\(-1\\), meets each vertical line \\(x=t\\) in at most one point, so it contributes at most one new sum \\(a+b\\)). Consequently we obtain precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.\n\n3. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   * Every point of \\(T_n\\setminus D\\) lies on some sunny line by Lemma 1.  \n   * Every point of \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n4. **Count of sunny lines.**  \n   By construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are ther