ence a sunny line can contain at most three points of\n   \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\), and if it contains three points then it must contain **exactly one**\n   point from each of \\(A,B,C\\).\n\n   Suppose, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) given lines are sunny.  \n   Because of (1), the total number of incidences between the lines and the points of\n   \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\) is at least \\(3n-2\\).  \n   If any line contained fewer than three of those points, the total would be \\(\\le 3n-3\\),\n   contradicting (1). Consequently **every** line contains three points, one from each set.\n\n   Let a line contain \\((i,1)\\in A\\) and \\((1,j)\\in B\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \n   The unique line through these two points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{i,j}:\\; y-1=\\frac{j-1}{1-i}\\,(x-i). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) to also meet the third set \\(C\\) it must pass through a point\n   \\((t,n+1-t)\\) for some \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Substituting \\((t,n+1-t)\\) into (2) yields\n\n   \\[\n   n+1-t-1 = \\frac{j-1}{1-i}\\,(t-i)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   t = i+j-1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) meets \\(C\\) exactly when the integer \\(i+j-1\\) lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n   i.e. when  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le i+j\\le n+1 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the \\(n\\) lines must cover the \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(C\\), the ordered pairs\n   \\((i,j)\\) that arise satisfy (4) and give **distinct** values of \\(i+j\\); otherwise two lines\n   would meet the same point of \\(C\\).  \n   Therefore the set of sums \\(\\{i+j\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n   The smallest possible sum is \\(2\\). The only way to obtain \\(i+j=2\\) with positive integers\n   \\(i,j\\) is \\(i=j=1\\). For this pair (2) becomes\n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = \\frac{0}{0}\\,(x-1) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -x+2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line \\(x+y=2\\), which is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n   Hence at least one of the \\(n\\) lines is **not** sunny, contradicting the assumption that\n   all are sunny. Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Choose any set \\(C\\subseteq\\{-\\,n+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|C|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(c\\in C\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\; y = x + c . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_c\\) has slope \\(1\\), hence is sunny.  \n   The line \\(\\ell_c\\) contains precisely those required points \\((a,b)\\) for which\n   \\(b-a=c\\).\n\n   *Points still uncovered.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S = \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the whole set of points that must be covered, and put  \n\n   \\[\n   S' = S\\setminus\\bigcup_{c\\in C}\\ell_c .\n   \\]\n\n   Fix a column \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   The points of \\(S\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,n+1-a\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Their differences \\(b-a\\) run through the consecutive integers  \n\n   \\[\n   1-a,\\;2-a,\\dots ,n+1-2a .\n   \\]\n\n   Removing the sunny lines eliminates exactly those points whose difference belongs to\n   \\(C\\). Because the interval above contains each integer at most once, **at most one**\n   value of \\(a\\) can be completely erased by a given \\(c\\). Hence after deleting all\n   \\(\\ell_c\\) there remain points in at least \\(n-k\\) different columns. Define  \n\n   \\[\n   A'=\\{a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid S' \\text{ contains a point with abscissa }a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |A'|\\ge n-k . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical (non‑sunny) lines.*  \n   For each \\(a\\in A'\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_a:\\; x = a . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(v_a\\) contains **all** points of \\(S'\\) whose abscissa equals \\(a\\); in particular it\n   covers the remaining points of that column. The lines \\(\\{v_a\\mid a\\in A'\\}\\) are distinct,\n   have slope \\(\\infty\\) and therefore are not sunny.\n\n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;|A'|\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;\\le\\;k+(n-k)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(|A'|<n-k\\) we may add arbitrary vertical lines with unused abscissae so that the total\n   becomes exactly \\(n\\); the added lines do not affect the covering property.\n\n   All lines are distinct: the sunny lines have different intercepts \\(c\\), the vertical lines\n   have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, and a vertical line can never coincide with a line of slope\n   \\(1\\).\n\n   Thus we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together\n   contain every point of \\(S\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (5) shows that no configuration can have \\(k=n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n   The construction above demonstrates that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) such a\n   configuration exists.\n\nHence the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
