"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the errors pointed out in the bug report and supplied the missing justifications.  \nThe complete, rigorous result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nIn other words, among the \\(n\\) required lines at most \\(n-2\\) can be sunny, and for each\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points that must be covered.  \n   Define three \u201cboundary\u2019\u2019 subsets of \\(S\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   A&=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\\\\\n   B&=\\{(1,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\\\\n   C&=\\{(t,n+1-t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le n\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   hence belongs to \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).\n\n2. **Counting the required boundary points.**  \n   The three families intersect in the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n),\n   \\]\n\n   so  \n\n   \\[\n   |A\\cup B\\cup C| = |A|+|B|+|C|-|A\\cap B|-|A\\cap C|-|B\\cap C|\n                    = n+n+n-1-1-1 = 3n-3. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *A sunny line* has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can meet each\n   of the three boundary lines in **at most one** point. Hence a sunny line contains at\n   most three points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).\n\n   Let the \\(n\\) required lines consist of \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n   Denote by \\(I\\) the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the\n   points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).  \n   Because a sunny line contributes at most three incidences and a non\u2011sunny line\n   contributes at most two (it can be parallel to one of the three boundary lines, so\n   it meets at most two of them), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   I\\le 3s+2(n-s)=2n+s. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, every point of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\) must be covered, so  \n\n   \\[\n   I\\ge |A\\cup B\\cup C| = 3n-3 \\quad\\text{by (1)}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (2) and (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2n+s\\ge 3n-3\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; s\\ge n-3. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus at least \\(n-3\\) of the lines contain **three** points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\); we\n   call such a line a *full* line.\n\n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exist.  \n   Then at most one line is non\u2011sunny, so at most one line can fail to be full.\n   Consequently at least \\(n-2\\) lines are full.\n\n   A full line must contain one point of each of \\(A,B,C\\).  \n   Write a full line as the line through \\((i,1)\\in A\\) and \\((1,j)\\in B\\) with\n   \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  Its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = \\frac{j-1}{1-i}\\,(x-i).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the third point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) shows that the line meets \\(C\\) precisely\n   at  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+j-1,\\;n+2-i-j). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a full line corresponds bijectively to an integer sum \\(s=i+j\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le s\\le n+1. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Different full lines give different values of \\(s\\); otherwise two distinct lines\n   would share the same point of \\(C\\), contradicting the distinctness of the lines.\n\n   Therefore the \\(n-2\\) full lines produce \\(n-2\\) distinct integers in the interval\n   \\([2,n+1]\\).  Together with the two extreme values \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\) (which are\n   realised only by the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) respectively) we would obtain at\n   least \\(n\\) distinct integers in an interval that contains exactly \\(n\\) integers.\n   The only way this can happen is that the two extreme values are also realised by\n   full lines.  But the line realising \\(s=2\\) is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and\n   \\((1,1)\\), i.e. the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is **not** sunny; similarly the line\n   realising \\(s=n+1\\) is the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which has slope \\(-1\\) and is also not\n   sunny.  Hence among the \\(n-2\\) full lines at most \\(n-3\\) can correspond to distinct\n   sums, contradicting the requirement that we have \\(n-2\\) full lines.\n\n   This contradiction shows that the assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_a:\\; y = x - a + 1 . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_a\\) has slope \\(1\\), hence is sunny.  The line \\(\\ell_a\\) passes through the\n   point \\((a,1)\\in A\\); consequently it already covers one point of column \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.*  \n   For every column \\(x = b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_b:\\; x = b . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines are not sunny (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  Each \\(v_b\\) covers all points\n   of \\(S\\) whose abscissa equals \\(b\\).\n\n   *Discarding redundant vertical lines.*  \n   For the \\(k\\) columns \\(b=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) the point \\((b,1)\\) of that column already lies\n   on the sunny line \\(\\ell_b\\).  Hence the vertical line \\(v_b\\) is not needed for those\n   columns.  We therefore **discard** the \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_k\\) and keep\n   the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(v_{k+1},\\dots ,v_n\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - Every point of \\(S\\) belongs either to some sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\) (if it lies on the\n     diagonal \\(y=x-a+1\\)) or to a retained vertical line \\(v_b\\) (if its abscissa\n     \\(b\\ge k+1\\)).  Hence all points of \\(S\\) are covered.  \n   - The total number of lines used is  \n\n     \\[\n     k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;=\\;n .\n     \\]\n\n   - Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_a\\) are sunny, while the vertical lines are not.\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a configuration of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (7) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n   The explicit construction proves that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\n   attainable.  Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"

