g \\(|A\\cup B\\cup C|\\) as a proxy for the total number of points that must be covered is unjustified.\n\n### 5. Upper bound for incidences (Equation (2))\n\n> “Because a sunny line contributes at most three incidences and a non‑sunny line contributes at most two … we have  \n> \\[\n> I\\le 3s+2(n-s)=2n+s. \\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\nHere \\(I\\) denotes the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).  \nThe reasoning is sound **for those three sets**: a sunny line can intersect each of the three boundary lines in at most one point, giving at most three incidences; a line parallel to one of the boundary lines can intersect the other two, giving at most two incidences. Hence (2) is correct **as a bound on incidences with \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\)**.\n\n### 6. Lower bound for incidences (Equation (3))\n\n> “On the other hand, every point of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\) must be covered, so  \n> \\[\n> I\\ge |A\\cup B\\cup C| = 3n-3 \\quad\\text{by (1)}. \\tag{3}\n> \\]”\n\nSince each point of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\) indeed has to lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, the inequality \\(I\\ge|A\\cup B\\cup C|\\) holds. This step is valid.\n\n### 7. Derivation of \\(s\\ge n-3\\) (Equation (4))\n\n> “Combining (2) and (3) gives  \n> \\[\n> 2n+s\\ge 3n-3\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; s\\ge n-3. \\tag{4}\n> \\]”\n\nAlgebraically correct. However, **interpretational error**: this inequality provides a *lower* bound on the number \\(s\\) of sunny lines, not an upper bound. The later parts of the solution treat it as if it limited the maximum possible number of sunny lines, which is a logical misstep.\n\n### 8. Definition of “full” lines and subsequent contradiction\n\n> “Thus at least \\(n-3\\) of the lines contain **three** points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\); we call such a line a *full* line. … Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exist. Then at most one line is non‑sunny, so at most one line can fail to be full. Consequently at least \\(n-2\\) lines are full.”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nThe statement “at least \\(n-3\\) lines are full” follows from (4) **only if every sunny line is full**, which has not been proved. A sunny line could intersect the three boundary lines in fewer than three points (e.g., a sunny line that misses one of the three sets). Hence the inference that “at most one line can fail to be full” when there are \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines is unjustified. The argument conflates the lower bound on the *number* of sunny lines with a lower bound on the *number* of full lines, which are distinct notions.\n\n### 9. Bijection between full lines and integer sums\n\n> “Write a full line as the line through \\((i,1)\\in A\\) and \\((1,j)\\in B\\)… its intersection with \\(C\\) is \\((i+j-1,\\;n+2-i-j)\\). Hence a full line corresponds bijectively to an integer sum \\(s=i+j\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\). Different full lines give different values of \\(s\\); otherwise two distinct lines would share the same point of \\(C\\)…”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nTwo distinct full lines can indeed share the same point of \\(C\\). For example, with \\(n\\ge5\\) the lines through \\((2,1),(1,3)\\) and through \\((3,1),(1,2)\\) are distinct, yet both intersect \\(C\\) at \\((4,n-3)\\). Therefore the mapping from full lines to the sum \\(i+j\\) is **not injective**, and the claimed bijection fails. Consequently the counting argument that follows (which asserts that \\(n-2\\) full lines would produce \\(n-2\\) distinct integers in \\([2,n+1]\\)) is invalid.\n\n### 10. Consequence of the faulty bijection\n\n> “Therefore the \\(n-2\\) full lines produce \\(n-2\\) distinct integers … together with the two extreme values … we would obtain at least \\(n\\) distinct integers … the only way this can happen is that the two extreme values are also realised by full lines … contradiction …”\n\nBecause the bijection is false, the entire chain of reasoning that leads to the contradiction is unsound. The conclusion that \\(k\\le n-2\\) is not justified by the presented argument.\n\n### 11. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n> “For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) define \\(\\ell_a: y = x - a + 1\\). … For every column \\(x = b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line \\(v_b: x = b\\). … Discard the \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_k\\) and keep the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(v_{k+1},\\dots ,v_n\\).”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nAfter discarding the first \\(k\\) vertical lines, points whose abscissa is \\(b\\le k\\) and whose ordinate is greater than 1 are **not** covered. For instance, with \\(k=1\\) the point \\((2,2)\\) (which belongs to \\(S\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\)) lies in column 2, but the vertical line \\(x=2\\) has been removed, and \\((2,2)\\) does not satisfy the equation of \\(\\ell_2\\) (since \\(\\ell_2\\) is defined only when \\(a=2\\le k\\)). Hence the construction fails to cover all points of \\(S\\). Consequently the claim that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable is false.\n\n### 12. Final conclusion\n\n> “Inequality (7) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines. The explicit construction proves that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable …”\n\nBoth the inequality (7) and the construction are invalid for the reasons detailed above; therefore the final statement is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains multiple critical logical and factual errors: an incorrect reduction of the covering condition to three boundary lines, misuse of the derived inequality, a false bijection between full lines and integer sums, and an erroneous construction that does not satisfy the covering requirement. Consequently the answer “\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\)” is not established, and the solution is not correct."
