th \\(k\\) sunny lines, and for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n+1\\) we obtain configurations with \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) are realizable.\n\n* **Upper bound.**  \n  Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\).\n\nThus, by induction, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of 3 lines (equations) | sunny lines |\n|------|--------------------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x=2\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=2\\) | none (all non‑sunny) – actually \\(k=0\\); to obtain \\(k=1\\) use \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=2x-1\\) (the last line has slope \\(2\\)) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=2x-1\\) | one sunny line (slope \\(2\\)) – add another sunny line e.g. \\(y=2x-3\\) (still distinct) |\n| 3 | \\(y=2x-1,\\; y=2x-3,\\; y=2x-5\\) | three sunny lines (all slopes \\(2\\)) |\n\nAll four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are therefore realizable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\n------------------