----------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive step: construction for \\(n+1\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) be given.  \nThe new triangle \\(T_{n+1}\\) contains, in addition to \\(T_n\\), the lattice points\n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\).  \nWe shall add **one** new line \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) in order to obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged  \n\nChoose \\(\\ell\\) to be the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y = n+2-a_0,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a_0\\) is any integer with \\(1\\le a_0\\le n+1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_h\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it is **not** sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\) satisfies \\(b=n+2-a\\); hence it lies on \\(\\ell_h\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_h\\) is not parallel to any line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) (the latter contain at most one horizontal line, and we may pick \\(a_0\\) so that \\(\\ell_h\\) is distinct), the resulting set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{\\ell_h\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nInstead of the horizontal line, take a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness choose  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y = 2x - (2a_0- (n+2)),\n\\]\n\nwith the same integer \\(a_0\\) as above.  \nThe slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_s\\) is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\) we have \\(b=n+2-a\\), and substituting \\(a\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2a-(2a_0-(n+2)) = n+2-a \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a=a_0,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a_0,\\,n+2-a_0)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThus \\(\\ell_s\\) covers **one** of the new points; the remaining points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) are already covered by the horizontal line used in the previous sub‑case, which we now keep as part of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).  \nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\), consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Obtaining every admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n+1\\)\n\nFrom the inductive hypothesis we have configurations for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* Using the construction of §3.1 we obtain configurations for the same \\(k\\) (now with \\(n+1\\) lines).\n* Using the construction of §3.2 we obtain configurations for \\(k+1\\) (hence for all \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n+1\\)).\n\nTogether these give configurations for **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nBy the base case and the inductive step, for each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non‑negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]"
