---\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of three distinct lines (equations) | sunny lines |\n|------|--------------------------------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x=2\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=2x-1\\) | the line \\(y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\)) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=2x-1,\\; y=2x-3\\) | the two lines of slope \\(2\\) |\n| 3 | \\(y=2x-1,\\; y=2x-3,\\; y=2x-5\\) | all three lines have slope \\(2\\) |\n\nEach family covers the three points of \\(T_3\\) and contains exactly the indicated number of sunny lines. Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive step: from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\},\n\\qquad\nT_{n+1}=T_n\\cup S_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) and have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Construction that does **not** increase the number of sunny lines  \n\nPick any integer \\(a_0\\) with \\(1\\le a_0\\le n+1\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nc=n+2-a_0 .\n\\]\n\nConsider the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h : y = c .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_h\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nThe point \\((a_0,c)\\) belongs to \\(S_{n+1}\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_h\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) have a different \\(y\\)-coordinate, so th