n  \n\n\\[\nb = a + 1 - i\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad i = a-b+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a sunny line iff the integer \\(a-b+1\\) lies in the interval \\([1,k]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The non‑sunny (vertical) lines\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nj\\in\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nintroduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x = j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines; they are all distinct and none of them is sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le a-b+1\\le k\\)*, then by (3) the point satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(L_{\\,a-b+1}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_{\\,a-b+1}\\).\n\n*If \\(a-b+1\\le 0\\) or \\(a-b+1>k\\)*, then \\(a\\) is an integer with \\(k+1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nConsequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) defined in (4) exists, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Distinctness of the whole family\n\n* The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) have different intercepts, so they are pairwise distinct.  \n* The \\(n-k\\) lines \\(V_j\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, so they are pairwise distinct.  \n* No line \\(L_i\\) is vertical, therefore the two families are disjoint.\n\nHence we indeed have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nTogether with the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (1), this shows that the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
