            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines’’ is precisely that the family of lines covers the set \\(S\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{e}_{x}=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{y}=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{d}= (1,-1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently a **non‑sunny** line is parallel to exactly one of these three vectors; we call the three possible types  \n\n* type‑\\(X\\): horizontal, equation \\(y=c\\);\n* type‑\\(Y\\): vertical, equation \\(x=c\\);\n* type‑\\(D\\): slope \\(-1\\), equation \\(x+y=c\\),\n\nwhere \\(c\\) is a real constant.\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is easily computed:\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(|S|>n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=0,1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S\\) and containing at most one sunny line.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑sunny.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nx=\\#\\{\\text{type‑\\(X\\) lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\ny=\\#\\{\\text{type‑\\(Y\\) lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{type‑\\(D\\) lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nx+y+d\\ge n-1,\\qquad x+y+d\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nA line of a fixed non‑sunny type contains **at most one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c>0\\).  \nIf it contained two distinct points \\((a_{1},c)\\) and \\((a_{2},c)\\) of \\(S\\) (\\(a_{1}\\neq a_{2}\\)), then  \n\n\\[\na_{1}+c\\le n+1,\\qquad a_{2}+c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAdding gives \\((a_{1}+a_{2})+2c\\le 2n+2\\).  \nSince \\(a_{1},a_{2},c\\) are positive integers, at least one of the sums \\(a_{i}+c\\) is \\(\\le n\\), which would place a third lattice point \\((a_{i},c)\\) of \\(S\\) on the same horizontal line – impossible because the two points already exhaust the line’s intersection with the integer lattice in the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe same reasoning applies verbatim to vertical lines \\(x=c\\) and to lines of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\). ∎  \n\n---\n\nBecause of Lemma 1, each non‑sunny line can cover **at most one** element of \\(S\\). Consequently the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by all non‑sunny lines is bounded by the number of such lines:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S\\cap\\bigcup_{\\ell\\text{ non‑sunny}}\\ell\\bigr)\\le x+y+d .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(x+y+d\\le n\\).  \nTogether with (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\ge x+y+d,\n\\]\n\nso at least one point of \\(S\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(S\\).\n\nHence the assumption that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one sunny line is false.  \nTherefore any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWe now show that each integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([2,n]\\) can actually be realised.\n\n---\n\n##### Step 1 – Two sunny lines covering many points  \n\nTake the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y = x ,\\qquad L_{2}:\\; y = 2x .\n\\]\n\nBoth are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so they are sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\) then \\(b=a\\) and the condition becomes \\(2a\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \nThus \\(L_{1}\\) contains exactly \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\) then \\(b=2a\\) and \\(3a\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\rfloor\\).  \nHence \\(L_{2}\\) contributes \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nSince the two lines intersect only at the origin (which is not in \\(S\\)), the points they cover are distinct.  \nTherefore the two sunny lines together cover at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge 2n-3\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\) (the inequality is verified directly for all \\(n\\ge3\\)).\n\n---\n\n##### Step 2 – Completing the covering with non‑sunny lines  \n\nThe number of points of \\(S\\) still uncovered after Step 1 is at most  \n\n\\[\n|S|-(2n-3)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(2n-3)=\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each such remaining point \\((a,b)\\) we introduce a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions that passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \nBecause of Lemma 1, a line of a fixed direction can contain at most one point of \\(S\\); consequently all these added lines are distinct.  \n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}{2}=n,\n\\]\n\nexactly the required number.\n\nThus we have exhibited, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a configuration with **exactly two sunny lines** that satisfies the covering condition.\n\n---\n\n##### Step 3 – Increasing the number of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the configuration of Step 2 