"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved a necessary lower bound on the number of sunny lines.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by counting how many of the required lattice points can be covered by the three families of *non\u2011sunny* lines (horizontal, vertical, and lines of slope\u202f\u20131).  \n* Each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-b\\) of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n* Each vertical line \\(x=a\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-a\\) of the points \\((a,b)\\).  \n* Each line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most one of the required points, namely the point \\((c-1,1)\\) (or any other point with that sum).  \n\nSumming these maximal contributions over all possible non\u2011sunny lines shows that at most \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) of the required points can be covered without using sunny lines.  \nSince there are exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points that must be covered, at least \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.  \n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThe converse (that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realized) remains open.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on the chosen lines.  \n\\(|T_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three distinguished directions:\n* horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\));\n* vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\));\n* slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) denote respectively the families of all horizontal, vertical and \\((-1)\\)-slope lines.  \nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) write \\(N(\\ell)=|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any horizontal line \\(y=b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nN(y=b)=\\max\\{0,\\;n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus exactly the points \\((1,b),\\dots,(n+1-b,b)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\). If \\(b>n\\) there is no point of \\(T_n\\) on that line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor any vertical line \\(x=a\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nN(x=a)=\\max\\{0,\\;n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Symmetric to Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFor any line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nN(x+y=c)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\\\[2pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the integer solutions of \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) are \\((1,c-1),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\).  \nAmong these, only the point \\((c-1,1)\\) (or any other) satisfies \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) **and** lies on the line \\(x+y=c\\); the other points have the same sum \\(c\\) and therefore would lie on the same line, which would give the line slope \\(-1\\). Consequently a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting maximal coverage without sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we are allowed to use only non\u2011sunny lines.  \nLet \\(h\\) be the number of horizontal lines used, \\(v\\) the number of vertical lines used, and \\(d\\) the number of \\((-1)\\)-slope lines used.  \nBecause the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nh+v+d\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\max}&=\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}\\bigl(n+1-b_i\\bigr)\n+\\sum_{j=1}^{v}\\bigl(n+1-a_j\\bigr)\n+d\\cdot 1,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(b_i\\) (resp. \\(a_j\\)) are the distinct ordinates (resp. abscissae) of the chosen horizontal (resp. vertical) lines.  \nSince the \\(b_i\\)\u2019s are distinct integers between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), the sum \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-b_i)\\) is maximised when the smallest possible ordinates are taken, i.e. \\(b_i=i\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-b_i)\\le \\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-i)=h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe same bound holds for the vertical contribution:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{v}(n+1-a_j)\\le v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert (3) and (4) into (2):\n\n\\[\nC_{\\max}\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}+d .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) to eliminate \\(d\\) (\\(d\\le n-h-v\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\max}\n&\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n+(n-h-v)\\\\[2pt]\n&=\nn(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side is maximal when \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are as small as possible.  \nSince \\(h,v\\) are non\u2011negative integers, the smallest possible sum \\(h+v\\) compatible with (1) is attained when \\(h+v\\) is as close as possible to \\(n\\).  \nThus the worst case (largest \\(C_{\\max}\\)) occurs for \\(h=v=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (or one of them \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and the other \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \nA direct computation gives\n\n\\[\nC_{\\max}\\le\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence  \n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, i.e. \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nIf we used only non\u2011sunny lines, the maximal number of points we could cover would be at most the right\u2011hand side of (7), which is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_n|\\) by exactly \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n\nTherefore at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.  \n\nLet \\(k\\) denote the number of sunny lines. We have proved\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\, } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks  \n\n* The argument uses only the pigeon\u2011hole principle in the form \u201cif we try to place the points into the available non\u2011sunny lines, the total capacity of those lines is insufficient, so some points must be placed on sunny lines.\u201d\n* The bound is sharp for small values of \\(n\\) (e.g. \\(n=3\\) where \\(k\\ge2\\) and a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists), but a complete classification of all attainable \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) remains open. \u220e"
