 right‑angled triangular lattice of side length  \n\n   \\[\n   s = n+1-(h+v)= n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Index these points as \\((v+i,\\;h+j)\\) with \\(i,j\\ge1\\) and \\(i+j\\le k+1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; (x-v)+(y-h)=t .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\) **after translation**, i.e. its direction vector is \\((1,-1)\\); therefore it is *sunny* because it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (the latter has slope \\(-1\\) through the origin, while \\(L_t\\) is shifted).  \n   Each line \\(L_t\\) contains exactly the points \\((v+i,\\;h+j)\\) with \\(i+j=t\\); thus the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k+1}\\) cover all remaining points, and they are all distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Step C – counting.*  \n   We have used \\(h+v=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, for a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\n   Because \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) can be chosen arbitrarily subject to \\(h+v=n-k\\) (e.g. \\(h=\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\rfloor,\\;v=n-k-h\\)), the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) in the prescribed interval.\n\nThus the lower bound and the explicit construction match, giving the exact set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines’’ is precisely that the union of the chosen lines contains \\(T_n\\).  \nA straightforward count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Lower bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three distinguished directions:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=\\text{const}\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=\\text{const}\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) the families of all such lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor a horizontal line \\(y=b\\),\n\n\\[\nN(y=b):=|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}\\,|=\\max\\{0,\\;n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-b\\) admissible \\(a\\)’s.  \nIf \\(b>n\\) there is no admissible \\(a\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor a vertical line \\(x=a\\),\n\n\\[\nN(x=a)=\\max\\{0,\\;n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Symmetric to Lemma 1. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\),\n\n\\[\nN(x+y=c)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\\\[2pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the integer solutions of \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) are \\((1,c-1),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\).  \nAll these points have the same sum \\(c\\); consequently they all lie on the same line \\(x+y=c\\).  \nSince the definition of \\(T_n\\) requires \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), exactly one of those points (any one) belongs to \\(T_n\\).  \nIf \\(c\\notin[2,n+1]\\) there is no point of \\(T_n\\) on the line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow suppose we are allowed to use only non‑sunny lines.  \nLet \\(h\\) be the number of distinct horizontal lines used, \\(v\\) the number of distinct vertical lines, and \\(d\\) the number of distinct slope \\(-1\\) lines.  \nBecause the total number of lines is at most \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nh+v+d\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemmas 1–3 the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\max}\n&\\le \\sum_{i=1}^{h}\\bigl(n+1-b_i\\bigr)\n   +\\sum_{j=1}^{v}\\bigl(n+1-a_j\\bigr)\n   + d\\cdot 1,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere the \\(b_i\\)’s (resp. \\(a_j\\)’s) are the distinct ordinates (resp. abscissae) of the chosen horizontal (resp. vertical) lines.  \nSince the \\(b_i\\)’s are distinct integers in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the sum \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-b_i)\\) is maximised when the smallest possible ordinates are taken, i.e. \\(b_i=i\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-b_i)\\le h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same bound holds for the vertical contribution:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{v}(n+1-a_j)\\le v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert (4) and (5) into (3) and use \\(d\\le n-h-v\\) from (2):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\max}\n&\\le h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n   +v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n   +(n-h-v)\\\\[2pt]\n&= n(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is largest when \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are as small as possible.  \nBecause \\(h,v\\) are non‑negative integers, the minimal possible values compatible with (2) are obtained by taking \\(h+v\\) as close as possible to \\(n\\).  \nA direct computation shows that the maximum of (6) under the constraint \\(h+v\\le n\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\max}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, setting \\(h=v=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) gives (7); any other admissible pair \\((h,v)\\) yields a smaller value.)\n\nFrom (1) we need to cover exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, but (7) shows that using only non‑sunny lines we can cover at most \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points.  \nTherefore at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points must be placed on sunny lines, i.e. at least that many of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (8).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le m\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor).\n\\]\n\nChoose non‑negative integers \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) with  \n\n\\[\nh+v=m. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(For instance, take \\(h=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor,\\;v=m-h\\).)\n\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nTake the \\(h\\) lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h .\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nTake the \\(v\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n\\]\n\nAll these \\(h+v=m=n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le v\\) or \\(b\\le h\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n#### 2.2  Remaining points\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered satisfy  \n\n\\[\na>v\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b>h .\n\\]\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na=v+i,\\qquad b=h+j,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(i,j\\ge1\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\ni+j\\le n+1-(v+h)=n+1-m = k+1 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points form a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(k+1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;(x-v)+(y-h)=t .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(L_t\\) has equation \\(x+y = t+v+h\\).  \nIts direction vector is \\((1,-1)\\); consequently it is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (the latter passes through the origin, while \\(L_t\\) is a translate).  \nHence each \\(L_t\\) is a sunny line.\n\nFrom (10) we see that a point \\((v+i,h+j)\\) belongs to \\(L_t\\) exactly when \\(i+j=t\\).  \nTherefore the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_2,\\;L_3,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_{k+1}\n\\]\n\ncover **all** remaining points, and they are pairwise distinct because they have different constant terms.\n\n#### 2.3  Counting\n\nWe have used  \n\n* \\(h+v=m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k+1}\\),\n\nfor a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines by construction.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (8) and the explicit construction together show that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Big\\lceil \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil,\\; \\Big\\lceil \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil+1,\\;\\dots ,\\; n } .\n\\]\n\n∎"
