\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\ns=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad t=n-s=\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2s+nt = 2s+n(n-s)=n^{2}-(n-2)s .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) from (1) into (2):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}-(n-2)s\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n(n-2)s \\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =\\frac{n(n-2)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ns\\le \\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) we can sharpen (3) by observing that (2) is strict unless **both** of the following hold:\n\n* every sunny line actually contains exactly two points of \\(S_n\\);\n* every non‑sunny line contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe latter can happen for at most two non‑sunny lines (a vertical line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, and the same bound holds for the other two directions).  Consequently, when \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have \\(t\\ge2\\), i.e. at least two non‑sunny lines.  Substituting \\(t\\ge2\\) into (2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2s+ n\\cdot t \\le 2s+ n\\cdot (n-2)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns\\le n-2 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) a direct enumeration shows that at most one line can be sunny, which also agrees with (4).  Hence **for every \\(n\\ge3\\)**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Existence of configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe construct families of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n#### 5.1 Base configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) for some \\(i\\), so the covering condition holds.  All these lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 5.2 Inductive step\n\nAssume we already have a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \nBecause \\(k\\le n-3\\), the configuration contains at least two non‑sunny lines.  Among the non‑sunny lines there is at least one that contains **three** points of \\(S_n\\): indeed, a vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains the points \\((i,1), (i,2),\\dots ,(i,n+1-i)\\); for \\(i\\le n-2\\) we have \\(n+1-i\\ge3\\).  Choose such a line and denote its three distinct points by  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(i,b_1),\\; P_2=(i,b_2),\\; P_3=(i,b_3),\\qquad b_1<b_2<b_3 .\n\\]\n\nNow modify the family as follows:\n\n1. **Delete** the chosen non‑sunny line \\(x=i\\).  \n2. **Add** the sunny line \\(M_{12}\\) passing through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \n3. **Add** the sunny line \\(M_{23}\\) passing through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\).  \n4. **Add** a new vertical line \\(x=j\\) where \\(j\\) is an integer not used by any existing vertical line (such a \\(j\\) exists because we have removed one vertical line and still have at most \\(n-1\\) vertical lines in total).\n\nThe two lines \\(M_{12}\\) and \\(M_{23}\\) are sunny because the three points are not collinear with slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); their slopes are \\((b_2-b_1)/(i-i)=\\infty\\) would contradict the choice of a non‑sunny line, and similarly for slope \\(-1\\).  Hence they are distinct sunny lines.  The new vertical line is non‑sunny and does not coincide with any line already present.\n\nAll three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) remain covered (each lies on one of the newly added lines), and every other point of \\(S_n\\) is untouched.  The total number of lines is still \\(n\\), and the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one (we removed one non‑sunny line and added two sunny lines).  Thus we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{k+1}\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBy iterating this step starting from the base configuration (\\(k=0\\)), we obtain configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe process stops at \\(k=n-2\\) because Lemma 5 shows that a larger number of sunny lines is impossible.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering uses \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nThen the number of non‑sunny lines is \\(t=n-k\\le1\\).\n\n*If \\(t=0\\)*, every line is sunny, so by Lemma 1 the total number of covered points is at most \\(2n\\).  Since \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\ge 10\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\), this is impossible.\n\n*If \\(t=1\\)*, the single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 2), and the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can contribute at most \\(2k\\) points (Lemma 1).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le n+2k\\le n+2(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut for \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 3n-2 ,\n\\]\n\nso the required set cannot be covered.  Consequently no configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) a direct check of all possibilities shows that the only feasible values are \\(k=0,1\\), which also agrees with the formula \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the upper bound (Section 4) with the explicit constructions (Section 5) we obtain that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\, } .\n\\]"
