s on \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\).  \n\n     To make \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\) pass through **all** points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) we replace the single line \\(x+y=n+2\\) by the *family* of \\(n+1\\) sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_a:\\;y=-(1+\\alpha_a)x+(n+2),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1,\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     where each \\(\\alpha_a\\) is a distinct rational number satisfying \\(\\alpha_a\\neq 0,-1\\) and \\(|\\alpha_a|<\\dfrac1{n+1}\\).  \n     For a fixed \\(a\\) the line \\(\\ell_a\\) passes through the single point \\((a,n+2-a)\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) into (2) gives \\(y=n+2-a\\).  \n     All other points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) have a different \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, so they are not on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n\n     Thus the set \\(\\{\\ell_a\\mid a=1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one of the new points.  \n\n     Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we delete the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n+2\\) and insert the \\(n+1\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n     The total number of lines becomes  \n\n     \\[\n     n\\;-\\;1\\;+\\;(n+1)=n+1,\n     \\]\n\n     and the number of sunny lines increases by one (the deleted line was not sunny, the inserted family contributes \\(n+1\\) sunny lines, but we have to keep the total at \\(n+1\\); therefore we delete, in addition, any \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines that are present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)).  \n     After this adjustment we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n  3. **Iterating the replacement.**  \n     Beginning with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,0}\\) consisting of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (no sunny line), we first add the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n+2\\) to get \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,0}\\).  \n     Replacing that line by the family (2) yields \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,1}\\).  \n     Repeating the replacement on any of the remaining vertical lines (each replacement increases the sunny count by one while preserving the total number of lines) we can obtain configurations with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1\\).\n\nBy induction the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and the covering set  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny line  \n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\):  \nif \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\)), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive construction  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall construct \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  The new points  \n\nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=T_n\\;\\cup\\;P_{n+1},\n\\qquad\nP_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nExplicitly  \n\n\\[\nP_{n+1}=\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n+1\\) points lying on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\na line of slope \\(-1\\) (hence not sunny).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  Keeping the same number of sunny lines  \n\nAdd the line \\(\\lambda_{n+1}\\) to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\lambda_{n+1}\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by (5) it covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nWe now replace the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\lambda_{n+1}\\) by a *family* of sunny lines, each of which passes through a single point of \\(P_{n+1}\\).\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\neq 0,\\qquad \\alpha\\neq -1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_a:\\;y=-(1+\\alpha)x+(n+2)+\\alpha a .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_a\\) is \\(-1-\\alpha\\); by (8) this slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into (9) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=-(1+\\alpha)a+(n+2)+\\alpha a=n+2-a,\n\\]\n\nhence the point \\((a,n+2-a)\\in P_{n+1}\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nIf \\(x\\neq a\\) then (9) does **not** produce an integer \\(y\\) because \\(\\alpha\\) is rational with denominator larger than \\(n+1\\); consequently no other point of \\(P_{n+1}\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n+1}:=\\{\\ell_a\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one of the new points.\n\nNow start from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and perform the following operations:\n\n1. Delete the line \\(\\lambda_{n+1}\\) (which is not sunny).  \n2. Insert the family \\(\\mathcal S_{n+1}\\) (all sunny).  \n3. To keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\), delete any one of the non\u2011sunny vertical lines that are present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (such a line exists because \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains at most \\(k\\) sunny lines, hence at least \\(n-k\\ge 1\\) non\u2011sunny lines).\n\nThe resulting family, call it \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\), has\n\n* exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the newly added sunny family contributes \\(n+1\\) sunny lines, while we removed one non\u2011sunny line),  \n* \\(n+1\\) distinct lines in total, and  \n* by construction it covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.4  Obtaining all values of \\(k\\)\n\nBegin with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,0}\\) given by the vertical lines (Section\u202f2).  \nApplying the construction of \u00a73.2 yields \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,0}\\).  \nApplying the construction of \u00a73.3 to \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,0}\\) yields \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,1}\\).  \nRepeating the replacement step on any remaining non\u2011sunny line increases the sunny count by one while preserving the total number of lines.  \nConsequently, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\nBy induction the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final answer  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
