\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then \\(a\\) is one of the numbers \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\); in\n  this case the point is still covered because the vertical line \\(V_a\\) is\n  **not** needed: the point already belongs to a line of the form\n  \\(y=x+d\\) for some integer \\(d\\).  Indeed, set \\(d=b-a\\); then\n  \\(y = x + d\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Since \\(d\\) is an integer, the line\n  \\(y = x + d\\) is one of the sunny lines \\(S_j\\) provided we choose the\n  intercepts in (3) to range over a set of \\(k\\) consecutive integers that\n  contains all possible values of \\(d\\) occurring for points with\n  \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).\n\n  The smallest possible value of \\(d=b-a\\) for such points occurs when\n  \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=1\\), giving \\(d=1-n\\).  The largest possible value occurs\n  when \\(a=n-k+1\\) and \\(b=n+1-a\\), giving \\(d=(n+1-a)-a = n+1-2a\\le k-1\\).\n  Hence all relevant \\(d\\) lie in the interval \\([1-n,\\,k-1]\\), which has\n  length \\(n+k-2\\).  By taking the \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) with intercepts\n  \\(-n+1, -n+2,\\dots , -n+k\\) we guarantee that each such \\(d\\) coincides\n  with the intercept of one of the sunny lines.  Consequently every point\n  with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) defined\nin (4) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand it covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nSince the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\), no value of \\(k\\)\noutside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\nTherefore the required set of non\u2011negative integers is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
