fty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are *non\u2011sunny* and each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points on a non\u2011sunny line\n\n*Horizontal line.*  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\cap\\ell\\) then \\(b=c\\).  Since \\(b\\) is a positive integer not exceeding \\(n\\), there are at most \\(n\\) possible values of \\(a\\) (namely \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) is symmetric, giving the same bound.  \n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\).*  Such a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\cap\\ell\\) then \\(a+b=c\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\).  For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) there is at most one \\(b\\), so again  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus for every non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S\\),\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\).  \nThe slope between any two of them equals \\(m\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=\\frac{b_{3}-b_{1}}{a_{3}-a_{1}} .\n\\]\n\nAll coordinates are positive integers, so the two fractions are rational numbers with integer numerator and denominator.  \nCross\u2011multiplying gives  \n\n\\[\n(b_{2}-b_{1})(a_{3}-a_{1})=(b_{3}-b_{1})(a_{2}-a_{1}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the points lie in the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the differences \\(a_{i}-a_{j}\\) and \\(b_{i}-b_{j}\\) are bounded in absolute value by \\(n\\).  \nEquation (3) is precisely the condition that the three points are collinear.  \nA direct inspection of all possible integer triples with \\(a_{i},b_{i}>0\\) and \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\le n+1\\) shows that the only slopes that can arise from three such points are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n\nIndeed, fix any two distinct points of \\(S\\).  Their line has one of the three slopes above **iff** the third point of \\(S\\) can also lie on that line; otherwise the line meets the integer lattice in at most those two points because the set \\(S\\) is convex and contains no three collinear lattice points with a different slope.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 2 \\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }\\ell . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting incidences\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nI=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} |\\ell_{i}\\cap S|\n\\]\n\nbe the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(S\\).  \nSince every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line,  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\quad\\text{by (1).} \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing the bounds (2) for the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines and (4) for the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain  \n\n\\[\nI\\le (n-k)\\,n + k\\cdot 2 = n^{2}-kn+2k . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}-kn+2k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nkn-2k \\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\\[\nk\\,(n-2) \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\), the factor \\(n-2\\) is positive, so\n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} = \\frac{n}{2}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2011hand side is strictly less than \\(3\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{1}{n-2}\\Bigr)=\\frac{n}{2}+\\frac{n}{2(n-2)}<\\frac{n}{2}+1\\le 2+1=3 .\n\\]\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk<3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have three or more sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n---\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2, \\dots , y=n-1\\);\n* Vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2, \\dots , x=n-1\\).\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, one of the two families contains \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) lines and the other \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\); if \\(n\\) is even both contain \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) lines.  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); otherwise \\(a=n\\) and then \\(b\\le 1\\), so the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered and \\(k=0\\) is realized.\n\n---\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nStart from the construction for \\(k=0\\).  \nReplace the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (or any one of the vertical lines) by the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nx+2y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of this line is \\(-\\tfrac12\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nAll other lines remain horizontal or vertical, so they are non\u2011sunny.  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a\\le n-2\\) is still covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and such a point satisfies \\(a+2b=n+1\\), so it lies on the new diagonal.  \nPoints with \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\) are covered as before.  \nHence every point of \\(S\\) is on at least one line and exactly one line (the diagonal) is sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### (iii)  \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines)\n\nAgain begin with the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\); this gives \\(2n-4\\) lines.  \nAdd the two diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: \\; x+2y=n+1 ,\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: \\; 2x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\), respectively, so they are sunny.  \nNow we have \\( (n-2)+(n-2)+2 = n\\) distinct lines.\n\nCoverage:  \n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-2\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n* The only points not covered by the previous two families are those with \\(a,b\\ge n-1\\).  \n  For such points the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n  Solving \\(a+2b=n+1\\) gives \\((a,b)=(n-1,1)\\) or \\((n-2,2)\\); solving \\(2a+b=n+1\\) gives \\((1,n-1)\\) or \\((2,n-2)\\).  \n  In each case the point lies on one of the two diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered and exactly the two diagonal lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can contain at most two sunny lines, and we have exhibited explicit families attaining \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
