s precisely \\(m\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n(and no point is covered twice, because the three families lie on three\ndifferent sides of the triangle).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Covering the remaining points with sunny lines\n\nAfter removing the \\(m\\) points already covered, there remain exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|-m = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - \\bigl(\\tfrac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\bigr)=2k\n\\]\n\nuncovered points.  None of these points lies on a line parallel to the three\nforbidden directions; otherwise it would have been captured by the families\nchosen in §2.1.\n\nPair the remaining points arbitrarily into \\(k\\) unordered pairs\n\\(\\{P_{i},Q_{i}\\}\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).  \nFor each pair let \\(L_{i}\\) be the unique line passing through the two points.\nBecause the two points are not collinear with any of the three forbidden\ndirections, the slope of \\(L_{i}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\);\nhence each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  Distinct pairs give distinct lines, so the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines are all different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Counting the lines\n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{m}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}\n= (t_{1}+t_{2}+t_{3})+k\n= \\bigl(\\tfrac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\bigr)+k = n .\n\\]\n\nThus we have produced a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\ncontaining precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe counting argument gave the necessary condition (3).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) a\nconfiguration exists.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rc