\nBy Lemma 1 each of the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points,\nand by Lemma 2 each of the \\(s\\) sunny lines contributes at most \\(2\\) points.\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\\le tn+2s=(n-s)n+2s=n^{2}-sn+2s. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (1) into (2) and solve for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-sn+2s\n\\Longrightarrow s\\,(n-2)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow s\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k .\n\\]\n\nBecause of the choice of \\(k\\), \\(m\\) is a non‑negative integer and\n\\(m\\le n^{2}-n\\).  We shall cover exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with\nnon‑sunny lines and the remaining \\(2k\\) points with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Covering \\(m\\) points with non‑sunny lines\n\nWrite \\(m\\) as a sum of three non‑negative integers\n\n\\[\nm=t_{1}+t_{2}+t_{3},\n\\qquad 0\\le t_{1},t_{2},t_{3}\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSuch a decomposition always exists: the right‑hand side of (4) can attain any\nvalue between \\(0\\) and \\(3(n-1)\\); since \\(m\\le n^{2}-n= n(n-1)\\le 3(n-1)\\) for\n\\(n\\ge3\\), a suitable triple \\((t_{1},t_{2},t_{3})\\) can be chosen.\n\nNow take  \n\n* the horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=t_{1}\\);\n* the vertical   lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots , x=t_{2}\\);\n* the diagonal   lines \\(x+y=2, x+y=3,\\dots , x+y=t_{3}+1\\).\n\nThese