(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the conditions\n\n#### (a) Number of lines and sunny lines\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines.\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, so the number of sunny\nlines is \\(k\\).\n\n#### (b) Covering property\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) which is\npresent in (2).\n\n*If \\(a> n-k\\)*, write \\(d=b-a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have\n\\(b\\le n+1-a\\); together with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) this yields the double inequality\n\n\\[\n-(n-k)\\le d\\le k-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe set of integers satisfying (5) is exactly\n\\(\\{- (n-1),- (n-2),\\dots , - (n-k)\\}\\cup\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\nAmong these, the \\(k\\) values\n\n\\[\nd_i = -(n-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the intercepts appearing in the equations of the lines\n\\(L_i\\) in (3).  Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n\\(b = a + d_i\\) for the unique \\(i\\) with \\(d=d_i\\), and therefore\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness of the answer\n\nSince a configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny\nlines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  The construction above shows that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of admissible\nnon\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

