\n\\[\nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) must also be present.  \nThus at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, and the remaining\n\\(k=n-2\\) lines at most can be sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and  \n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\).  We build the required family in four steps.\n\n1. **Two mandatory non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Include  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_V:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of the sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) defined in Lemma 1.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) iff \\(b-a=i\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Solving these inequalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-i}{2},\\qquad b=a+i .\n   \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,\\,a+i)\\quad\\bigl(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr).\n   \\tag{2.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) whose difference \\(b-a\\) belongs to\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) is already covered.\n\n3. **Remaining points.**  \n   After Steps 1–2 the uncovered points are precisely those satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad b-a\\ge k .\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed column \\(a\\) condition (3.1) forces \\(b\\ge a+k\\).\n   Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}.\n   \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the only columns that may still contain uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   a=2,3,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The number of such columns is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\n   \\le n-(k+2) ,\n   \\tag{3.4}\n   \\]\n\n   where the inequality follows from \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Lemma 1).\n\n   For each column listed in (3.3) we introduce a vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   x = a .\n   \\tag{3.5}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and\n   each such line contains **all** points of its column, in particular\n   all points satisfying (3.1) that lie in that column.  Hence after adding\n   the lines (3.5) every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   * the two lines \\(\\ell_V,\\ell_H\\);  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\);  \n   * at most \\(n-(k+2)\\) vertical lines of the form (3.5).\n\n   Thus the total number of lines does not exceed \\(n\\).  \n   If it is smaller than \\(n\\), we may add any further non‑sunny lines\n   (for instance additional vertical lines with unused \\(x\\)-coordinates);\n   they do not affect the covering property because every point is already\n   covered.\n\n   The resulting family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) are sunny.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that any feasible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a\nconfiguration exists.  Therefore the set of all admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct