cal nor horizontal contains at most one point of \\(A\\) and at most\none point of \\(B\\); therefore \\(m(\\ell)\\le 2\\).\n\nThe two special lines satisfy \\(m(x=1)=n+1\\) and \\(m(y=1)=n+1\\).\n\nNow let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  In particular they cover all\npoints of \\(A\\cup B\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} m(\\ell_i)\\ge |A\\cup B|=2n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause of Claim 1, each summand is at most \\(2\\) unless the line is\n\\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).  Since the right–hand side of (1) is odd, at least one\nsummand must be odd.  The only way a summand can be odd (and larger than\n\\(1\\)) is to be \\(n+1\\), i.e. the line is \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).  Hence at\nleast one of these two lines belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nRemove that line; we are left with \\(n-1\\) lines that must cover the\nremaining \\(2n-2\\) points of \\(A\\cup B\\).  Applying the same argument to\nthis reduced family forces the other axis line to be present as well.\nThus both \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are necessarily among the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nConsequently at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny (they are\nparallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and to the \\(x\\)-axis respectively).  Hence the\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and  \n* together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n1. **Two mandatory non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Include  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_V:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover all points of the sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) defined in\n   Lemma 1.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n