nes.*  \n     The construction uses  \n\n     \\[\n     2\\;(\\ell_V,\\ell_H)+k\\;(\\text{sunny})+\n     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\;(\\text{extra vertical})\n     \\]\n\n     lines.  For \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n\n     \\[\n     2+k+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\n     \\le n .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     (Indeed, write \\(n=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\); a short case check of the\n     parity of \\(k\\) shows (4) holds.)  If fewer than \\(n\\) lines have been used,\n     we add arbitrary further non\u2011sunny lines (e.g. vertical lines with unused\n     \\(x\\)-coordinates); they do not affect the covering property.  Thus we obtain\n     exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are\n     sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines;\n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), undefined, and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\),\nat least two of the lines are non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nso \\(|A\\cup B|=2n-1\\).\n\nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) let  \n\n\\[\nm(\\ell)=|\\ell\\cap (A\\cup B)|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of points of \\(A\\cup B\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*Claim.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\neq x=1\\) then \\(m(\\ell\\cap A)\\le 1\\);  \nif \\(\\ell\\neq y=1\\) then \\(m(\\ell\\cap B)\\le 1\\).\n\nIndeed, all points of \\(A\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(1\\).  Any line\nthat is not vertical meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point,\nhence can contain at most one point of \\(A\\).  The same reasoning with the\nhorizontal line \\(y=1\\) gives the second statement.\n\nConsequently, any line that is neither \\(x=1\\) nor \\(y=1\\) satisfies\n\\(m(\\ell)\\le 2\\).\n\nNow let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  In particular they cover all points\nof \\(A\\cup B\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} m(\\ell_i)\\ge |A\\cup B|=2n-1 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf the line \\(x=1\\) were absent from \\(\\mathcal L\\), then each \\(\\ell_i\\) would\ncontribute at most one point of \\(A\\); the left\u2011hand side of (1) would then be\nat most \\(n+(n-1)=2n-1\\) only if **every** line contributed exactly one point\nof \\(A\\).  That would require each line to intersect the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) at a distinct point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Since a line\ncannot pass through two different points of \\(A\\) unless it is \\(x=1\\), the\nonly way to achieve the required total \\(2n-1\\) is to have the line \\(x=1\\)\nitself in the family.  Hence \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\nRemoving the line \\(x=1\\) leaves \\(n-1\\) lines that must cover the remaining\n\\(2n-2\\) points of \\(A\\cup B\\).  Applying the same argument to this reduced\nfamily forces the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) to be present as well.  Thus both\n\\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nSince the two lines \\(x=1\\) (vertical) and \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) are parallel\nto the \\(y\\)-axis and the \\(x\\)-axis respectively, they are **not** sunny.\nTherefore at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, and the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and  \n* together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n1. **Two mandatory non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Include  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_V:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover all points of the sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) defined in\n   Lemma\u202f1.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) iff \\(b-a=i\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Solving these inequalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-i}{2},\\qquad b=a+i .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,\\,a+i)\\quad\\text{for }a=1,2,\\dots ,\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) whose difference \\(b-a\\) belongs to\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) is already covered.\n\n3. **Remaining points and extra non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   After Steps\u202f1\u20132 the uncovered points are precisely those satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad b-a\\ge k . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed column \\(a\\) condition (4) forces \\(b\\ge a+k\\).  Together\n   with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the only columns that may still contain uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   a=2,3,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For each such column we introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   x = a . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and each contains\n   all points of its column, in particular all points described by (4) that\n   lie in that column.  After adding the lines (6) every point of \\(T_n\\) is\n   covered.\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\ell_V,\\ell_H)+k\\;(\\text{sunny})+\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\;(\\text{extra vertical})\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  We verify that this number never exceeds \\(n\\) when\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   Write \\(n=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor q+\\frac{r+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le q+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{r+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\ge 0\\), the term \\(\\frac{r+1-k}{2}\\) is at most \\(\\frac{r+1}{2}\\).\n   Hence\n\n   \\[\n   2+k+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\n   \\le 1+k+q+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{r+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(r\\le1\\), \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{r+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le1\\), and\n   \\(k\\le n-2=2q+r-2\\).  Substituting the maximal possible \\(k\\) gives\n\n   \\[\n   1+(2q+r-2)+q+1 = 3q+r \\le 2q+r = n .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the total number of lines does not exceed \\(n\\).  If it is\n   strictly smaller than \\(n\\), we may add any further non\u2011sunny lines\n   (for instance vertical lines with unused \\(x\\)-coordinates); these extra\n   lines do not affect the covering property.\n\n   Thus we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the\n   \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(i=0,\\dots ,k-1\\)) are sunny.\n\n\\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any feasible configuration can contain at most\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a\nconfiguration exists.  Hence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
