sunny line by a suitable sunny line that still covers all required points, thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\nUsing Lemmas\u202f2 and\u202f3 we obtain the inductive step, completing the proof.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering Lemma)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m)\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le m\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).  \nHence the line \\(x=a\\) (which is one of the \\(L_i\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny\u2011Insertion Lemma)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the given \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) and add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}:\\;x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{n+1}\\) is not sunny (it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_n\\) (hence is already covered) or has first coordinate \\(n+1\\); the latter points are covered by \\(L_{n+1}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}:=\\mathcal F_n\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}\\) satisfies the required properties and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Sunny\u2011Replacement Lemma)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and suppose a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nThen there exists another family \\(\\mathcal F'_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing **one more** sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) has at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, at least one of its lines, say \\(M\\), is non\u2011sunny.  \nThere are three possible directions for \\(M\\):\n\n1. **Horizontal** (\\(y=c\\)).  \n   Choose a sunny line \\(S\\) of slope \\(2\\) passing through the point \\((1,c)\\).  \n   Since the slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), \\(S\\) is sunny.  \n   The point \\((1,c)\\) already belongs to \\(M\\); all other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(M\\) have abscissa \\(\\ge 2\\).  \n   For each such point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  \n   The line \\(S\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at \\((1,c)\\) and, because its slope is \\(2\\), it meets the line \\(x=2\\) at \\((2,c+2)\\).  \n   Since \\(c+2\\le n\\) (otherwise \\((1,c)\\) would not belong to \\(T_n\\)), the point \\((2,c+2)\\) also lies in \\(T_n\\).  \n   Continuing, the intersection of \\(S\\) with each vertical line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) yields a point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) that was on the horizontal line \\(M\\) is also on the sunny line \\(S\\).  \n   Replacing \\(M\\) by \\(S\\) yields the desired family.\n\n2. **Vertical** (\\(x=c\\)).  \n   By symmetry we replace \\(x=c\\) with the sunny line of slope \\(-2\\) passing through \\((c,1)\\).  \n   The same argument as above shows that all points of \\(T_n\\) formerly on \\(x=c\\) lie on the new sunny line.\n\n3. **Diagonal** (\\(x+y=d\\)).  \n   Replace it by the sunny line of slope \\(1/2\\) passing through \\((1,d-1)\\).  \n   Again every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) on the diagonal also lies on the new line, because moving one unit right increases the ordinate by \\(1/2\\), staying inside the triangle.\n\nIn each case we have replaced a non\u2011sunny line by a distinct sunny line while preserving coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence the number of sunny lines increases by one. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction\n\nWe prove the statement\n\n\\[\nP(n):\\qquad \\text{For the given }n\\ge 3,\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are exactly the admissible }k.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2, x=3\\) (all non\u2011sunny) cover \\(T_3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): take the two vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2\\) and the sunny line \\(y=x\\); the three lines cover the three points.  \n* \\(k=2\\): use \\(x=1\\) together with the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\; y=2x,\\; y=3x\\) are distinct and each passes through one of the three points.\n\nThus \\(P(3)\\) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume \\(P(n)\\) holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\); i.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe prove \\(P(n+1)\\).\n\n*Existence for \\(k\\le n\\).*  \nTake \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(k)}\\) and apply Lemma\u202f2.  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(k)}\\) has \\(n+1\\) lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Existence for \\(k=n+1\\).*  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(n)}\\) (which has all \\(n\\) lines 