            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of integer points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering Lemma)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m)\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le m\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).  \nHence the line \\(x=a\\) (one of the \\(L_i\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny‑Insertion Lemma)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}:\\;x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{n+1}\\) is not sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_n\\) (hence already covered) or has first coordinate \\(n+1\\); the latter points are covered by \\(L_{n+1}\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}:=\\mathcal F_n\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Sunny‑Replacement Lemma)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and suppose a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nThen there exists another family \\(\\mathcal F'_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing **one more** sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the number of sunny lines is \\(\\le n-1\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) is non‑sunny.  \nWe treat the three possible directions of a non‑sunny line.\n\n1. **Horizontal line** \\(M:\\;y=c\\) (slope \\(0\\)).  \n   Choose the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=2x+(c-2)\n   \\]\n\n   (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The point \\((1,c)\\) lies on both \\(M\\) and \\(S\\).  \n   For any integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) the intersection of \\(S\\) with the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is \\((i,2i+c-2)\\).  \n   Since \\((i,c)\\in T_n\\) implies \\(c\\le n+1-i\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   2i+c-2\\le 2i+(n+1-i)-2=n+i-1\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   and also \\(2i+c-2\\ge 1\\).  \n   Hence \\((i,2i+c-2)\\in T_n\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) that was on the horizontal line \\(M\\) also lies on the sunny line \\(S\\).  \n   Replacing \\(M\\) by \\(S\\) yields a new family with one additional sunny line.\n\n2. **Vertical line** \\(M:\\;x=c\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n   By symmetry replace it with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=-2x+(c+2),\n   \\]\n\n   which has slope \\(-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The same computation as above shows that every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(x=c\\) also lies on \\(S\\).  \n\n3. **Diagonal line** \\(M:\\;x+y=d\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Replace it with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=\\tfrac12 x+(d-\\tfrac12),\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope \\(1/2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) on \\(M\\) we have \\(b=d-a\\).  \n   Substituting into the equation of \\(S\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   d-a=\\tfrac12 a+(d-\\tfrac12)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a=1,\n   \\]\n\n   so the only lattice point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(M\\) is \\((1,d-1)\\).  \n   This point also satisfies the equation of \\(S\\); thus all points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(M\\) are covered by \\(S\\).\n\nIn each case we have replaced a non‑sunny line by a distinct sunny line while preserving coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence the number of sunny lines increases by one. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction\n\nDefine the statement  \n\n\\[\nP(n):\\quad\\text{For the given }n\\ge 3,\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are exactly the admissible }k.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,x=3\\) (all non‑sunny) cover the three points.  \n* \\(k=1\\): lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=x\\) – exactly one sunny line.  \n* \\(k=2\\): lines \\(x=1,\\;y=x,\\;y=2x\\) – two sunny lines.  \n* \\(k=3\\): lines \\(y=x,\\;y=2x,\\;y=3x\\) – all three sunny.\n\nThus \\(P(3)\\) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume \\(P(n)\\) holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\); i.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe prove \\(P(n+1)\\).\n\n*Existence for \\(k\\le n\\).*  \nApply Lemma 2 to \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(k)}\\).  \nWe obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(k)}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering 