"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) one can find \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and such that precisely \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*; and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible (trivially \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 obvious: at most \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, so \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) )** \u2013  \n   *Let \\(m=n-k\\).*\n\n   * Use the \\(m\\) *non\u2011sunny* lines  \n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     These are vertical, hence not sunny, and they already cover every point whose\n     \\(x\\)-coordinate lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\).\n\n   * The remaining points are those with \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\) satisfying \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n     For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t+1}{\\,n-m\\,}\\,x + \\frac{(t+1)n}{\\,n-m\\,}+1 .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     The slope of \\(L_t\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{t+1}{n-m}\\); because \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) and\n     \\(n-m=k\\ge 1\\), this slope is never \\(0\\), never undefined (vertical), and never \\(-1\\).\n     Hence each \\(L_t\\) is *sunny*.\n\n   * One checks that for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\) the point\n     \\((a,\\,n+1-a)\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_t\\):\n     solving \\(y=n+1-a\\) in (2) gives \\(t = a-m-1\\), which is an integer between\n     \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\). Consequently each point of the triangular set\n     \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is covered either by a vertical line\n     (if \\(a\\le m\\)) or by a sunny line \\(L_t\\) (if \\(a\\ge m+1\\)).\n\n   * The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are distinct because they have distinct\n     slopes, and together with the \\(m\\) vertical lines we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct\n     lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Special cases** \u2013  \n   *\\(k=0\\)*: take the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\)*: set \\(m=0\\) and use the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) defined in (2).\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and no other values are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must be covered.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the configuration, therefore the number of sunny\nlines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad (0\\le m\\le n).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m}\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(if \\(m=0\\) this set is empty).  \nEach line in (4) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) of (4).\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t+1}{\\,n-m\\,}\\,x + \\frac{(t+1)n}{\\,n-m\\,}+1}\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(the denominator \\(n-m=k\\) is non\u2011zero because \\(k\\ge 1\\) when we use (5);\nif \\(k=0\\) the set \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) is empty).\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{t+1}{k}\\).  \nSince \\(k\\ge 1\\) and \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\), this slope is never \\(0\\), never undefined,\nand never \\(-1\\). Consequently each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  Different values of \\(t\\) give different slopes, so the lines\n\\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 2.3  Coverage of the remaining points  \n\nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge m+1\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b=n+1-a\\).  \nInsert \\(x=a,\\;y=n+1-a\\) into (5) and solve for \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nn+1-a = -\\frac{t+1}{k}\\,a + \\frac{(t+1)n}{k}+1\n\\Longrightarrow t = a-m-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) ranges from \\(m+1\\) to \\(n\\), the obtained \\(t\\) ranges from\n\\(0\\) to \\(k-1\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique sunny line \\(L_{a-m-1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line of (4) (if\n\\(a\\le m\\)) or by a sunny line of (5) (if \\(a\\ge m+1\\)).\n\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe configuration consists of the \\(m\\) vertical lines (4) and the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines (5); altogether there are \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which\nare sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Special values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Take \\(m=n\\) and use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\)\n  together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  All are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is\n  realized.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  Here \\(m=0\\); the construction reduces to the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n  \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) given by (5).  They cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have\nproduced an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering\ncondition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConversely, no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
