\mathbf{e}_{-}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the set of sunny lines, \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\), and let \\(\\mathcal N\\) be the set of non\u2011sunny lines, \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf \\(L\\in\\mathcal N\\) then \\(|L\\cap T|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible \\(a\\) satisfy\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) such points.  \nIf \\(L\\) is vertical, the argument is symmetric.  \nIf \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\), it can be written \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); the points of \\(T\\) on it are\n\\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf \\(L\\in\\mathcal S\\) then \\(|L\\cap T|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a sunny line contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in T\\).\nThe vectors \\((a_{2}-a_{1},b_{2}-b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{3}-a_{1},b_{3}-b_{1})\\) are both direction vectors of \\(L\\).\nSince the coordinates are integers, one of these vectors must be a non\u2011zero integer multiple of\n\\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); otherwise the two vectors would be linearly independent, contradicting that they lie on the same line.\nThus \\(L\\) would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), which is impossible for a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T\\); from Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can cover at most \\(2\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k) \\;\\ge\\; |T| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying (1):\n\n\\[\n2k + n^{2} - nk \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\n(2-n)k \\ge -\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### Sunny lines\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-1 = i\\,(x-i) \\qquad\\text{(equivalently } y = i x - i^{2}+1\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n- The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i\\neq 0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n- \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\); both belong to \\(T\\) because\n\\(i+1\\ge 1\\), \\(1\\ge 1\\) and \\(i+1+1\\le n+1\\) (the latter holds since \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \n- For distinct \\(i\\) the lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\nThus we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering exactly the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\).\n\n#### Non\u2011sunny lines\nAfter removing the points already covered, the remaining points of \\(T\\) are\n\n\\[\nT' = T\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{(i,1),(i+1,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T'\\) satisfy either \\(b\\ge 3\\) or \\(a\\ge k+2\\).\n\n- For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(3\\le j\\le n\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n  \\[\n  H_j:\\; y=j .\n  \\]\n\n  The intersection \\(H_j\\cap T\\) consists of the points \\((1,j),(2,j),\\dots ,(n+1-j,j)\\); all of them lie in \\(T'\\).\n\n- Finally take the vertical line  \n\n  \\[\n  V:\\; x=k+2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Its intersection with \\(T\\) is the set\n  \\[\n  \\{(k+2,1), (k+2,2),\\dots ,(k+2, n+1-(k+2))\\}.\n  \\]\n  The first two points \\((k+2,1)\\) and \\((k+2,2)\\) are not among those already covered (they would require \\(i=k+1\\) or \\(i=k\\), which are impossible because \\(i\\le k\\)). Hence all points of this set belong to \\(T'\\).\n\nThe number of horizontal lines used is \\((n-2)-k\\) (they correspond to \\(j=3,4,\\dots ,n\\) after the two rows \\(y=1,2\\) have been partially used by the sunny lines). Together with the single vertical line we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical}) = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(T\\) lies on at least one of these lines:\n- points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\) are on \\(L_i\\);\n- points with \\(b\\ge 3\\) are on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n- points with \\(b\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) and \\(a\\ge k+2\\) are on the vertical line \\(x=k+2\\).\n\nThus the required configuration exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\nThe construction works for the extreme values as well:\n- \\(k=0\\): we use only the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny).\n- \\(k=n-2\\): we use the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\) together with the single vertical line \\(x=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final answer\n\nCombining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the explicit construction for every\n\\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
