). Hence a non\u2011vertical line can contain **at most one** lattice point of any given column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Necessity of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)\n\nConsider the first column, \\(x=1\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x=1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n\\) distinct points.\n\n*If the line \\(x=1\\) is not present* in the configuration, each of these \\(n\\) points must be covered by a different line that is not vertical. By the observation above, a non\u2011vertical line can contain at most one of them, so we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011vertical lines. Adding any further lines needed for the remaining points would give more than \\(n\\) lines in total, which is impossible. Therefore the line \\(x=1\\) **must** belong to every admissible configuration.\n\nNow remove the column \\(x=1\\) from consideration. The next column, \\(x=2\\), contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,(2,n-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n-1\\) points. The same reasoning shows that the line \\(x=2\\) must also be present; otherwise we would need at least \\(n-1\\) additional non\u2011vertical lines, exceeding the allowed total.\n\nProceeding inductively, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the column \\(x=i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Since we have already used the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=i-1\\), the only way to keep the total number of lines at most \\(n\\) is to include the vertical line \\(x=i\\) as well; otherwise we would need at least \\(n+1-i\\) new non\u2011vertical lines, and the cumulative count would surpass \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently **all** vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nmust appear in any configuration satisfying the covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Counting sunny