nny line \\(y=\\sqrt2\\,x\\) (which passes only through \\((2,1)\\)).  \n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   \\(T_4\\) consists of the points with \\(a+b\\le5\\); the interior set is \\(\\{(2,2)\\}\\).  \n   - \\(k=0\\): take \\(L_0:x=1\\), \\(y=1\\), \\(x=2\\), and \\(x=3\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   - \\(k=1\\): as above, replace the line \\(x=3\\) by the sunny line \\(y-2=\\sqrt2\\,(x-2)\\) which covers \\((2,2)\\).  \n   - \\(k=2\\): keep \\(L_0\\) and \\(y=1\\); use the two sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     y-2=\\sqrt2\\,(x-2),\\qquad\n     y-1=\\sqrt2\\,(x-3),\n     \\]\n     which cover \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) respectively.  \n\n   In each case the required number of sunny lines \\(k\\) (with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) is realized.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 a non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\) is unavoidable. Lemma\u202f2 shows that a second non\u2011sunny line is also unavoidable (the point \\((n,1)\\) cannot be covered by a sunny line without leaving some other point of the leg \\(y=1\\) uncovered). Hence any admissible configuration contains **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. This proves the upper bound (1), which together with the constructions above yields the exact set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]"

