tion**\n\nWe work with the integer lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(|P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) such points.\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) be \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P_{n}\\).\nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)=|L\\cap P_{n}|\n\\]\n\nthe number of lattice points of \\(P_{n}\\) that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n*If \\(L\\) is sunny*, then its slope is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nTwo distinct points of \\(P_{n}\\) have different sums \\(a+b\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P_{n}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)\\le 1\\qquad\\text{for sunny }L. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L\\) is not sunny* (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or \\(x+y=0\\)), then \\(L\\) contains at least two points of \\(P_{n}\\). Indeed:\n\n* parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis: \\(L\\) has equation \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the points \\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\) all belong to \\(P_{n}\\);\n* parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis: analogous;\n* parallel to \\(x+y=0\\): \\(L\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); the points \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\) lie in \\(P_{n}\\).\n\nThus for a non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)\\ge 2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSumming \\(\\mu(L)\\) over all lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mu(L_{i})\\;=\\;|P_{n}|\\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(L_{i}\\).  \nUsing (1) and (2) in (3) we get\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;=\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mu(L_{i})\n\\;\\le\\; s\\cdot 1+(n-s)\\cdot (n) \\quad\\text