t \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe shall produce a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(n+1)}_{k}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(P_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(i) Obtaining a configuration with the same number of sunny lines.**  \n\nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_{k}\\) (which exists by the hypothesis).  \nAll points of \\(P_{n}\\) are already covered.  \nThe new set \\(P_{n+1}\\setminus P_{n}\\) consists of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nThese points lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{*}:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is **non\u2011sunny** (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAdd \\(L_{*}\\) to \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_{k}\\).  \nWe obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(P_{n+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have a configuration with **at most** \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(ii) Raising the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n\nSuppose we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\(n+1\\) that contains \\(t\\) sunny lines, where \\(t\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect a non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\) of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, the set \\(M\\cap P_{n+1}\\) consists of at least two points, say \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) with \\(a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}=c\\) (the common sum equals the constant defining \\(M\\)).  \n\nChoose a sunny line \\(S\\) passing through **exactly one** of these two points, for instance the line through \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) with slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nReplace \\(M\\) by \\(S\\).  \n\n*The covering property is preserved*: the point \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) remains covered (now by \\(S\\)); the point \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) is still covered because it lies on some other line of the family (otherwise \\(M\\) would have been the unique line containing it, contradicting the distinctness of the lines).  \n\nThus we have produced a new family with the same 