uitable family exists, and the upper bound shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are  \n\n\\[\n|P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints in \\(P_{n}\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) set \\(\\mu(L)=|L\\cap P_{n}|\\).\n\n*If \\(L\\) is sunny*, two distinct points of \\(P_{n}\\) have different sums \\(a+b\\); a sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence it can meet the set \\(\\{a+b=c\\}\\) for at most one value of \\(c\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)\\le 1\\qquad\\text{for sunny }L. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L\\) is not sunny* (parallel to one of the three forbidden directions), then \\(L\\) contains at least two points of \\(P_{n}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n) &\\Longrightarrow (1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\in P_{n},\\\\\nx=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n) &\\Longrightarrow (c,1),(c,2),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\in P_{n},\\\\\nx+y=d\\;(2\\le d\\le n+1) &\\Longrightarrow (1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\in P_{n}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)\\ge 2\\qquad\\text{for non‑sunny }L. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(P_{n}\\).  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSumming \\(\\mu(L_i)\\) and using (2)–(3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|P_{n}|=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mu(L_i)\n\\ge s\\cdot 1+(n-s)\\cdot 2=2n-s .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSo any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--