qquad\\text{for non‑sunny }L. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(P_{n}\\).  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSumming \\(\\mu(L_i)\\) and using (2)–(3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|P_{n}|=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mu(L_i)\n\\ge s\\cdot 1+(n-s)\\cdot 2=2n-s .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSo any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(P_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n\n  They cover all six points and none is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) and replace the third line by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n  The three lines \\(\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=x\\}\\) still contain all six points (the point \\((2,2)\\) lies on \\(y=x\\)).  \n\nThus configurations exist for \\(k=0,1\\). By (4) we must have \\(k\\le 1\\) when \\(n=3\\); the base case is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there is a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step: from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\). We construct a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(n+1)}_{k}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(P_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### (i) Obtaining a configuration with the same number of sunny lines  \n\nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_{k}\\).  \nAll points of \\(P_{n}\\) are already covered.  \nThe new points in \\(P_{n+1}\\setminus P_{n}\\) are precisely those with \\(a+b=n+2\\); they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{*}:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAdd \\(L_{*}\\) to \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_{k}\\).  \nThe resulting family has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers all of \\(P_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have a configuration with **at most** \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### (ii) Raising the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal M\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(P_{n+1}\\) that contains \\(t\\) sunny lines, where \\(t\\le n-2\\).  \nBecause \\(t\\le n-2\\), at least three lines of \\(\\mathcal M\\) are non‑sunny.  \nPick one non‑sunny line \\(N\\). By the discussion preceding (3), \\(N\\) contains at least two points of \\(P_{n+1}\\); denote two of them by \\(P_{1}=(a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\(P_{2}=(a_{2},b_{2})\\).\n\nConsider the sunny line \\(S\\) that passes through \\(P_{1}\\) and has slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) (such a line exists because we may choose any slope other than the three forbidden ones).  \n\nReplace the line \\(N\\) by \\(S\\).  \nThe point \\(P_{1}\\) remains covered (now by \\(S\\)).  \nThe point \\(P_{2}\\) is still covered because it lies on at least one other line of \\(\\mathcal M\\): if \\(N\\) were the **only** line containing \\(P_{2}\\), then removing \\(N\\) would uncover \\(P_{2}\\); however, the set \\(P_{n+1}\\) contains more points than the total capacity of sunny lines (each sunny line can cover at most one point), so at least one other line must already pass through \\(P_{2}\\). Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\nThe new family still has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one (we added a sunny line and removed a non‑sunny one).  \n\nRepeating this replacement as long as the number of sunny lines is below \\(n-1\\) yields configurations with every integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy induction, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (4) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, while the inductive construction proves that every number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
