ercepts \\(b_{i}\\) so that each sunny line passes through a **different** point of \\(S\\); this is always possible because \\(|S|\\ge n\\ge k\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines already cover \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Step\u202fb \u2013 Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines will be taken from the three forbidden directions.  \n   - Use \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor\\) horizontal lines \\(y=c_{j}\\) with distinct integer constants \\(c_{j}\\).  \n   - Use \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor\\) vertical lines \\(x=d_{j}\\) with distinct integer constants \\(d_{j}\\).  \n   - Use the rest as diagonal lines \\(x+y=e_{j}\\) with distinct integer constants \\(e_{j}\\).  \n\n   Because each horizontal (resp. vertical) line contains exactly one lattice point of \\(S\\) (the one whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals the line\u2019s constant), we can choose the constants so that the horizontal and vertical lines cover **new** points of \\(S\\) not already covered by the sunny lines.  \n\n   A diagonal line \\(x+y=e\\) contains at most two points of \\(S\\); we select the constants \\(e\\) so that each diagonal line contributes two fresh points whenever possible, and a single fresh point when only one remains.  \n\n   By a straightforward greedy selection (always pick a still\u2011uncovered point of \\(S\\) and assign it to a line of the appropriate direction) we can guarantee that the total number of points covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k) \\quad\\text{(horizontal/vertical)}\\;+\\;2\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor \\quad\\text{(diagonal)}\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k .\n   \\]  \n\n   Since \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), after the \\(k\\) sunny lines have covered \\(k\\) points, the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\) points can always be assigned to the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines as described.  \n\n   Hence for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility result for \\(k=0\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\) with the explicit constructions for all other admissible values yields the complete description of the set of possible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary sunny line for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (with real constant \\(c\\)) contains at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(c\\notin\\mathbb Z\\) then no point of \\(S\\) lies on the line because all points of \\(S\\) have integer \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \nIf \\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\) then the points of \\(S\\) on the line are \\(\\{(a,c)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a\\le n+1-c\\}\\).  \nAll such points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so they lie on the same horizontal line; however two distinct horizontal lines would have different constants, hence a *single* horizontal line can be used for at most one value of \\(c\\). Consequently the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by all horizontal lines together is at most the number of horizontal lines present. \u220e\n\nThe same argument gives:\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Lemma 3.*  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=d\\) with constant \\(d\\)) contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(d\\notin\\mathbb Z\\) there is no lattice point on the line.  \nIf \\(d\\in\\mathbb Z\\) then the integer solutions of \\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) are \\((1,d-1),\\dots,(d-1,1)\\).  \nBecause of the extra condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(d\\le n+1\\).  \nThus for a fixed integer \\(d\\) there are at most \\(d-1\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\) on the line, but any two distinct diagonal lines have different constants, so each diagonal line contributes at most two *new* points that are not already covered by other diagonal lines (the two points are symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=y\\)). \u220e\n\nNow suppose that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Let \\(h,v,d\\) be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines respectively; then  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nh+v+2d \\le (h+v)+2d = (n-d)+2d = n+d. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n+d\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad d\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d\\le n\\), inequality (4) forces  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;n\\le3.\n\\]\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny is impossible; at least one sunny line must appear. For \\(n=3\\) the inequality becomes \\(d\\ge3\\), which is compatible with \\(d=3\\) and \\(h=v=0\\). Indeed the three diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\qquad x+y=3,\\qquad x+y=4\n\\]  \n\ncover exactly the six points of \\(S\\) (see (1) with \\(n=3\\)). Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable only when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of configurations for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each \\(n\\ge3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nk=0 &\\text{if }n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n1\\le k\\le n &\\text{if }n\\ge4,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]  \n\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0,\\;n=3\\)\n\nAs noted above, the three diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x+y=2,\\qquad L_{2}:x+y=3,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4\n\\]  \n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),\n\\]  \n\nwhich are precisely the set \\(S\\) for \\(n=3\\). Hence the required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) (any \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nLet us fix a slope \\(m\\) that is **not** equal to \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); for concreteness take \\(m=2\\).  \nAll lines of the form  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = 2x + b_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]  \n\nare sunny because their direction vector \\((1,2)\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n\nBecause \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge n\\ge k\\), we can select \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(S\\), say \\((a_i,b_i')\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\), and define \\(b_i = b_i' - 2a_i\\). Then each line \\(L_i\\) passes through the chosen point \\((a_i,b_i')\\). By taking the points distinct we guarantee that the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct.\n\nThus after this step exactly \\(k\\) points of \\(S\\) are already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remaining points.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS' = S\\setminus\\{(a_i,b_i')\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\},\n\\qquad |S'| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nWe must cover all points of \\(S'\\) with the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines, which we are free to choose among the three forbidden directions.\n\nWe proceed greedily:\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) that still appears as a \\(y\\)-coordinate of some uncovered point of \\(S'\\), introduce the horizontal line \\(y=c\\). This line covers exactly one uncovered point (the one whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(c\\) and whose \\(x\\)-coordinate satisfies the inequality \\(x\\le n+1-c\\)). Continue until either all uncovered points are exhausted or we have used \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor\\) horizontal lines.\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nApply the same idea to the remaining uncovered points, using vertical lines \\(x=d\\) for suitable integers \\(d\\). Again each such line covers exactly one new point, and we use at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor\\) of them.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nAll points that are still uncovered after the previous two steps have distinct values of \\(x+y\\) (otherwise they would have been removed by a horizontal or vertical line). Group the remaining points into pairs having the same sum \\(e\\); each pair can be covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=e\\). If an odd point remains, it is covered by a diagonal line on its own. The number of diagonal lines needed is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{|S'|-\\text{(points already covered by horiz./vert.)}}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n\\le n-k-\\;2\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSumming the three groups we obtain exactly \\(n-k\\) lines:  \n\\[\n\\underbrace{\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Big\\rfloor}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n+\\underbrace{\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Big\\rfloor}_{\\text{vertical}}\n+\\underbrace{\\Big(n-k-2\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Big\\rfloor\\Big)}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n= n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny by construction, they are distinct (different constants), and together they cover every point of \\(S'\\). Consequently the whole family of \\(n\\) lines covers all points of \\(S\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\n- For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n  The case \\(k=0\\) is realised by the three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\); the cases \\(k=1,2,3\\) follow from the general construction above.\n\n- For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\nThus the complete description is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ if }n=3,\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 1\\le k\\le n\\text{ for }n\\ge4\\; } .\n\\]"
