---------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines (proof by contradiction)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **more than \\(n\\)** sunny lines; i.e. the number of sunny lines is \\(k\\ge n+1\\).\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence its slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nTwo distinct sunny lines can intersect at most once; consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the set \\(S\\). (If a sunny line contained two distinct points of \\(S\\), those two points would have the same sum of coordinates, forcing the line to be of the form \\(x+y=c\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore not sunny.)\n\nTherefore the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the \\(k\\ge n+1\\) sunny lines is at most \\(k\\). The remaining \\(n-k\\le -1\\) lines are non‑sunny; each non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or of the form \\(x+y=c\\). Such a line can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S\\), but in any case the total number of points covered by **all** \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le n\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). Equality would require every non‑sunny line to be of the form \\(x+y=c\\) and to pass through exactly \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S\\), which is impossible when \\(k\\ge n+1\\) because then fewer than \\(n\\) lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) remain. Hence the covering condition cannot be satisfied, contradicting the assumption.\n\nThus **no** configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines; i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence of a sunny line through a prescribed point).  \n\nFor any \\((a_0,b_0)\\in S\\) there exists a sunny line passing through it.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the point \\((a_0+1,b_0+2)\\). The line through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and this second point has slope \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the line is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny line).  \n\nLet \\(c\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) and let \\((a,b)\\in L_c\\cap S\\).  \nIf we delete the line \\(L_c\\) and insert a sunny line \\(M\\) through \\((a,b)\\) (as supplied by Lemma 2), the resulting family of \\(n\\) lines still covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(S\\) different from \\((a,b)\\) remain covered because they lie on one of the unchanged lines \\(L_{c'}\\) with \\(c'\\neq c\\).  \nThe point \\((a,b)\\) is now covered by the newly added sunny line \\(M\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Start** with the family \\(\\{L_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) from Lemma 1 (all non‑sunny).  \n2. **Select points.** Choose a set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{P}=\\{(a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_k,b_k)\\}\\subset S\n   \\]\n\n   such that the points lie on distinct lines \\(L_{c_i}\\) (possible because there are \\(n\\) distinct lines and \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n3. **Create sunny lines.** For each \\((a_i,b_i)\\) apply Lemma 2 to obtain a sunny line \\(M_i\\) through that point.  \n   To guarantee that the \\(M_i\\) are pairwise distinct, we may use the construction in Lemma 2 with a different second point for each \\(i\\) (e.g. \\((a_i+1,b_i+2+i)\\)), which yields distinct slopes.  \n4. **Replace.** Delete the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{c_i}\\) and insert the sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\).\n\nThe resulting collection consists of  \n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(M_i\\), and  \n* the \\(n-k\\) remaining lines \\(L_c\\) with \\(c\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\).\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct: the \\(M_i\\) have distinct slopes, and each remaining \\(L_c\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with a different constant \\(c\\).\n\nBy Lemma 3, after each replacement every point of \\(S\\) is still covered; after performing all \\(k\\) replacements the covering condition holds for the whole family. Consequently we have produced a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom the contradiction argument we have shown that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
