 non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).** \\(\\tag{3}\\)\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S\\).\nAssume exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.\nBy (3) the non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(S\\).\nSince all \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n>0\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the sharper bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\,}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently no configuration can have fewer than \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) and set  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le m\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor).\n\\]\n\nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny part ( \\(m\\) lines)\n\nTake the following \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2, \\dots , y=m\\);\n* Vertical   lines \\(x=1, x=2, \\dots , x=m\\);\n* Diagonal   lines \\(x+y=n+1, x+y=n, \\dots , x+y=n+2-m\\).\n\nBecause \\(m\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), these three families are pairwise disjoint (no two of them coincide) and altogether consist of exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines.  \n\nA direct inspection shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with either \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) or \\(a+b\\ge n+2-m\\) lies on one of the chosen non‑sunny lines.  \nConsequently the only points of \\(S\\) **not** covered by the non‑sunny part are those satisfying  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1,\\qquad b\\ge m+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1-m .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe set described by (5) is precisely the triangular lattice  \n\n\\[\nT_m:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge m+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1-m\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich, after translating by \\((-m,-m)\\), is isomorphic to the original set \\(S\\) with side length \\(m\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus after the non‑sunny lines we have reduced the covering problem to covering the smaller triangle \\(T_m\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The sunny part ( \\(k\\) lines)\n\nWe now cover \\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*First* we use \\(m\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,m\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(m+1)=i\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((m+1,m+1)\\) and has slope \\(i\\); consequently it meets the hypotenuse of \\(T_m\\) (the segment \\(a+b=n+1-m\\)) at a distinct lattice point.  A standard induction on the “Manhattan distance’’ shows that the collection \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\}\\) covers **all** points of \\(T_m\\): any point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) can be reached from the vertex \\((m+1,m+1)\\) by a sequence of steps \\((1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\); each such step lies on one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) with appropriate \\(i\\).\n\nThus the \\(m\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\) already cover the whole set \\(T_m\\).  \n\n*Second*, we still have to reach the total of \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Since \\(k=m+(k-m)\\), we simply add any \\(k-m\\) additional sunny lines (for instance, lines of slopes \\(m+1,m+2,\\dots ,k\\) passing through the point \\((0,0)\\)).  These extra lines are unnecessary for covering, but they increase the sunny count to exactly \\(k\\).\n\nAll added lines are sunny because none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Distinctness of all lines\n\nThe \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines belong to three different families (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) and are pairwise distinct by construction.  \nThe \\(m\\) sunny lines