w \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n   Because the slopes are all distinct, the lines are distinct.  \n   Every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to the line that contains its first\u2011coordinate\u2011\\(1\\) neighbour, so the whole set is covered. All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n5. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines (Lemma 2).**  \n   In any admissible configuration at most one line can be parallel to each of the three forbidden directions.  \n   *Proof.* Suppose two distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are both vertical, say \\(x=a\\) and \\(x=a'\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\).  \n   The points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a',1)\\) are both in \\(S_{n}\\) (because \\(a,a'\\le n\\)). They lie on different lines, so each of those two vertical lines is needed to cover its own column. If a third vertical line existed, it would be redundant, contradicting the requirement that the family consists of exactly \\(n\\) *distinct* lines covering all points (any redundancy would allow us to delete a line and still cover all points, contradicting minimality of the size \\(n\\)). The same argument applies to horizontal lines and to lines of slope \\(-1\\). \u220e  \n\n   Hence the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(3\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k \\ge n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Eliminating the case \\(k=n-1\\) (Lemma 3).**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration exists with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line. By Lemma\u202f2 the non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; without loss of generality assume it is vertical, say \\(x=a_{0}\\).  \n\n   The vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\) can cover at most the points \\(\\{(a_{0},b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a_{0}\\}\\). All remaining points of \\(S_{n}\\) have first coordinate different from \\(a_{0}\\) and therefore must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   Each sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,-1\\); consequently its intersection with the vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\) is a single point. Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points on the column \\(x=a_{0}\\).  \n   But the column contains \\(n+1-a_{0}\\ge 2\\) points (since \\(a_{0}\\le n-1\\)). For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-a_{0} > n-1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction. The same contradiction is obtained if the unique non\u2011sunny line is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\). Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n7. **Realising every admissible value \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We now construct, for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines as in step\u202f4 (different slopes, each passing through a distinct point of the first row).  \n   - To obtain the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines, use the three non\u2011sunny directions as follows:\n     * If \\(n-t\\ge 3\\), take one vertical line \\(x=1\\), one horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and one line of slope \\(-1\\), namely \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n     * If \\(n-t=2\\), take a vertical line \\(x=1\\) and a horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n     * If \\(n-t=1\\), take a vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n\n   One checks directly that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies either on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (all points with first coordinate \\(1\\)), on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (all points with second coordinate \\(1\\)), on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the points on the hypotenuse of the triangle), or on one of the \\(t\\) sunny lines (which were chosen to pass through the remaining points of the first row, thereby covering the whole first row and, by their distinct slopes, covering all interior points). Because the slopes of the sunny lines are all different from \\(0,-1\\), each such line meets each of the three non\u2011sunny lines in at most one point, guaranteeing that no point is left uncovered. The total number of lines is exactly \\(t+(n-t)=n\\), and the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(t\\).\n\n   This construction works for every \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\); the case \\(t=n\\) was handled in step\u202f4, and the case \\(t=n-1\\) is impossible by Lemma\u202f3.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the constructions for \\(k=0\\), for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\), and for \\(k=n\\), together with Lemma\u202f3 which excludes \\(k=n-1\\), we obtain the complete classification:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. every non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines, except the single value \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe are asked to determine all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are **sunny**, i.e. are not parallel to any of  \n\n   \\[\n   x=0,\\qquad y=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Characterisation of non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nA line that is not sunny is parallel to exactly one of the three forbidden directions; consequently it is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\),\n\nfor some constant \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n*Proof.* By definition a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to at least one of the three given lines. Since the three directions are pairwise distinct, a line can be parallel to at most one of them. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (At most three non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nIn any admissible configuration the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most three; equivalently  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-3 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Suppose two distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are both vertical, say \\(x=a\\) and \\(x=a'\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\).  \nBoth columns \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1\\}\\) and \\(\\{(a',b)\\mid b\\ge 1\\}\\) intersect \\(S_{n}\\) (because \\(a,a'\\le n\\)).  \nIf a third vertical line existed, one of the vertical lines would be redundant: removing it would still leave a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used. The same argument applies to horizontal lines and to lines of slope \\(-1\\). Hence at most one line of each forbidden direction can appear, giving at most three non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (The case \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible)\n\nNo configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line exists.\n\n*Proof.* Assume such a configuration exists. By Lemma\u202f1 the unique non\u2011sunny line is, after a possible relabelling, vertical: \\(x=a_{0}\\).  \n\nThe column \\(x=a_{0}\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\nC_{a_{0}}=\\{(a_{0},b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a_{0}\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(|C_{a_{0}}|=n+1-a_{0}\\ge 2\\) because \\(a_{0}\\le n-1\\) (otherwise the column would be empty).  \n\nEach sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore it meets the vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\) in at most one point. With \\(n-1\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_{a_{0}}\\).  \n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|C_{a_{0}}| = n+1-a_{0} > n-1\n\\]\n\nfor every admissible \\(a_{0}\\) (indeed \\(a_{0}\\le n-1\\) implies \\(n+1-a_{0}\\ge 2\\) and the inequality is strict unless \\(a_{0}=n\\), which cannot happen because then the column would be empty). Hence some point of \\(C_{a_{0}}\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis. The same contradiction arises if the unique non\u2011sunny line is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of lines for all admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}: x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on \\(L_{a}\\).  \nAll lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny. Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 2.  \\(k=n\\)\n\nChoose \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = (i+1)x + (1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(i+1\\) are all different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), so each line is sunny, and the lines are distinct because their slopes differ.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\); the line \\(L_{a}\\) passes through the point \\((a,1)\\) and, having slope \\(a+1\\), also passes through \\((a,b)\\). Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered. Consequently \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n#### 3.  \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(t=k\\) and write \\(r=n-t\\) (so \\(2\\le r\\le n-1\\)).  \nWe construct a family consisting of\n\n* \\(t\\) sunny lines, and  \n* \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines, using at most one line of each forbidden direction.\n\n**Sunny part.**  \nSelect \\(t\\) distinct slopes \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{t}\\) from the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (all different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) let the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = m_{i}x + c_{i},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(c_{i}\\) is chosen so that \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the point \\((1,i)\\).  \nThus the \\(t\\) sunny lines cover the whole first row \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le t\\}\\).\n\n**Non\u2011sunny part.**  \nAccording to the value of \\(r\\) we add:\n\n* if \\(r\\ge 3\\): the vertical line \\(V:\\;x=1\\), the horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=1\\), and the line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\);