            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\nx=0,\\qquad y=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nA line that is not sunny is parallel to exactly one of the three forbidden directions; consequently it is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\),\n\nfor some constant \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n*Proof.* By definition a non‑sunny line is parallel to at least one of the three given lines. The three directions are pairwise distinct, so a line can be parallel to at most one of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\). We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. The sunny lines\n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nb_{1},b_{2},\\dots ,b_{k}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 1\\le b_{i}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(m_{i}\\) from the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{-1,0\\},\n\\]\n\nensuring that the slopes \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) are pairwise different.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = m_{i}x + (b_{i}-m_{i}) .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the point \\((1,b_{i})\\) and its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\), each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny. Distinct slopes guarantee that the \\(S_{i}\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. The non‑sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall use at most one line of each of the three non‑sunny types; this is always enough because \\(r\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(r\\ge 3\\):* take the three lines  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=1,\\qquad H:\\; y=1,\\qquad D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(r=2\\):* take \\(V\\) and \\(H\\).\n\n*If \\(r=1\\):* take only \\(V\\).\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny (they are respectively vertical, horizontal, and of slope \\(-1\\)), and they are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Coverage of \\(S_{n}\\)\n\nWe show that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n*Case 1 – first column (\\(a=1\\)).*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\) (present whenever \\(r\\ge 1\\)). If \\(r=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\)), then \\((1,b)\\) is one of the points \\((1,b_{i})\\) used to define a sunny line, so it lies on the corresponding \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n*Case 2 – first row (\\(b=1\\)).*  \nAnalogously, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (present whenever \\(r\\ge 2\\)). If \\(r\\le 1\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) is \\((1,1)\\) or \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a>1\\); when \\(a>1\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) so \\(a\\le n\\). Choose \\(b_{i}=1\\) among the \\(b_{i}\\)’s (possible because the set \\(\\{b_{i}\\}\\) can be any \\(k\\) distinct numbers; if \\(k=0\\) this case does not arise because \\(r\\ge 2\\) when \\(k=0\\)). Hence \\((a,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) that passes 