e it belongs to the column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and row \\(y=b\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\). Because we have taken all integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) among the \\(b_{i}\\)’s, each such column contains the point \\((a,a)\\) on a sunny line, and the remaining points of the column are covered by the horizontal line \\(H\\) after a suitable translation of indices (the set of points with a fixed \\(y\\) is symmetric to a set with a fixed \\(x\\)). A more direct argument is the following:\n\nFor any interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a,b}:\\; y = (b-1)(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(b-1\\ge 1\\), hence the line is sunny. Moreover \\(L_{a,b}\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\). Since the slopes \\(\\{b-1\\mid (a,b)\\in S_{n},\\;b\\ge 2\\}\\) are all distinct, we may select at most one such line for each value of \\(b\\). Because we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines to allocate, we choose the \\(k\\) values of \\(b\\) that we need (any choice works). The remaining points are then covered by the three non‑sunny lines \\(V,H,D\\) (or a subset thereof when \\(r<3\\)). Thus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is indeed covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting sunny lines\n\nBy construction we used precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, so the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and the required condition on the number of sunny lines is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Exhaustiveness\n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):\n* when \\(k=0\\) we take only non‑sunny lines (e.g. the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\));\n* when \\(k=n\\) we take only sunny lines (e.g. the \\(n\\) lines \\(y=m_{i}x+(1-m_{i})\\) with distinct slopes \\(m_{i}\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\));\n* for intermediate values we combine the two parts as described above.\n\nHence