2013 Case\u202f2 (points \\((1,b)\\), \\(b\\ge2\\))\n\n> \u201cIf \\(r\\ge 1\\) the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains them.  \n> If \\(r=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\)) then \\(b\\) equals one of the slopes \\(s_i\\) because the set \\(\\{s_i\\}\\) can be chosen to be \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n> For that \\(i\\) we have \\(b=s_i=s_i\\cdot1-(s_i-1)\\), so \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n* When \\(r=0\\) we have \\(k=n\\). The construction of Block\u202fA uses the set \\(\\{s_i\\}\\) of size \\(k=n\\). The solution claims we can choose \\(\\{s_i\\}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). This indeed makes each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1\\) equal to some \\(s_i\\). However, the point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1\\) satisfies the line equation \\(y = s_i x-(s_i-1)\\) **only** when \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=1\\), not \\(y=b\\). Substituting \\(x=1\\) yields \\(y = s_i-(s_i-1)=1\\). Thus \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\) is **not** on any \\(S_i\\). The algebraic statement \u201c\\(b = s_i\\cdot1-(s_i-1)\\)\u201d is false; the right\u2011hand side equals 1 for any \\(s_i\\). Consequently the case fails for \\(k=n\\). This is a fundamental flaw in the covering argument.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Coverage proof \u2013 Case\u202f3 (points \\((a,1)\\), \\(a\\ge2\\))\n\n> \u201cSymmetric to Case\u202f2: they are covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) when \\(r\\ge 2\\); otherwise, when \\(r\\le 1\\) we again use the sunny lines.  \n> Indeed, if \\(a=s_i\\) for some \\(i\\) then\n> \\[\n> 1=s_i\\,a-(s_i-1)=s_i^{2}-(s_i-1),\n> \\]\n> which holds precisely because \\(a=s_i\\) and the right\u2011hand side simplifies to \\(1\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (Algebraic mistake):**  \n* Substituting \\(a=s_i\\) into \\(y = s_i x-(s_i-1)\\) gives \\(y = s_i^2-(s_i-1)=s_i^2-s_i+1\\), which equals 1 **only** when \\(s_i=1\\). Since sunny slopes satisfy \\(s_i\\ge2\\), the equality is false. Hence the argument that \\((a,1)\\) lies on a sunny line is incorrect. The case fails for many points, especially when \\(r\\le1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Coverage proof \u2013 Case\u202f4 (points on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\))\n\n> \u201cWhen \\(r\\ge 3\\) the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains all such points.  \n> If \\(r\\le 2\\) the hypotenuse consists of at most two lattice points, namely \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); each of them is covered by the argument of Cases\u202f2 and\u202f3 because the numbers \\(n\\) and \\(1\\) belong to the set \\(\\{s_i\\}\\cup\\{1\\}\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap / Incorrect Claim:**  \n* The statement \u201cthe hypotenuse consists of at most two lattice points when \\(r\\le2\\)\u201d is false. The set of points satisfying \\(x+y=n+1\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) always contains exactly \\(n-1\\) lattice points, regardless of \\(r\\). For example, with \\(n=5\\) the hypotenuse contains \\((1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\\). The solution\u2019s claim that only \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) may appear is unjustified, leaving many points uncovered.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Coverage proof \u2013 Case\u202f5 (interior points)\n\n> \u201cFor such a point set \\(t=a+b\\) (so \\(3\\le t\\le n\\)).  \n> Choose the sunny line \\(S_i\\) whose slope equals \\(t\\); this is possible because the collection \\(\\{s_i\\}\\) can be taken to contain **all** integers from \\(2\\) up to \\(n+1\\) (when \\(k=n\\)) and, when \\(k<n\\), we simply add the missing integers to the set of non\u2011sunny lines\u2026  \n> Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n> \\[\n> y = t a-(t-1)= a+b-a+1 = b+1 .\n> \\]  \n> Hence the point \\((a,b+1)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\). \u2026\u201d\n\n**Issues \u2013 Multiple Critical Errors:**  \n\n1. **Incorrect algebra:** Starting from \\(y = t x-(t-1)\\) and substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y = t a-(t-1)\\). Since \\(t = a+b\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   y = (a+b)a-(a+b-1)=a^{2}+ab-a-b+1.\n   \\]  \n   This expression does **not** simplify to \\(b+1\\) (or to \\(b\\) later). The derivation in the solution is erroneous.\n\n2. **Misuse of the line:** Even if the algebra were correct, the point obtained is \\((a,b+1)\\), not the original \\((a,b)\\). The solution then attempts to \u201cmove one unit down\u201d using vertical or horizontal lines, but this relies on the presence of those non\u2011sunny lines, which may be absent (e.g., when \\(k=n\\)). The argument is therefore incomplete.\n\n3. **Dependence on unavailable lines:** For the case \\(k=n\\) (no non\u2011sunny lines), the \u201cmove one unit down\u201d step cannot be performed, yet the solution claims coverage for all interior points. This contradicts the earlier failure to cover \\((1,b)\\) points and shows the construction does not work when all lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Alternative verification (equation (3))\n\n> \u201cDefine \\(m = a+b-1\\) ( \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) ).  \n> The sunny line \\(L_{m}: y = m x-(m-1)\\) belongs to Block\u202fA \u2026 Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (3) yields  \n> \\[\n> y = m a-(m-1)= (a+b-1)a-(a+b-2)= b .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (Algebra again):**  \n* The simplification \\((a+b-1)a-(a+b-2)=b\\) is false. Expanding gives \\(a^{2}+ab-a-a-b+2 = a^{2}+ab-b+2\\), which is not equal to \\(b\\) in general. Hence the claimed equality is incorrect, and the conclusion that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_m\\) is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Counting sunny lines  \n\n> \u201cBy construction Block\u202fA contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and Block\u202fB contains \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines; together they give the required total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Assuming the constructions were correct, the count would be fine. However, as shown above, Block\u202fB may not provide enough lines when \\(r>3\\), and Block\u202fA may not contain enough distinct slopes when \\(k<n\\) but the solution still needs to cover all points. Therefore the counting argument rests on a flawed construction.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Existence for every \\(k\\)\n\n> \u201c*k=0*: take only the three non\u2011sunny lines listed in (2) and, if necessary, add further vertical lines \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny) until we have \\(n\\) lines.  \n> *k=n*: choose the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_{m}\\) with slopes \\(m=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); they are distinct and cover \\(T_{n}\\) by the computation in step\u202f4.  \n> *intermediate k*: combine the two blocks as described.\u201d\n\n**Issues \u2013 Critical Errors:**  \n\n* For \\(k=n\\) the earlier algebraic errors show that the lines \\(L_m\\) do **not** cover all points, especially those with \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\). Hence the claim is false.  \n* For \\(k=0\\) the solution suggests adding vertical lines \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\). These are indeed non\u2011sunny, but the solution does not verify that the resulting set of vertical lines together with the three basic non\u2011sunny lines actually covers every point of \\(T_n\\). For instance, points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) would not lie on any vertical line, nor on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). Thus the coverage claim for \\(k=0\\) is also unsupported.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution contains several **critical errors**:\n\n1. The construction for \\(k=n\\) (all sunny lines) fails to cover points on the line \\(x=1\\) (and symmetrically \\(y=1\\)).  \n2. Algebraic manipulations in Cases\u202f3,\u202f5, and the later verification are incorrect, leading to false statements that certain points lie on the constructed lines.  \n3. The handling of the non\u2011sunny block is incomplete for many values of \\(r\\); the solution does not guarantee a total of \\(n\\) lines nor coverage when \\(r>3\\).  \n4. The claim that the hypotenuse contains at most two lattice points when \\(r\\le2\\) is false.  \n\nBecause the core covering argument collapses for several values of \\(k\\) (in particular \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=0\\)), the conclusion that **all** integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable is unsupported. Consequently, the solution is **invalid**."

