"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines that contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.  \nNo configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is different from the three forbidden directions  \n   \\[\n   \\mathbf v_{x}=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf v_{y}=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf v_{d}=(1,-1).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Characterisation of non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n   A line is **not** sunny iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, i.e. it is of one of the forms  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  The slope of a line is \\(\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\).  \n   The three forbidden directions have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence a line fails to be sunny exactly when its slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal).  Writing the equations of the corresponding families gives (1). \u220e\n\n3. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   The three families (1) are the only ways to obtain a non\u2011sunny line.  \n   In order to cover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\) we must have **at least two** lines from these families:\n\n   * a vertical line \\(x=1\\) is needed to cover the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\);\n   * a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is needed to cover the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\).\n\n   If we used only one non\u2011sunny line, say \\(x=1\\), then the point \\((2,1)\\) would have to lie on a sunny line.  \n   Any sunny line has slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently it cannot pass through both \\((1,1)\\) (which is forced by the vertical line) and \\((2,1)\\).  Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines are indispensable.\n\n   Moreover, the diagonal family \\(x+y=n+1\\) is needed only when we have **exactly** two non\u2011sunny lines; otherwise the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) already cover the whole boundary of the triangle and the interior can be handled by sunny lines (see the construction below).  \n   Therefore the number of non\u2011sunny lines must be **at least** \\(2\\), i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   This proves that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Let \\(r=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  By (2) we have \\(r\\ge 2\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part (Block\u202fB).*  \n   Choose the following two lines, which are always distinct:\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}: x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_{2}: y=1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(r>2\\) we add further non\u2011sunny lines of the diagonal family, all with distinct constants, e.g.\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{3}: x+y=n+1,\\;\n   \\ell_{4}: x+y=n,\\;\n   \\ell_{5}: x+y=n-1,\\dots\n   \\]\n\n   until we have exactly \\(r\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Because each added line belongs to the family \\(x+y=c\\), Lemma\u202f1 guarantees that they are non\u2011sunny.  The constants are chosen so that the lines are distinct; this is always possible because we may take any \\(r-2\\) different integers among \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   *Sunny part (Block\u202fA).*  \n   For each integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m}:\\; y = m x-(m-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_{m}\\) is \\(m\\neq 0,-1\\); consequently each \\(L_{m}\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover \\(L_{m}\\) passes through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=m-(m-1)=1\\).\n\n   We now select **exactly** \\(k\\) of the lines \\(\\{L_{m}\\}\\).  \n   Since \\(k\\le n-2\\), we can choose any \\(k\\) distinct integers from the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\); denote the chosen integers by \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}=L_{m_{i}}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny and distinct (different slopes give different lines).\n\n   *Total family.*  \n   The final collection consists of the \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines from Block\u202fB together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines from Block\u202fA; altogether we have \\(r+k=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n5. **Verification that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Three cases are considered.\n\n   *Case\u202f1 \u2013 points on the left or bottom border.*  \n   If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (3).  \n   If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (3).  \n   Thus all points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) are covered.\n\n   *Case\u202f2 \u2013 points on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).*  \n   If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{3}: x+y=n+1\\) which is present in Block\u202fB whenever \\(r\\ge 3\\).  \n   If \\(r=2\\) (i.e. we have only the two lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\)), then the only points of \\(T_{n}\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); both are already covered by \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) respectively.  Hence every point on the hypotenuse is covered for any admissible \\(r\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f3 \u2013 interior points, i.e. \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\).*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   m = a+b-1\\qquad\\bigl(2\\le m\\le n\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_{m}\\) defined in (4) has equation \\(y = m x-(m-1)\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = m a-(m-1)= (a+b-1)a-(a+b-2)= a^{2}+ab-a-a-b+2\n   = b + (a-1)(a-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge 2\\), the term \\((a-1)(a-1)\\) is a non\u2011negative integer.  However, note that the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\), hence \\(m\\le n\\) and the line \\(L_{m}\\) is among the family \\(\\{L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\).  Moreover, **by construction** we have included in Block\u202fA **all** lines \\(L_{m}\\) with \\(m\\) in the set  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\}^{c}.\n   \\]\n\n   In other words, for every integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) either the line \\(L_{m}\\) is one of the sunny lines we selected, or it is not needed because the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b-1=m\\) is already covered by a non\u2011sunny line.  Indeed, if the line \\(L_{m}\\) is **not** among the selected sunny lines, then \\(m\\) belongs to the set of constants used for the diagonal non\u2011sunny lines in Block\u202fB.  Specifically, we added the diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y = n+2-m,\\qquad m\\in\\{m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contain precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b = n+2-m\\).  Since for an interior point we have \\(a+b = m+1\\le n\\), the equality \\(a+b = n+2-m\\) cannot hold; consequently the interior point cannot be covered by a diagonal non\u2011sunny line.  The only remaining possibility is that the interior point is covered by the sunny line \\(L_{m}\\) **that we have deliberately kept**.  To guarantee this, we simply **choose the set of sunny slopes** to be exactly the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_{1},c_{2},\\dots ,c_{r-2}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{r-2}\\) are the constants appearing in the diagonal non\u2011sunny lines of Block\u202fB.  Because there are \\(r-2\\) such constants and \\(k=n-r\\), the two sets are complementary in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently every integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) appears **either** as the slope of a sunny line **or** as the constant of a diagonal non\u2011sunny line, but never both.\n\n   For an interior point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(m=a+b-1\\).  If \\(m\\) is the constant of a diagonal non\u2011sunny line, then that diagonal line is \\(x+y = n+2-m\\).  Since \\(a+b = m+1\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b = (n+2-m) - (n-m-1) = n+2-m,\n   \\]\n\n   which is impossible because \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Hence \\(m\\) cannot be a diagonal constant; therefore \\(m\\) must be a sunny slope, and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{m}\\).\n\n   Consequently **every interior point** of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to one of the selected sunny lines.\n\n   Combining the three cases, every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n6. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   By construction Block\u202fA contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while Block\u202fB contains the remaining \\(r=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Hence the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   *Necessity*: any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, giving the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   *Sufficiency*: for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) the explicit construction above yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\text{ is admissible iff }0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"

