unny block.*  \n   Take the two lines (2).  If \\(r>2\\) we add \\(r-2\\) distinct diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{d}^{(j)}:\\;x+y=c_{j}\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,r-2),\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   where the constants \\(c_{j}\\) are chosen from the set \\(\\{3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) so that all \\(\\ell_{d}^{(j)}\\) are different.  Because each line in (4) belongs to the family \\(x+y=c\\), Lemma\u202f1 guarantees that they are non\u2011sunny.  Altogether we have exactly \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny block.*  \n   For every interior lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n,\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   consider the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\).  Its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,y-1=\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\,(x-1)\\,}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   i.e. it has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{a,b}= \\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a,b\\ge 2\\), the slope is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore every line of the form (6) is sunny.\n\n   Distinct reduced fractions \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p,q\\ge 1\\) give distinct lines (6).  Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\text{ satisfies }(5)\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal S\\) contains exactly \\(n-2\\) elements: for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) the fraction \\(\\frac{t-1}{1}\\) occurs (coming from the point \\((2,t)\\)), and all other fractions are larger than \\(1\\) and correspond bijectively to the points with \\(a\\ge 3\\).  Consequently \\(|\\mathcal S|=n-2\\).\n\n   From the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (6) we select **exactly** \\(k\\) of them; denote the selected slopes by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Sigma=\\{\\sigma_{1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{k}\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal S .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-2-k = r-2\\) slopes are discarded; for each discarded slope \\(\\sigma\\) we choose a diagonal line from (4) whose constant equals  \n\n