\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(|S_n|=\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (parallel respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nLet a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k_n\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Base case).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(k_3\\ge 1=\\big\\lceil\\frac{3-2}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nIf all three lines were non\u2011sunny, then each would be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nTwo distinct vertical (or horizontal, or diagonal) lines cannot both contain a point of \\(S_3\\) because the points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct values of \\(x+y\\).  \nHence at most three non\u2011sunny lines could be used, but they would be forced to be parallel, contradicting the distinctness of the lines.  \nThus at least one line must be sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Inductive step).  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) every configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) covering \\(S_n\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk_n\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThen any configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk_{n+1}\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{(n+1)-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\cup T,\\qquad \nT=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T\\) consists of the points lying on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\); there are exactly \\(n+1\\) such points.\n\nConsider a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covering \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) be the sub\u2011collection of those lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) that contain at least one point of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince the points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) is a valid configuration for \\(S_n\\); it may contain fewer than \\(n\\) lines because some lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) could be used solely for points of \\(T\\).\n\nObserve the following:\n\n1. **At most one family of non\u2011sunny lines can intersect \\(T\\).**  \n   Suppose a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and a horizontal line \\(y=d\\) both meet \\(T\\). Then the point \\((c,d)\\) would satisfy \\(c+d=n+2\\) and also belong to \\(S_n\\) (since \\(c,d\\ge 1\\) and \\(c+d\\le n+1\\) would be violated). Hence such a pair cannot occur. The same argument excludes simultaneously a vertical (or horizontal) line together with a diagonal line \\(x+y=e\\) intersecting \\(T\\). Consequently, all points of \\(T\\) that are covered by non\u2011sunny lines are covered by lines belonging to **exactly one** of the three families (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).\n\n2. **If a line from that family covers a point of \\(T\\), it cannot also cover any point of \\(S_n\\).**  \n   For a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meeting \\(T\\) we have a point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(c+b=n+2\\). Any other point on the same vertical line has the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(c\\) and a smaller \\(y\\)-coordinate, hence its sum \\(c+y\\le n+1\\); such a point would belong to \\(S_n\\). But then the line would contain two points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with distinct sums, contradicting the fact that a vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore cannot intersect the diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) in more than one point. An analogous argument holds for horizontal and diagonal lines.\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we deduce that **every point of \\(T\\) that is not covered by a sunny line must be covered by a distinct non\u2011sunny line**, and that such a non\u2011sunny line does not belong to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\). Hence the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\