----------------------\n\n#### 2.  A necessary lower bound  \n\nWe prove:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k(\\mathcal L)\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\qquad\\text{for every admissible }\\mathcal L.}\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.1 (Base case \\(n=3\\)).  \n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny covering of these three points by three distinct lines must contain at least one sunny line, i.e. \\(k(\\mathcal L)\\ge1=\\lceil(3-2)/2\\rceil\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf all three lines were non\u2011sunny, each would be vertical, horizontal or diagonal.  \nTwo distinct vertical (resp. horizontal, diagonal) lines cannot both contain a point of \\(S_3\\) because the points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates (resp. distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, distinct values of \\(x+y\\)).  \nThus at most three non\u2011sunny lines could be used, but they would have to be parallel, contradicting the hypothesis that the lines are distinct. Hence at least one line is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n##### Inductive step  \n\nAssume (2.1) holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nConsider a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covering \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\cup T,\\qquad \nT=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T\\) consists of the \\(n+1\\) points lying on the outer diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\) be the subset of lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) that intersect \\(T\\).  \nEach line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is either sunny or belongs to one of the three non\u2011sunny families.\n\n**Claim 2.2.** *All lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) that are non\u2011sunny belong to the same family (all vertical, or all horizontal, or all diagonal).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and a horizontal line \\(y=d\\) both meet \\(T\\).  \nThen \\((c,d)\\in T\\) and satisfies \\(c+d=n+2\\).  \nBut the same point would also belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(c,d\\ge1\\) and \\(c+d=n+2>n+1\\) is impossible; hence such a pair cannot exist.  \nThe same argument excludes a vertical line together with a diagonal line, and a horizontal line together with a diagonal line. \u220e\n\nConsequently, at most one non\u2011sunny family can be used to cover points of \\(T\\).  \n\nIf a line from that family covers a point of \\(T\\), it cannot also cover any point of \\(S_n\\); otherwise the line would intersect the diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) in two distinct points, which is impossible for a line of fixed slope.  \nTherefore every point of \\(T\\) that is not covered by a sunny line requires a **new** non\u2011sunny line that does not appear among the lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\nSince \\(|T|=n+1\\) and a single non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T\\), at least one point of \\(T\\) must be covered by a sunny line. Hence the number of sunny lines increases by at least one when we pass from a covering of \\(S_n\\) to a covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\nk(\\mathcal L_{n+1})\\ge k(\\mathcal L_n)+1 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nApplying the induction hypothesis \\(k(\\mathcal L_n)\\ge\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) and (2.3) gives  \n\n\\[\nk(\\mathcal L_{n+1})\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil+1\n               =\\Big\\lceil\\frac{(n+1)-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus (2.1) holds for \\(n+1\\). By induction it holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Trivial upper bound  \n\nBecause the configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, clearly  \n\n\\[\nk(\\mathcal L)\\le n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Sufficiency: constructing configurations for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\qquad \n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\nWe treat the parity of \\(m\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  The non\u2011sunny backbone  \n\n*If \\(m\\) is even*, write \\(m=2r\\) and take  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=r\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m\\) is odd*, write \\(m=2r+1\\) and take  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=r\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=r\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\) (and possibly \\(D\\)) are non\u2011sunny, pairwise non\u2011parallel, hence distinct.  \nThese lines cover every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with  \n\n\\[\na\\le r\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b\\le r .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(a\\le r\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains the point; if \\(b\\le r\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) does.  \nWhen \\(m\\) is odd the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) additionally covers the whole outer layer \\(\\{a+b=n+1\\}\\), which is useful but not essential for the argument.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS'_n = S_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\text{points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nFrom (4.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS'_n\\subseteq\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge r+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set \\(S'_n\\) is exactly the triangular set \\(S_{n'}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nn' = n-2r = n-m = k .\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered region is a smaller triangle of the same shape, now of size \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  Sunny completion  \n\nWe now cover \\(S_{n'}=S_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause \\(k\\ge\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\ge 2\\) for all admissible \\(k\\) (the only exception is \\(k=n\\), which is handled trivially by taking all lines sunny), the triangle contains at least two rows, so a greedy construction works:\n\n1. List the points of the first row of the triangle, i.e. the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k)\\).  \n2. For each such point that is still uncovered, draw a sunny line through it with a slope that has not been used before (choose, for instance, slopes \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\)).  \n   Because a line of a new slope meets the triangle in at most one point of the first row, the line will also pass through some other points of the triangle, thereby deleting them from the uncovered set.  \n3. Continue until all points are covered.  \n\nSince we start with \\(k\\) points in the first row and each newly drawn line removes at least one of them, the process stops after at most \\(k\\) lines.  \nWe can enforce that it stops **exactly** after \\(k\\) steps by prescribing the slopes so that each line removes precisely one new point of the first row; this is always possible because the slopes are free parameters and the points are in general position with respect to the forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\nThus we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal S\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(S_{n'}\\).\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny, hence none coincides with any of the previously chosen non\u2011sunny lines.  \nMoreover, the slopes are all distinct, guaranteeing that the sunny lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.3  Final configuration  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = (\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\;[\\cup D])\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\nThe number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L| = m + k = n .\n\\]\n\nBy construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is exactly \\(|\\mathcal S| = k\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) proved in Section\u202f2 is necessary, and the explicit construction of Section\u202f4 shows that it is also sufficient.  \nTherefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\big|\\;\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\right\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis solves the problem."
