"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe construction proceeds as follows.\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) lines that are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions\n   (horizontal, vertical or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Choose them so that they together contain all points of the triangular lattice\n   except for exactly \\(k\\) points that we shall later cover with sunny lines.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each of the remaining \\(k\\) uncovered points, draw a distinct line that\n   passes through that point and is not parallel to any of the three forbidden\n   directions.  Because the line is not parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\),\n   it meets each of the previously used non\u2011sunny lines in at most one point,\n   guaranteeing that the \\(k\\) sunny lines are all distinct from the\n   \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny ones.\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); all are distinct; every lattice\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the\n   chosen lines; and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), proving\nthat these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints; geometrically they form a right\u2011angled isosceles triangle of side\nlength \\(n\\) with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A family of non\u2011sunny lines covering most of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThe intersection \\(L_i\\cap\\mathcal{P}\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) together contain **all** points of\n\\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  In particular, for any integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) the\nfirst \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\dots ,L_m\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncover exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Leaving precisely \\(k\\) points uncovered\n\nFix a number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) from (3).  \nThese \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) **except** those\nlying on the remaining vertical lines \\(L_{m+1},\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nConsequently the set of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\bigcup_{i=m+1}^{n}\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the indices in (5) run over exactly \\(k\\) values, the cardinality of\n\\(U\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\sum_{i=m+1}^{n}(n+1-i)=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Covering the uncovered points with sunny lines\n\nWe now construct \\(k\\) **sunny** lines, each passing through a distinct point of\n\\(U\\) and through no other point of \\(U\\).\n\nList the points of \\(U\\) in the order  \n\n\\[\nP_{1,1},\\;P_{2,1},P_{2,2},\\;P_{3,1},P_{3,2},P_{3,3},\\dots ,P_{k,1},\\dots ,P_{k,k},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P_{i,j}=(m+i,\\,j)\\).  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = \\frac{1}{2}\\,x + \\frac{2j-i}{2}\\qquad\\text{with }j=i .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nExplicitly, \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\(P_{i,i}=(m+i,i)\\) and has slope\n\\(\\frac12\\).  Since the slope \\(\\frac12\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\),\neach \\(S_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nBecause the slope is the same for all \\(S_i\\) but the intercepts differ,\nthe lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct.  Moreover, a line of slope\n\\(\\frac12\\) can intersect a vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point; hence\n\\(S_i\\) meets each of the previously chosen vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) in\na single point distinct from the points of \\(U\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines do not coincide with any of the \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nFinally, every point of \\(U\\) lies on one of the lines \\(S_i\\) (by construction),\nand every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\setminus U\\) lies on one of the vertical lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\).  Thus **all** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting the lines\n\nWe have used  \n\n* \\(m=n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines,\n* \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(\\frac12\\) (sunny) lines.\n\nAll together there are \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny.  This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem asks for *non\u2011negative* integers \\(k\\).  \nSince we must have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\); likewise it cannot be negative.  Hence the only possible\nvalues are the integers in the interval \\([0,n]\\).  The construction above\nshows that each of them is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\nprecisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
