"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions, and that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \nHence the admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is constructive.\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny covering.**  \n   Choose the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) for \\(i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (hence non\u2011sunny) and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) **except** the \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines for the remaining points.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x + (n+1-2i) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)).\n   Hence each \\(S_i\\) is **sunny**.  \n   The lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct because they have different\n   intercepts, and each passes through exactly one of the uncovered points\n   \\(P_i\\).\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   * Total number of lines: \\((n-k)+k=n\\).  \n   * Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(S_i\\)).  \n   * Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on a\n     vertical line \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\ge k+1\\) or on one of the sunny lines\n     \\(S_i\\).  \n   * All lines are distinct.\n\nSince the construction works for any \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) and a\nsunny line count cannot exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\), the set of\nadmissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are the lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Non\u2011sunny lines covering all but \\(k\\) points\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i ,\\qquad i=m+1,m+2,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nFor a given \\(i\\) the intersection \\(L_i\\cap\\mathcal{P}\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the collection \\(\\{L_{m+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (there are \\(n-m=k\\) of\nthem) covers **all** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) except the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for each \\(i\\le m\\) the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=i\\), which is **not** among the lines (1); all other points of the column\n\\(x=i\\) have a smaller \\(y\\)-coordinate and are therefore covered by the line\n\\(x=m+1\\) or a larger one.\n\nThus after using the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines we have exactly the uncovered set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Sunny lines covering the uncovered points\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (n+1-2i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is **not** parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis (slope \\(0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical), nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)\n(slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence each \\(S_i\\) is a sunny line.\n\nBecause the intercepts \\(n+1-2i\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), the lines\n\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct.  Substituting \\((i,n+1-i)\\) into (5) shows that\n\\(P_i\\in S_i\\) for every \\(i\\); thus each uncovered point is now covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Counting and distinctness\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nWe have used \\(m=n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, a total\nof  \n\n\\[\nm+k = (n-k)+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, so the required number of sunny\nlines is \\(k\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines\n\\(L_{m+1},\\dots ,L_n\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(m+1\\)) or on the\nsunny line \\(S_i\\) that contains it (if its coordinates are \\((i,n+1-i)\\)).\nHence the first condition of the problem is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem asks for a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) equal to the number of sunny\nlines among a collection of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Obviously\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  The construction above shows that **every** integer in this\ninterval can be achieved.  Therefore no other values are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
