hich is insufficient to replace a line \\(L_{c}\\) that contains \\(c-1\\ge 2\\) points (recall \\(n\\ge3\\)). An analogous argument rules out vertical lines.  \nHence a non‑sunny line must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. it coincides with some \\(L_{c}\\). ∎\n\nTherefore the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines are precisely \\(t\\) distinct members of the family \\(\\{L_{c}\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Counting argument\n\nFix a particular \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe line \\(L_{c}\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T\\).  \nAmong the \\(s\\) sunny lines, at most \\(s\\) of those points can be covered (Lemma 1).  \nThe remaining \\((c-1)-s\\) points must be covered by non‑sunny lines. Since a non‑sunny line that is not \\(L_{c}\\) is parallel to \\(L_{c}\\) and therefore disjoint from \\(L_{c}\\), the only way to cover points of \\(L_{c}\\) is to use the line \\(L_{c}\\) itself. Consequently, for each \\(c\\) we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\bigl\\{1,\\; (c-1)-s\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines. Summing over all \\(c\\) gives a lower bound for the total number of lines required:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{required lines}\n&\\ge \\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\max\\{1,\\;c-1-s\\}\\\\\n&= \\underbrace{\\sum_{c=2}^{s+1}1}_{\\text{first }s\\text{ terms}}+\n   \\underbrace{\\sum_{c=s+2}^{n+1}(c-1-s)}_{\\text{remaining terms}}\\\\[2mm]\n&= s + \\sum_{j=1}^{n-s}(j) \\qquad (j=c-1-s)\\\\\n&= s + \\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe must have this quantity \\(\\le n\\) because only \\(n\\) lines are available. Hence\n\n\\[\ns + \\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsider the function  \n\n\\[\nf(s)=s+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(s\\ge 1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(s)-n = \\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}+s-n\n        = \\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)-2(n-s)}{2}\n        = \\frac{(n-s)(n-s-1)}{2}\\ge 0,\n\\]\n\nwith equality only when \\(n-s\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  \nIf \\(n-s=0\\) then \\(s=n\\) and (1) becomes \\(n\\le n\\), but this would mean that **all** 