011sunny line can cover points of \\(L_{c}\\) only if it is exactly \\(L_{c}\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \n   Consequently, for each \\(c\\) we need at least \\(\\max\\{1,(c-1)-s\\}\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Summing over all \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) gives a lower bound for the total number of lines required:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\text{required lines}\n   &\\ge \\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\max\\{1,(c-1)-s\\}\\\\\n   &=\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=2}^{s+1}1}_{s\\text{ terms}}\n     +\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=s+2}^{n+1}(c-1-s)}_{n-s\\text{ terms}}\\\\[2mm]\n   &= s+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\,n-s}j\n    = s+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines, (1) must satisfy\n   \\[\n   s+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}\\le n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Analysis of inequality (2).**  \n   Define \\(f(s)=s+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}\\) for \\(0\\le s\\le n\\).  \n   For \\(s\\ge 1\\),\n   \\[\n   f(s)-n\n   =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s-1)}{2}\\ge 0,\n   \\]\n   with equality only when \\(n-s\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n   *Case \\(n-s=0\\) (\\(s=n\\)).*  \n   All lines would be sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most one point to each \\(L_{c}\\); thus on \\(L_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points of \\(T\\)) we could cover at most \\(n\\) points, but we would need \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each providing a different point. This forces every sunny line to pass through a distinct point of \\(L_{n+1}\\) and none of them to pass through any other point of \\(T\\). However, each sunny line would then be parallel to none of the forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of a sunny line (it would be parallel to none of the three given directions, which is allowed) \u2013 the real contradiction is that we would have no non\u2011sunny line to cover the points on the other \\(L_{c}\\) (for \\(c\\le n\\)), each of which contains at least two points, while a sunny line can supply at most one point per such line. Hence the covering