y=0\\). Hence it is not parallel to any \\(L_{c}\\) and therefore intersects each \\(L_{c}\\) in exactly one point of the plane. Since two distinct lines intersect in at most one point, at most one point of \\(L_{c}\\cap T\\) can belong to a given sunny line. \u220e\n\nThus the \\(s\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(s\\) points of \\(T\\) on each line \\(L_{c}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2  \nEvery non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) coincides with some \\(L_{c}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to at least one of the three forbidden directions.  \nIf it were parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal), all its points would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, say \\(y=b\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-b\\); consequently the horizontal line contains at most one point of \\(T\\). The same holds for a vertical line.  \nSince each \\(L_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) contains at least two points of \\(T\\), a horizontal or vertical line cannot replace any \\(L_{c}\\) in a covering. Hence a non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); the only lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) that pass through points of \\(T\\) are precisely the lines \\(L_{c}\\) themselves. \u220e\n\nTherefore the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines are distinct members of the family \\(\\{L_{c}\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Counting\n\nFix \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nThe set \\(L_{c}\\cap T\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points.  \nAt most \\(s\\) of them can be covered by sunny lines (Lemma\u202f1).  \nThe remaining \\((c-1)-s\\) points must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines, but a non\u2011sunny line can cover points of \\(L_{c}\\) only if it is exactly \\(L_{c}\\) (Lemma\u202f2). Hence for each \\(c\\) we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{1,(c-1)-s\\}\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines (the \u201c1\u2019\u2019 accounts for the case \\((c-1)-s\\le 0\\), i.e. when sunny lines already cover all points of that \\(L_{c}\\)).\n\nSumming over all \\(c\\) yields a lower bound for the total number of lines required:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{required lines}\n&\\ge \\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\max\\{1,(c-1)-s\\}\\\\\n&= \\underbrace{\\sum_{c=2}^{s+1}1}_{s\\text{ terms}}\n   +\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=s+2}^{n+1}(c-1-s)}_{n-s\\text{ terms}}\\\\[2mm]\n&= s+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\,n-s}j\n = s+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines, (3) must satisfy\n\n\\[\ns+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Impossibility of \\(s\\ge 1\\)\n\nFor \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nf(s)=s+\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA direct computation gives  \n\n\\[\nf(s)-n=\\frac{(n-s)(n-s-1)}{2}\\ge 0,\n\\]\nwith equality only when \\(n-s\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(n-s=0\\) (\\(s=n\\)).*  \nAll lines are sunny. On the line \\(L_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) there are at least two points of \\(T\\), but a sunny line can contribute at most one point to that line (Lemma\u202f1). With only sunny lines available we cannot cover the required two (or more) points, a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(n-s=1\\) (\\(s=n-1\\)).*  \nExactly one non\u2011sunny line exists, say \\(L_{c_{0}}\\).  \nConsider \\(L_{n+1}\\); it contains \\(n\\) points of \\(T\\). Sunny lines can supply at most one point each, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points, leaving at least one point uncovered. Hence the covering fails.\n\nThus no integer \\(s\\ge 1\\) can satisfy (4) together with the geometric constraints, contradicting the assumption that a sunny line exists.\n\n---\n\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe assumption that a sunny line appears leads to a contradiction; therefore every admissible configuration must contain **no** sunny lines. Consequently the only possible value of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
