nts left uncovered is at most \\(\\dfrac{m(m+1)}2\\le 2s\\) because of the inequality derived in step 1 (recall \\(s=n-t\\)).  \n\n   *Step C – covering the remaining points with sunny lines.*  \n   The uncovered set \\(U\\) contains at most \\(2s\\) points, and no two of them share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, nor the same sum \\(x+y\\) (otherwise they would have been hit by a non‑sunny line already chosen). Consequently any two distinct points of \\(U\\) determine a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. the line is sunny.  \n\n   Pair the points of \\(U\\) arbitrarily (if \\(|U|\\) is odd, leave one point unpaired).  \n   - For each pair we draw the unique line through the two points; this line is sunny and covers exactly those two points.  \n   - If a single point remains, we draw any sunny line through it (for instance the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through the point).  \n\n   Because we have at most \\(s\\) pairs (indeed \\(|U|\\le 2s\\)), we obtain at most \\(s\\) sunny lines, exactly the required number. All these sunny lines are distinct: two different pairs give different lines because the four points are in general position (no three of them are collinear, otherwise a third would share a forbidden slope).  \n\n   Thus we have produced a configuration consisting of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(s\\) sunny lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can indeed be realised. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\; \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\, n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines’’ is precisely that the family of lines covers the set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) then the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them. The same bound holds for \\(y=c\\) and for \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\). The slope of the line is the same for any pair of these points. If the three points are not collinear with a forbidden direction, the slope would have to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. Hence at most two points can lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nCounting incidences (point–line pairs) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le tn+2s .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), substituting \\(t=n-s\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n(n-s)+2s=n^{2}-ns+2s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nns-2s\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\ns\\,(n-2)\\le\\frac{n(n-2)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\), \\(n-2>0\\) and we may divide:\n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) and put  \n\n\\[\ns=k,\\qquad t=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(s\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Choosing the non‑sunny lines  \n\nConsider the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1-c\\mid 0\\le c\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal V\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\)) is vertical (resp. horizontal, slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nSelect the first \\(t\\) lines from the concatenated list  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1,\\;x=2,\\;y=2,\\;x+y=n,\\;x=3,\\;y=3,\\;x+y=n-1,\\dots\n\\]\n\nuntil exactly \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines have been taken.  \nDenote this set by \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\).  \nBecause each chosen line belongs to one of the three families, Lemma 1 guarantees that every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU = T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}}L\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points not yet covered.  \nA point of \\(U\\) cannot share its \\(x\\)-coordinate, its \\(y\\)-coordinate, nor its sum \\(x+y\\) with any other point of \\(U\\); otherwise the two points would lie on a vertical, horizontal or diagonal line that belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\). Consequently any two distinct points of \\(U\\) determine a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Bounding the size of \\(U\\)\n\nEach of the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences, so the total number of incidences supplied by \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) is at most \\(tn\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, hence\n\n\\[\n|U| = |T_n| - \\text{(incidences supplied by }\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}) \n      \\le \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - tn .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(t=n-s\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|U|\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n(n-s)=ns-\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(s\\ge n/2\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n|U|\\le 2s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering \\(U\\) with sunny lines  \n\nIf \\(|U|=0\\) we are done (no sunny lines are needed, but this case cannot occur because \\(s\\ge1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \nOtherwise pair the points of \\(U\\) arbitrarily; if \\(|U|\\) is odd, leave one point unpaired.\n\n* For each pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset U\\) draw the unique line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) through them.  \n  By the observation above \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is sunny.  \n* If a single point \\(P\\) remains, draw any sunny line through \\(P\\) (for instance the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through \\(P\\)).\n\nBecause each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(U\\), the number of sunny lines used is at most \\(\\lceil |U|/2\\rceil\\).  \nFrom (4) we have \\(|U|\\le 2s\\), therefore we need at most \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \nWe may discard any unused sunny lines, leaving exactly \\(s\\) distinct sunny lines (if fewer than \\(s\\) were required we simply add arbitrary sunny lines that do not pass through any point of \\(T_n\\); this does not affect the covering property).\n\nThus we have produced a family consisting of the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) together with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines that together cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\n- Inequality (3) shows that no configuration can have fewer than \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \n- The construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\; \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\, n\\; } .\n\\]"
