n\n   Thus the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all points and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the construction yields the answer \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines among a collection of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a non\u2011sunny line is either\n\n* horizontal \\((y=c)\\),\n* vertical \\((x=c)\\), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (which contains **no** sunny line) already satisfies the covering condition.\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines can be replaced by a configuration with at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1 Choice of the lines\n\n1. Begin with the horizontal lines \\((1)\\).  \n2. Choose an arbitrary subset  \n\n   \\[\n   I\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |I|=k .\n   \\]\n\n3. For each \\(i\\in I\\) **remove** the horizontal line \\(L_i\\) and **insert** a sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-i = \\alpha_i\\,(x-1) \\qquad\\text{with }\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((1,i)\\in T_n\\).  \n   We may take, for instance,\n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   1, & i\\neq 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   2, & i=1,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   which guarantees \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\nThe resulting family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\cup\\{L_j\\mid j\\notin I\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n\\) lines; the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny by construction, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\nAll lines are distinct: the horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\); each \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0\\), and two different \\(S_i,S_{i'}\\) cannot coincide because they pass through different points \\((1,i)\\neq(1,i')\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2 Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\notin I\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(L_b\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n*If \\(b\\in I\\).*  \nThe point \\((1,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\) by definition.  \nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\in I\\) and \\(|I|=k\\), the largest possible value of \\(b\\) is \\(n\\); consequently \\(n+1-b\\le n-k+1\\).  \nFrom (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n-k+1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nc = b+1-a .\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(c\\ge b+1-(n-k+1)=b-n+k\\ge 1\\), and from \\(a\\ge2\\) we get \\(c\\le b-1\\).  \nThus \\(c\\) is a positive integer satisfying \\(c\\le n-k\\).  \n\nNow consider the horizontal line \\(L_c\\) (which is present because \\(c\\notin I\\)).  \nThe point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b = c + a -1\\), i.e. \\(a = b+1-c\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_c\\) (since \\(L_c\\) is the set of points with \\(y=c\\)).  \nTherefore every point with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\in I\\) is covered by a remaining horizontal line.\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3 Conclusion of the construction\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points required by the problem.  \nSince the choice of the subset \\(I\\) was arbitrary, such a configuration exists for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction of \u00a72 shows that this condition is also sufficient.  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
