            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\ny=0,\\qquad x=0,\\qquad x+y=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  All other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nThe set \\(\\{y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) horizontal (hence non‑sunny) lines and clearly covers every point of \\(T_n\\) because for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  Therefore a configuration of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A useful family of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_r:\\; y = x + (r-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_r\\) is \\(1\\); consequently \\(\\ell_r\\) is sunny.\n\n**Lemma.**  \nFor any \\(r\\) the intersection \\(\\ell_r\\cap T_n\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\bigr\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_r\\) iff \\(y=x+r-1\\).  Substituting this into the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx+(x+r-1)\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2x\\le n+2-r\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;x\\le n+1-r .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x\\ge1\\), the admissible integer values of \\(x\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-r\\).  \nFor each such \\(x\\) we obtain the point \\((x,\\,x+r-1)\\).  When \\(x=1\\) this point is \\((1,r)\\); when \\(x\\ge2\\) we have \\(y\\ge r+1\\).  Conversely, every point of the form \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) satisfies \\(y=x+r-1\\) for \\(r=b\\).  Hence (3) holds. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nI_k=\\{\\,n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\\qquad |I_k|=k. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=j\\mid j\\notin I_k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,\\ell_r\\mid r\\in I_k\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny lines of slope }1}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\); each \\(\\ell_r\\) has slope \\(1\\) and a different intercept \\(r-1\\), so no two lines coincide.  Hence \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_r\\) (\\(r\\in I_k\\)) have slope \\(1\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Coverage of all required points\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\notin I_k\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\in I_k\\).*  \nBy the Lemma, the sunny line \\(\\ell_b\\) contains every point of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\) and whose first coordinate satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (which holds because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)) is exactly \\(a\\le n+1-b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_b\\subset\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (1) gives \\(k\\le n\\).  The construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
