y lines\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nFor a fixed row \\(b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) a sunny line meets at most one point of the set  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  Intersecting with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) gives the unique abscissa \\(a=(b-c)/m\\).  Hence at most one point of the row can lie on the line. \u220e\n\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) rows, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(\\text{points of }T_n\\text{ on a sunny line}\\bigr)\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(kn\\) points by (1).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; they are horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) behaves exactly like a sunny line with respect to Lemma\u202f1, so to maximise the total number of covered points we may replace every slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line by a vertical line (which never covers fewer points).  Hence we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are **all vertical**.\n\nA vertical line \\(x=j\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\},\n\\]\n\nwhose cardinality is \\(n+1-j\\).  Choosing the leftmost columns \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) gives the largest possible total, namely\n\n\\[\nV_{n,k}= \\sum_{j=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-j)\n       =\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny lines we obtain the inequality\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n   \\le V_{n,k}+kn\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), (3) forces\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\le 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We build \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1.  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-k-1\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\ny=1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines in total.\n\nThe points covered by (5) are all points of the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1\\).  \nThe horizontal line (6) covers the remaining points of the first row, i.e. the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k\\).\n\n#### 3.2.  Sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=(t+2)x-\\frac{(t+1)(t+2)}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(t+2\\), which is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(\\ell_t\\)\u2019s are distinct because their slopes differ.\n\nLet us examine the points of \\(T_n\\) that are **not** already covered by the lines (5)\u2013(6).  \nSuch a point must satisfy\n\n\\[\na\\ge n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(t\\) and a row index \\(b\\ge 2\\) the point  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(b-(t+1),\\,b\\bigr)\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(\\ell_t\\) (substituting \\(x=b-(t+1)\\) into (7) gives \\(y=b\\)).  \nCondition (8) together with the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nb-(t+1)\\ge n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\le n+1-(b-(t+1)),\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nt+1\\le b\\le n-k+t+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus, for each \\(t\\) the line \\(\\ell_t\\) contains exactly the points \\((b-(t+1),b)\\) with \\(b\\) ranging over the interval (10).  These