nfigurations for \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exist three distinct lines covering these points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\) (all non‑sunny).\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;L\\) where \\(L\\) is any sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) (e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\)).\n* \\(k=2\\): \\(x=1,\\;L_1,\\;L_2\\) where \\(L_1\\) (sunny) passes through \\((1,2)\\) and \\(L_2\\) (sunny) passes through \\((2,1)\\) and is not parallel to \\(L_1\\).\n* \\(k=3\\): three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three points (for instance \\(y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2,\\;y=5x-9\\)).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n> 1. each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nWe prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).  \nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\). We treat two cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1: \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nStart from a configuration for \\(n\\) given by (**H\\(_n\\)**) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe new set of points is  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=T_n\\cup\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is **non‑sunny** (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAdd this line to the previous family; we now have \\(n+1\\) distinct lines (the new line is not parallel to any of the old