ds for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) the statement  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(n,k)\\quad\\text{holds for every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{IH}\n\\]\n\nFix a particular \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n(k)=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines witnessing \\(\\mathcal P(n,k)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  The new points when passing from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\) differs from \\(T_n\\) by exactly two points:\n\n\\[\nP_1=(n+1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n+1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) must have either \\(a=n+1,\\;b=1\\) or \\(a=1,\\;b=n+1\\) because \\(a,b\\ge1\\).\n\nConsequently, to extend a covering of \\(T_n\\) to a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) we only have to ensure that \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) lie on the new family of lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Construction of a family for \\(n+1\\)\n\nWe shall produce two families, one with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and one with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 5.1.  A non\u2011sunny line covering \\(P_1\\)\n\nTake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIt passes through \\(P_1\\) and is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n\n#### 5.2.  A sunny line covering \\(P_2\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\text{slope of each sunny line in }\\mathcal F_n(k)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is a finite set of real numbers, none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=\\alpha x + (n+1-\\alpha).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); because \\(\\alpha\\neq0\\) it is not horizontal; and because \\(\\alpha\\) is finite it is not vertical. Hence \\(\\ell_s\\) is sunny. Moreover\n\n\\[\n\\ell_s\\text{ passes through }P_2=(1,n+1):\n\\quad n+1=\\alpha\\cdot1+(n+1-\\alpha).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_s\\) covers the second new point.\n\n#### 5.3.  Deleting one old line\n\nConsider the original family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\).  \nIf it already contains a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) that is **not** needed to cover any point of \\(T_n\\) uniquely (i.e. every point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\)), we simply discard \\(\\ell_{ns}\\).  \n\nIf every non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) is essential, then at least one sunny line \\(\\ell_{sun}\\) is essential (otherwise all lines would be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the existence of a sunny line when \\(k>0\\)). In this case we discard an essential sunny line \\(\\ell_{sun}\\) **and** keep the newly added sunny line \\(\\ell_s\\).  \n\nIn either situation we remove exactly one line from \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) and keep the two new lines \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\).  \nThe resulting family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{\\text{deleted line}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines because:\n\n* \\(\\ell_h\\) is horizontal, a direction not used by any line of \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) (all horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) are non\u2011sunny; we either deleted such a line or, if none existed, \\(\\ell_h\\) is new);\n* \\(\\ell_s\\) has slope \\(\\alpha\\) which, by (3), differs from the slope of every sunny line already present, and it is not horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is distinct from all remaining lines.\n\n#### 5.4.  Counting sunny lines\n\n*If the deleted line was non\u2011sunny* (possible when \\(k\\le n-1\\)), the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}\\) is still \\(k\\).  \n*If the deleted line was sunny* (this occurs when we need to increase the number of sunny lines), the number becomes \\(k+1\\) because we removed one sunny line and added the sunny line \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nThus from a configuration for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain:\n\n* a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**, and  \n* a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with **exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines**.\n\nBoth families satisfy the covering requirement for \\(T_{n+1}\\) because every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered (the deleted line was redundant) and the two new points are covered by \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case supplies configurations for \\(n=3\\) and all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal P(m,k)\\) holds for all \\(k\\le m\\) for some \\(m\\ge3\\).  \nApplying the construction of Section\u202f5 with \\(n=m\\) yields configurations for \\(m+1\\) with every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal P(m+1,k)\\) holds for all admissible \\(k\\).\n\nBy mathematical induction, \\(\\mathcal P(n,k)\\) is true for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Answer\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof."
