ered, and a line of one of the three allowed directions always passes through at least one of them, so the process can continue until all points are covered.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, and no other \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the legs). Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound on the number of lines\n\nA line in the plane can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nIndeed, if a line has slope \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) then the equation is\n\\(y=mx+b\\).  Since the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) have integer coordinates,\ndifferent values of \\(a\\) give different values of \\(b\\); the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n\\).  Hence at most \\(n\\) distinct values of\n\\(a\\) (and therefore at most \\(n\\) points) can lie on the same line.\nThe same bound holds for the three special directions\n\\(y=c,\\;x=c,\\;x+y=c\\).\n\nConsequently any family of lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\)\nmust contain at least  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{|\\mathcal{P}|}{n}\\right\\rceil\n   =\\left\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)/2}{n}\\right\\rceil=n\n\\]\n\nlines.  Since the problem asks for exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, a\nfeasible configuration uses **exactly** \\(n\\) lines, and therefore the\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying (2).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n\\(k\\) of which