ains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line be given by \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite.  \nIf it contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\), then the slopes between successive points are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the differences \\(a_{i+1}-a_i\\) and \\(b_{i+1}-b_i\\) are positive integers, the common value \\(m\\) must be a rational number with numerator and denominator both equal to \\(1\\) or both equal to \\(-1\\). The only possibilities are \\(m=0\\) (horizontal) or \\(m=-1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), both of which are excluded for a sunny line. Hence a sunny line cannot pass through three distinct points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are three families of non‑sunny lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=b_0\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,b_0)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); their number is at most \\(n\\).\n* Vertical lines \\(x=a_0\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a_0,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_0\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n* Lines of slope \\(-1\\) have equation \\(x+y=c\\). Inside the triangle we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); for each admissible \\(c\\) the line meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(c-1\\) points, which is at most \\(n\\).\n\nThus any non‑sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument (Proof by Contradiction)\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct covering lines there are **at most one** non‑sunny line.  \nLet this possible non‑sunny line be \\(L\\); if no such line exists, set \\(|L\\cap T_n|=0\\).\n\nBy Lemma 2, \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(T_n\\) must lie on sunny lines.  \n