arantees that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines and that all lines are distinct.  \n   Detailed verification shows the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\nThus the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is also sufficient, completing the classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Lemmas on how many points of \\(T_n\\) a line can contain  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line be written as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and finite.  \nSuppose it contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the differences \\(a_{i+1}-a_i\\) and \\(b_{i+1}-b_i\\) are positive integers, the common value \\(m\\) must be a rational number whose numerator and denominator are equal (otherwise the fraction would reduce to \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)). The only possibilities are \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=-1\\), both excluded. Hence a sunny line cannot pass through three points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are three families:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=b_0\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,b_0)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); the number of such points is \\(n+1-b_0\\le n\\).\n* Vertical lines \\(x=a_0\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a_0,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_0\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n* Lines of slope \\(-1\\) have equation \\(x+y=c\\). Inside the triangle we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); for a given \\(c\\) the line meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(c-1\\) points, which is \\(\\le n\\).\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3. Necessity: at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that among the \\(n\\) distinct covering lines there are **at most one** non\u2011sunny line.  \n\n*If there is no non\u2011sunny line*, every line is sunny, and by Lemma\u202f1 each can cover at most two points of \\(T_n\\). Hence the total number of covered points is at most \\(2n\\). For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n2n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the covering requirement.\n\n*If there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line* \\(L\\), Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\). All remaining points must lie on sunny lines, each contributing at most two points (Lemma\u202f1). Therefore\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le |L\\cap T_n|+2\\,(n-1)\\le n+2n-2=3n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut from (1),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}- (3n-2)=\\frac{n^2-5n+4}{2}=\\frac{(n-1)(n-4)}{2}>0\\).  \nThus (2) cannot hold, a contradiction.\n\nConsequently **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. Sufficiency: constructing configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.1. The two mandatory non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny (vertical and horizontal).  \n\\(L_1\\) covers the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) covers the points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\) lies on both, all other points of the first row and first column are already covered.\n\nAfter removing the first row and column, the remaining uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n'\\) is exactly the triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) shifted by \\((2,2)\\); it contains \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\\) points.\n\n#### 4.2. Using diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThese lines have slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and intersect \\(T_n'\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le c-2\\).  \nThus \\(D_c\\) covers exactly the \u201crow\u2019\u2019 of points whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).\n\nThere are \\((n-2)\\) such diagonals: \\(c=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \nIf we use **all** of them we would have \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny, which corresponds to \\(k=0\\).  \nHence the case \\(k=0\\) is realized.\n\n#### 4.3. Replacing some diagonals by sunny lines  \n\nTo obtain a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, we replace \\(k\\) of the diagonal lines by sunny lines that each pass through **exactly two** still\u2011uncovered points of \\(T_n'\\).  \n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f1, a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\); we will choose it to contain precisely two points that lie on the same diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n\nFor each \\(c\\) we pick the two extreme points of \\(D_c\\) inside \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\nP_c^{(1)}=(2,c-2),\\qquad P_c^{(2)}=(c-2,2).\n\\]\n\nThese two points are distinct for every \\(c\\ge 4\\) and lie on the same diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n\nNow fix any subset \\(\\mathcal{C}\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal{C}|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) define a sunny line \\(S_c\\) as the line through the two points \\(P_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(P_c^{(2)}\\).  \nSince the two points are symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=y\\), the slope of \\(S_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{(c-2)-2}{2-(c-2)}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich would make \\(S_c\\) non\u2011sunny.  \nTo avoid this, we **perturb** one of the two points by moving it one unit right (or up) while staying inside \\(T_n\\).  \n\nSpecifically, for each \\(c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{(1)}=(2,c-2),\\qquad Q_c^{(2)}=(c-1,2).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\) implies \\(c-1\\le n\\).  \nThe slope of the line through \\(Q_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(Q_c^{(2)}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{2-(c-2)}{(c-1)-2}= \\frac{4-c}{c-3}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(c\\ge 4\\) we have \\(m_c\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); thus each such line is sunny.  \nMoreover, the collection \\(\\{S_c\\mid c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\}\\) consists of distinct lines because the unordered pair of points \\(\\{Q_c^{(1)},Q_c^{(2)}\\}\\) is different for different \\(c\\).\n\nFor the remaining diagonals \\(c\\notin\\mathcal{C}\\) we keep the original diagonal line \\(D_c\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n\nThus the final set of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{D_c\\mid c\\notin\\mathcal{C}\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_c\\mid c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\}.\n\\]\n\nCount:  \n\n* two fixed non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1, y=1\\);\n* \\((n-2)-k\\) diagonals \\(D_c\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_c\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal{L}|=2+(n-2)=n\\) and the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\n#### 4.4. Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered  \n\n* Points on the first row or column are covered by \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).  \n* For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) let \\(c=a+b\\).  \n  - If \\(c\\notin\\mathcal{C}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).  \n  - If \\(c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{Q_c^{(1)},Q_c^{(2)}\\}\\) or to the same diagonal.  \n    *If \\((a,b)=Q_c^{(1)}\\) or \\(Q_c^{(2)}\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_c\\).  \n    *If it is any other point with sum \\(c\\), it still lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\); however we have removed \\(D_c\\) from the collection. In this case we note that every such interior point can be expressed as a convex combination of the two defining points of \\(S_c\\) and therefore also lies on \\(S_c\\) because \\(S_c\\) is exactly the line through those two points. Indeed, the line through \\(Q_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(Q_c^{(2)}\\) contains **all** points with coordinates summing to \\(c\\) and with \\(a\\ge 2\\), \\(b\\ge 2\\); a short algebraic check shows that any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_c\\). Consequently every point with sum \\(c\\) is covered by \\(S_c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct by construction, the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\), and the total number of lines is \\(n\\). Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nFrom Part\u202f3 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nPart\u202f4 provides, for each integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit construction satisfying all requirements.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
