at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The three families (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) are correctly described.  \n- For a horizontal line \\(y=b_0\\) the admissible \\(a\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); the maximum occurs at \\(b_0=1\\) giving \\(n\\) points.  \n- Similar reasoning for vertical lines.  \n- For slope \\(-1\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\) the number of integer points is \\(c-1\\), and \\(c\\le n+1\\) gives at most \\(n\\) points.  \n- **Correct** (though the lemma is not needed later because the necessity argument already fails).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Necessity: at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny  \n\nThe argument proceeds as follows:\n\n1. Assume at most one non\u2011sunny line.  \n2. *Case 0 non\u2011sunny lines*: uses Lemma\u202f1 to claim each sunny line covers \u2264\u202f2 points, giving total \u2264\u202f2n points, which is less than \\(|T_n|\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n3. *Case 1 non\u2011sunny line*: uses Lemma\u202f2 for the single non\u2011sunny line (\u2264\u202fn points) and Lemma\u202f1 for the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\u2264\u202f2 points each), obtaining \\(|T_n|\\le 3n-2\\).  \n4. Shows \\(3n-2<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), contradiction.  \n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Because Lemma\u202f1 is false, the bound \u201ceach sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\)\u201d is invalid.  \n- Consequently the inequalities \\(2n\\) and \\(n+2n-2\\) are not guaranteed.  \n- The derived necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) (i.e., at least two non\u2011sunny lines) is **not proven**.  \n- The whole necessity section collapses.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (propagation of the false Lemma\u202f1).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Sufficiency: constructing configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nEven if the necessity part were correct, the construction must be examined.\n\n#### 4.1 Two mandatory non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) indeed cover the first row and column. No issue.\n\n