 there are \\(c-1\\) of them.\nBecause \\((a,b)\\) and \\((b,a)\\) are symmetric, at most half of them can have\n\\(a\\le b\\); the other half have \\(b<a\\). Hence the number of distinct points is\n\\(\\lfloor (c-1)/2\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequences of the three lemmas:\n\n* a horizontal or vertical line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\);\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n* \\(h\\) = number of horizontal lines,\n* \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines,\n* \\(d\\) = number of lines of slope \\(-1\\),\n* \\(k=n-h-v-d\\) = number of sunny lines.\n\nUsing Lemmas\u202f2.1\u20132.3, the total number of points that can be covered by the\nnon\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}\n&\\le h\\cdot n + v\\cdot n + d\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; therefore it can intersect the triangular region \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most two lattice points (a line can meet the boundary of the triangle at most twice). Hence each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) new points, i.e.\n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{sunny}}\\le 2k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered, so by (1), (2) and (3)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le h n + v n + d\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor + 2k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(h+v+d=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le (n-k)n + 2k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\nk \\ge \\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge k_{\\min}:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction attaining the lower bound  \n\nWe now show that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a configuration with exactly\n\\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines, proving that the bound (4) is sharp.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the following \\(2m\\) non\u2011sunny lines (some may be omitted when \\(n\\) is odd):\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=m\\)  \u2003(\\(m\\) lines);\n* Vertical   lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\)  \u2003(\\(m\\) lines).\n\nThese \\(2m\\) lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except those lying on the\noutermost \u201cborder\u201d defined by \\(x+y=n+1\\) and the two corners \\((1,1)\\) and\n\\((n,1)\\) (or \\((1,n)\\) when \\(n\\) is odd).  \n\n**Step\u202f2 \u2013 the diagonal line.**  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt is of slope \\(-1\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and passes through every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that was not yet covered, except possibly the point \\((1,1)\\) when\n\\(n\\) is odd. Consequently after Step\u202f2 all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered\nexcept at most one point.\n\n**Step\u202f3 \u2013 sunny lines.**  \nIf a point remains uncovered (this happens only when \\(n\\) is odd), we cover it\nwith a sunny line of arbitrary slope different from \\(0,\\pm1\\); this line is\nnecessarily distinct from all previously used lines.  \n\nNow count the total number of lines used.\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2m\\).  \n  We have \\(2m\\) horizontal/vertical lines plus the diagonal line, i.e. \\(n+1\\)\n  lines. To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we discard **one** of the horizontal (or\n  vertical) lines and replace it by a sunny line that passes through any point\n  already covered by that discarded line (possible because each such line\n  contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)).  \n  The resulting configuration uses \\(n\\) lines, of which the only sunny line is\n  the one we just introduced. Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  k = 1 = \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n  \\]\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n  After Steps\u202f1\u20132 we have \\(2m+1=n\\) lines already, and all points are covered\n  (the extra point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)).  \n  Among these \\(n\\) lines exactly one is sunny: we replace one of the horizontal\n  (or vertical) lines by a sunny line through two of its former points, as in\n  the even case. Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  k = 1 = \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n  \\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructed a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontaining precisely \\(k_{\\min}= \\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines and covering\nall points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Obtaining any larger number of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the configuration of Section\u202f4 (which has exactly \\(k_{\\min}\\)\nsunny lines), we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one:\n\n*Pick a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \nBecause \\(L\\) contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), we can split those\npoints into two disjoint non\u2011empty subsets and draw two distinct sunny lines,\neach passing through one of the subsets.  \nRemove \\(L\\) and add the two sunny lines; the total number of lines stays\n\\(n\\) while the number of sunny lines grows by one.*\n\nRepeating this operation we can raise the sunny count from \\(k_{\\min}\\) up to the\ntrivial extreme \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny). Hence **every integer**\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{k_{\\min},k_{\\min}+1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the necessary condition (4) with the constructive proof of Sections\u202f4\nand\u202f5, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\text{For a given }n\\ge3,\\;\nk\\text{ is admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;(0\\le k\\le n).}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\{\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]"
