ine contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(H,V,D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(H\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so the line through them is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), which is not sunny. The same argument works for \\(V\\) (vertical line, slope \\(\\infty\\)) and for \\(D\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). ∎\n\nConsequently, if a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covered all points of \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) **and** all its members were sunny, then each line would have to contain exactly one point of each of the three sets, because otherwise the total number of covered points would be \\(<3n\\).\n\nAssume for a contradiction that such a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.  \nPick a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nL\\cap H=\\{(a,1)\\},\\qquad L\\cap V=\\{(1,b)\\},\\qquad L\\cap D=\\{(c,n+1-c)\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(1\\le a,b,c\\le n\\).  \nSince the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{1-a}= \\frac{n+1-c-1}{c-a}= \\frac{b-1}{1-a}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the equality of the first and the second fractions we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(b-1)(c-a) = (n-c)(1-a). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a-1)(b-1) = (c-1)(n-c). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side of (2) depends only on the chosen point of \\(H\\) and the chosen point of \\(V\\); the right‑hand side depends only on the chosen point of \\(D\\).  \n\nFor fixed \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) the value \\((a-1)(b-1)\\) can take at most \\(n-1\\) distinct possibilities (because \\(a-1\\) and \\(b-1\\) range from \\(0\\) to \\(n-1\\)).  \nSimilarly, as \\(c\\) runs through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the product \\((c-1)(n-c)\\) also attains at most \\(n-1\\) distinct values.  \n\nThus equation (2) can be satisfied for at most \\(n-1\\) different triples \\((a,b,c)\\).  \nBut we have \\(n\\) lines, each providing a distinct triple, a contradiction.\n\nHence a covering family of \\(n\\) lines cannot consist solely of sunny lines; at leas