hen \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     *If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);*  \n     *if \\(b=2\\) it lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) because \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\).*  \n   - If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\), and again the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nHence for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nFrom (3) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient. Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
