            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary  \n\nDefine three disjoint subsets of \\(T_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH &=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nV &=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach set contains exactly \\(n\\) points, and  \n\n\\[\nH\\cup V\\cup D\\subseteq T_{n+1},\\qquad |H\\cup V\\cup D|=3n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(H,V,D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(H\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so the line through them is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), which is not sunny. The same argument works for \\(V\\) (vertical line, slope \\(\\infty\\)) and for \\(D\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). ∎\n\nConsequently, if a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covered all points of \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) **and** all its members were sunny, then each line would have to contain exactly one point of each of the three sets, because otherwise the total number of covered points would be \\(<3n\\).\n\nAssume for a contradiction that such a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.  \nPick a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nL\\cap H=\\{(a,1)\\},\\qquad L\\cap V=\\{(1,b)\\},\\qquad L\\cap D=\\{(c,n+1-c)\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(1\\le a,b,c\\le n\\).  \nSince the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{1-a}= \\frac{n+1-c-1}{c-a}= \\frac{b-1}{1-a}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the equality of the first and the second fractions we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(b-1)(c-a) = (n-c)(1-a). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a-1)(b-1) = (c-1)(n-c). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side of (2) depends only on the chosen point of \\(H\\) and the chosen point of \\(V\\); the right‑hand side depends only on the chosen point of \\(D\\).  \n\nFor fixed \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) the value \\((a-1)(b-1)\\) can take at most \\(n-1\\) distinct possibilities (because \\(a-1\\) and \\(b-1\\) range from \\(0\\) to \\(n-1\\)).  \nSimilarly, as \\(c\\) runs through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the product \\((c-1)(n-c)\\) also attains at most \\(n-1\\) distinct values.  \n\nThus equation (2) can be satisfied for at most \\(n-1\\) different triples \\((a,b,c)\\).  \nBut we have \\(n\\) lines, each providing a distinct triple, a contradiction.\n\nHence a covering family of \\(n\\) lines cannot consist solely of sunny lines; at least one line must be non‑sunny.\n\nNow remove any one non‑sunny line, say a vertical line. The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines still have to cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(V\\). By Lemma 1 each of those points can be covered by at most one sunny line, so among the remaining lines at least one more must be non‑sunny (otherwise we could cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(V\\)).  \n\nTherefore **any** family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) contains **at least two** non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   They are pairwise distinct and not sunny.\n\n2. **The remaining two lines.**  \n   - If \\(k=0\\) we need both of the remaining lines to be non‑sunny.  \n     Choose  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{n-1}: y=1 ,\\qquad L_{n}: x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n   - If \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) we need exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n     Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of arbitrary slopes (different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)); for concreteness we may take  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{n-1-j}: y = \\alpha_j x + \\beta_j\\qquad (j=0,\\dots ,k-1),\n     \\]\n\n     where the real numbers \\(\\alpha_j,\\beta_j\\) are chosen so that the lines are distinct and none of the slopes \\(\\alpha_j\\) equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n     The two remaining lines are taken to be the non‑sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   In all cases we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Verification that all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   - If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     *If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);*  \n     *if \\(b=2\\) it lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) because \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\).*  \n   - If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\), and again the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nHence for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#