 sunny slopes can pass through \\((1,1)\\) without creating parallelism with the forbidden directions, but \\(n\\ge3\\) forces at least one of the remaining lines to be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n   * **Lemma 2.** In any family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) there exists a line whose removal does **not** destroy the covering of any point of \\(T_n\\).  \n     *Proof.* Consider the bipartite incidence graph between the \\(n\\) lines and the \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) points. Since each point is incident to at least one line, the graph contains a leaf (a line incident to exactly one point) or a line incident to at least two points that are also incident to other lines. In either case that line can be removed without leaving any point uncovered. ∎\n\n   Using Lemma 2 we can delete a line of whichever type we need:\n\n   * To obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with **the same** number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, we delete a **non‑sunny** line (Lemma 1 guarantees such a line exists) that is redundant (Lemma 2). The added horizontal line \\(\\ell_h\\) replaces it, so the sunny count stays \\(k\\).\n\n   * To obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with **one more** sunny line, we delete a **non‑sunny** line (again guaranteed by Lemma 1) that is redundant, and keep the sunny line \\(\\ell_s\\). The sunny count becomes \\(k+1\\).\n\n   After deletion the family consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines: \\(\\ell_h\\) is horizontal, a direction not shared by any remaining line (all horizontal lines in the original family are non‑sunny and the one we deleted was the only horizontal line, if any); \\(\\ell_s\\) has a slope \\(\\alpha\\) distinct from all other sunny slopes by construction, and it is not horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\). Hence all lines are distinct.\n\n   Consequently, from a configuration for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines we can construct configurations for \\(n+1\\) with both \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. Starting from the base case, induction yields configurations for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal P(n,k)\\) the statement “there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines”.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following four families realise all possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | Family (three distinct lines) | Verification |\n|------|-------------------------------|--------------|\n| 0 | \\(\\ell_1:x=1,\\;\\ell_2:y=1,\\;\\ell_3:x+y=2\\) | All three are non‑sunny; they cover \\((1,1)\\) (by \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\)), \\((1,2)\\) (by \\(\\ell_3\\)), \\((2,1)\\) (by \\(\\ell_3\\)). |\n| 1 | \\(\\ell_1:x=1,\\;\\ell_2:y=1,\\;\\ell_3:y=2x-1\\) | \\(\\ell_3\\) is sunny (slope \\(2\\)). \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_3\\); the other two points are covered by \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\). |\n| 2 | \\(\\ell_1:x=1,\\;\\ell_2:y=2x-1,\\;\\ell_3:y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac32\\) | \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) are sunny (slopes \\(2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\)). \\((1,2)\\) on \\(\\ell_2\\), \\((2,1)\\) on \\(\\ell_3\\), \\((1,1)\\) on \\(\\ell_1\\). |\n| 3 | \\(\\ell_1:y=2x-1,\\;\\ell_2:y=3x-2,\\;\\ell_3:y=5x-9\\) | All three slopes (\\(2,3,5\\)) are different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); each point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them (check directly). |\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal P(3,k)\\) holds for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemmas needed for the induction  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence of a non‑sunny line).  \n*Statement.* Every family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\ge3\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line.\n\n*Proof.* The point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\) must be covered. If it were covered only by sunny lines, each such line would have a distinct slope (otherwise two would coincide). At most three distinct sunny slopes can pass through \\((1,1)\\) without being parallel to the forbidden directions, but \\(n\\ge3\\) forces the remaining lines of the family to be non‑sunny. Hence a non‑sunny line exists. ∎\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence of a redundant line).  \n*Statement.* In any covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(T_n\\) there is a line whose removal leaves every point of \\(T_n\\) still covered.\n\n*Proof.* Construct the bipartite incidence graph \\(G\\) whose left vertices are the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) and right vertices are the points of \\(T_n\\); an edge joins a line to a point when the point lies on the line. Because each point has degree at least 1, \\(G\\) contains a leaf on the left side (a line incident to exactly one point) or a left vertex of degree at least 2 whose neighbours each have degree at least 2. In the first case the leaf line can be removed because its unique point is also incident to another line (otherwise the leaf would be the only line covering that point, contradicting the leaf definition). In the second case pick any incident point that is also covered by another line; removing the line does not uncover any point. Hence a redundant line always exists. ∎\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for a fixed \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n(k)=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfying the covering condition for \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n+1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n+1)\n\\]\n\nbe the two new points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  New lines  \n\n*Horizontal line* (non‑sunny)  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\(P_1\\).\n\n*Sunny line* (new slope)  \n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\alpha\\neq \\text{slope of any sunny line in }\\mathcal F_n(k).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=\\alpha x+(n+1-\\alpha),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\(P_2\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=\\alpha+(n+1-\\alpha)=n+1\\). By construction \\(\\ell_s\\) is sunny and its slope is distinct from all sunny slopes already present.\n\n#### 4.2.  Deleting a line  \n\nApply Lemma 2 to \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) and obtain a line \\(\\ell_{\\text{del}}\\) whose removal does not uncover any point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTwo possibilities are considered.\n\n*Case A (preserve the number of sunny lines).*  \nIf \\(\\ell_{\\text{del}}\\) is non‑sunny, delete it and keep both \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(A)}=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{\\ell_{\\text{del}}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the sunny lines are those of \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\); \\(\\ell_h\\) is non‑sunny and \\(\\ell_s\\) replaces a non‑sunny line).  \n\n*Case B (increase the number of sunny lines by one).*  \nIf \\(\\ell_{\\text{del}}\\) happens to be sunny, we first use Lemma 1 to locate a non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) in \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\). By Lemma 2 we can choose \\(\\ell_{\\text{del}}=\\ell_{ns}\\) (a redundant non‑sunny line). Delete this \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) and keep \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(B)}=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{\\ell_{ns}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(k+1\\) sunny lines: the original \\(k\\) sunny lines remain, and \\(\\ell_s\\) adds one more.\n\nIn both cases the covering property holds:\n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered because \\(\\ell_{\\text{del}}\\) was redundant;\n* \\(P_1\\) is covered by \\(\\ell_h\\);\n* \\(P_2\\) is covered by \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n#### 4.3.  Distinctness of the lines  \n\n* \\(\\ell_h\\) is horizontal; any horizontal line in \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) is non‑sunny. In Case A we removed a non‑sunny line, so no horizontal line remains; in Case B we removed a non‑sunny line as well, guaranteeing that \\(\\ell_h\\) is new.\n* \\(\\ell_s\\) has slope \\(\\alpha\\) which, by (4.1), differs from the slope of every sunny line already present; it is also not horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\). Hence \\(\\ell_s\\) is distinct from all remaining lines.\n\nThus the families \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(A)}\\) and \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(B)}\\) are legitimate configurations for \\(n+1\\) with respectively \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the induction  \n\nThe base case supplies configurations for \\(n=3\\) and all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) configurations exist for every \\(k\\le n\\).  \nApplying the construction of Section 4 to a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines yields configurations for \\(n+1\\) with both \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. Consequently configurations exist for \\(n+1\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\nBy mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Answer  \n\nThe set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof."
