e slopes (\\(2,3,5\\)) are sunny and distinct. Direct substitution shows that each of the six points satisfies at least one of the three equations. |\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal P(3,k)\\) holds for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A useful combinatorial fact  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any finite collection of lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nConsider the bipartite incidence graph \\(G\\) whose left vertices are the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and whose right vertices are the points of \\(T_n\\); an edge joins a line to a point exactly when the point lies on the line.\n\nBecause each point of \\(T_n\\) is incident to at least one line, every right vertex has degree \\(\\ge 1\\).  \nIf every left vertex had degree \\(\\ge 2\\), then the total number of edges would be at least \\(2|\\mathcal L|\\).  \nBut the total number of edges is also at most \\(|T_n|\\) (each point contributes at most one edge to a given line).  \nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}<2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), the inequality \\(2|\\mathcal L|\\le |T_n|\\) cannot hold when \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  \nConsequently **some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is incident to exactly one point of \\(T_n\\)**.  \n\nWe call such a line **redundant** because removing it leaves every point of \\(T_n\\) still covered (the unique point it covered is also covered by at least one other line, otherwise that point would have degree 1, contradicting the definition of redundancy).  \n\nHence:\n\n> **Fact 1.** In any covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines for \\(T_n\\) there exists a redundant line.\n\nFact 1 replaces the erroneous Lemma 2 and does not rely on any special property of sunny or non‑sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n(k)=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall construct families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  The two new points  \n\nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) and, in addition, the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_h=(n+1,1),\\qquad P_s=(1,n+1).\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2.  Two auxiliary lines  \n\n*Horizontal line (non‑sunny)*  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=1,\n\\]\nwhich passes through \\(P_h\\).\n\n*Sunny line (new slope)*  \n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\alpha\\neq \\text{slope of any sunny line already present in }\\mathcal F_n(k).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=\\alpha x+(n+1-\\alpha),\n\\]\nso that \\(\\ell_s\\) passes through \\(P_s\\) (substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=n+1\\)). By (4.1) the line \\(\\ell_s\\) is sunny and its slope is distinct from the slopes of all sunny lines already in the family.\n\n#### 4.3.  Deleting a redundant line  \n\nApply **Fact 1** to \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\); obtain a redundant line \\(\\ell_{\\text{red}}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_{\\text{red}}\\) is redundant, removing it leaves every point of \\(T_n\\) still covered.\n\nTwo possibilities are considered.\n\n*Case A (keep the number of sunny lines).*  \nIf \\(\\ell_{\\text{red}}\\) is **non‑sunny**, delete it and keep **both** \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(A)}=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{\\ell_{\\text{red}}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.  \nThe sunny lines are exactly the original \\(k\\) sunny lines (since \\(\\ell_h\\) is non‑sunny and \\(\\ell_s\\) replaces a non‑sunny line), so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\n*Case B (increase the number of sunny lines by one).*  \nIf the redundant line \\(\\ell_{\\text{red}}\\) happens to be sunny, we first locate **any** non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) in \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) (such a line always exists because the vertical line \\(x=1\\) belongs to every covering of \\(T_n\\); explicitly, the line \\(x=1\\) is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column).  \nReplace \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) by the two new lines \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}^{(B)}=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{\\ell_{ns}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow the family has \\(k+1\\) sunny lines: the original \\(k\\) sunny lines plus the newly added \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nIn both cases every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered:\n\n* points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because the removed line was redundant;\n* \\(P_h\\) lies on \\(\\ell_h\\);\n* \\(P_s\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n#### 4.4.  Distinctness of the lines  \n\n* \\(\\ell_h\\) is horizontal; any horizontal line already present in \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) would be non‑sunny. In Case A we removed a non‑sunny line, so no horizontal line remains; in Case B we removed a non‑sunny line as well, guaranteeing that \\(\\ell_h\\) is new.\n* \\(\\ell_s\\) has slope \\(\\alpha\\) which, by (4.1), differs from the slope of every sunny line already present; it is also not horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\). Hence \\(\\ell_s\\) is distinct from all remaining lines.\n\nThus the constructed families are valid.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the induction  \n\nThe base families for \\(n=3\\) provide, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), a configuration \\(\\mathcal F_3(k)\\).  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) configurations exist for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nApplying the construction of Section 4 to a configuration with a given \\(k\\) yields:\n\n* a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines (Case A);\n* a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (Case B).\n\nConsequently, for \\(n+1\\) we obtain configurations for all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).  \nBy induction, the statement holds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Answer  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof."
