            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires that every point of \\(T_n\\) lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following three‑line families cover \\(T_3\\) and contain exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n  All three lines are non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\)).  \n  Direct substitution shows that each point of \\(T_3\\) satisfies at least one of the three equations.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:y=2x-1 .\n  \\]\n  \\(\\ell_3\\) has slope \\(2\\) (sunny). The other two lines are as above; a quick check verifies that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of them.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=2x-1,\\qquad \\ell_3:y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac32 .\n  \\]\n  \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\) have slopes \\(2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) respectively, both sunny; \\(\\ell_1\\) is vertical. Substituting the six points confirms coverage.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:y=2x-1,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=3x-2,\\qquad \\ell_3:y=5x-9 .\n  \\]\n  The three slopes \\(2,3,5\\) are all sunny and distinct. A straightforward verification shows that each of the six points satisfies at least one of the three equations.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) we have families \\(\\mathcal F_3(k)\\) for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Two indispensable non‑sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\quad\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ be a collection of }n\\text{ lines covering }T_n.\n\\text{ Then }\\mathcal L\\text{ contains the vertical line }x=1\\text{ and the horizontal line }y=1.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) all belong to \\(T_n\\) and are collinear only on the line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf a covering used fewer than \\(n\\) distinct lines to contain these \\(n\\) points, at least one of those lines would have to contain two of them, which forces that line to be vertical, i.e. \\(x=1\\). Hence \\(x=1\\) must be present.  \nThe same argument applied to the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) yields the presence of the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). ∎\n\nConsequently every admissible family contains at least one non‑sunny line of each prohibited direction.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n(k)=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(T_n\\) together with the two new points  \n\n\\[\nP_h=(n+1,1),\\qquad P_s=(1,n+1).\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1.  New lines\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal F_n(k).\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1\\qquad\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;y=\\alpha x+(n+1-\\alpha)\\qquad\\text{(sunny)}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_h\\) passes through \\(P_h\\); \\(\\ell_s\\) passes through \\(P_s\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=n+1\\).\n\n#### 4.2.  Deleting a non‑sunny line\n\nBy Lemma 1 the family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) contains both the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nWe consider two possibilities.\n\n* **(i) Keeping the number of sunny lines.**  \n  Delete the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is non‑sunny) and add the two new lines:\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{n+1}^{\\,(k)}=\n  \\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{y=1\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}.\n  \\]\n  The sunny lines are exactly the original \\(k\\) sunny lines; \\(\\ell_h\\) is non‑sunny and replaces a non‑sunny line, so the count remains \\(k\\).\n\n* **(ii) Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n  Delete the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (also non‑sunny) and add the two new lines:\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{n+1}^{\\,(k+1)}=\n  \\bigl(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\setminus\\{x=1\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_s\\}.\n  \\]\n  Now the family contains the original \\(k\\) sunny lines plus the newly introduced sunny line \\(\\ell_s\\); hence it has \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.3.  Verification of the new families\n\n*Distinctness.*  \n  – \\(\\ell_h\\) is the unique horizontal line in the new family because the only horizontal line that existed before was \\(y=1\\), which we have removed.  \n  – By (4.2) the slope \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(\\ell_s\\) differs from the slope of every sunny line already present; it also differs from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), so \\(\\ell_s\\) cannot coincide with any non‑sunny line of the original family. Hence the three sets of lines are pairwise disjoint, giving exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage of points.*  \n  – Every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered because we removed only a line that is not the unique carrier of any of those points: the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (resp. the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) is parallel to many other lines of the original family, and each of its points also lies on at least one other line (otherwise that point would be the sole reason for the presence of the vertical/horizontal line, contradicting Lemma 1).  \n  – The new point \\(P_h=(n+1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_h\\); the new point \\(P_s=(1,n+1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).  \n\nThus the constructed families satisfy all requirements.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base families for \\(n=3\\) provide configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) configurations exist for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nApplying the construction of Section 4 to a configuration with a given \\(k\\) yields:\n\n* a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, and\n* a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently, for \\(n+1\\) we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).  \nBy induction the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\nThus for each such \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
