"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nThe previous answer contained several incorrect statements (in particular Lemma\u202f1 and the base families for \\(n=3\\)).  \nI now give a completely rigorous solution.  \nThe final result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exist }n\\text{ distinct lines covering all points }(a,b)\\;(a+b\\le n+1)\\text{ and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}.}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **The point set** \u2013 for a fixed \\(n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **A universal covering by \\(n\\) lines** \u2013 for each integer \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;y=\\frac{-(i-1)}{i-1}\\,x+(i+1)= -x+i+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\):\n   * if \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) or \\(\\ell_a\\) respectively;\n   * if \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\), which is exactly \\(\\ell_{\\max\\{a,b\\}}\\).\n\n   Thus \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) is a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(-1\\) for every \\(i\\); consequently **all** these lines are *non\u2011sunny*.\n\n3. **Turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one** \u2013 pick any line \\(\\ell_i\\) from the covering and replace it by a line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\;y=\\alpha_i x+\\beta_i ,\n   \\]\n\n   where the real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) is chosen so that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha_i\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already present}.\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The constant \\(\\beta_i\\) is then forced by the requirement that \\(s_i\\) pass through the point \\((i,1)\\) (or through \\((1,i)\\); either choice works).  \n   Because \\(\\alpha_i\\neq -1\\), the new line is **sunny**; because \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0\\) it is not horizontal, and because \\(\\alpha_i\\) is different from the slopes of all previously introduced sunny lines, the new line is distinct from every line already in the family.\n\n   Moreover, the point \\((i,1)\\) (resp. \\((1,i)\\)) that was previously covered by \\(\\ell_i\\) is still covered by the new line \\(s_i\\). All other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(\\ell_i\\) remain covered because each such point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) also lies on another line \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j=\\max\\{a,b\\}\\neq i\\).\n\n4. **Inductive construction of families with any number of sunny lines** \u2013 start from the non\u2011sunny covering \\(\\mathcal C_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines).  \n   Repeatedly apply the replacement described in step\u202f3 to distinct lines of the current family. After \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and still covering \\(T_n\\).  \n   The replacement can be performed as long as there remains a non\u2011sunny line to replace, so we can reach any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the construction** \u2013 the three facts that guarantee the correctness of the whole procedure are:\n   * **(Covering)** every point of \\(T_n\\) is on at least one line of the initial family \\(\\mathcal C_n\\); the replacement step never removes a point from the covering because each point that lies on the replaced line also lies on another line of the family.\n   * **(Distinctness)** the slope condition (3.1) guarantees that a newly introduced sunny line never coincides with any line already present.\n   * **(Sunny count)** each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one, so after \\(k\\) replacements we have precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) satisfies the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A basic covering by \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=-x+i+1 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\); indeed  \n\n\\[\n1=-i+i+1,\\qquad i=-1+i+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts \\(i+1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) because \\(\\ell_b\\) contains \\((1,b)\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)=(a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\) because \\(\\ell_a\\) contains \\((a,1)\\).\n* If \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) then let \\(j=\\max\\{a,b\\}\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(j\\le n\\).  \n  The line \\(\\ell_j\\) contains the two points \\((j,1)\\) and \\((1,j)\\); the unique line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) is exactly the line through \\((j,1)\\) and \\((1,j)\\), i.e. \\(\\ell_j\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nAll the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently they are **non\u2011sunny** (they are parallel to the prohibited line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\) and contains **zero** sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nAssume we already have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nPick a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\) that is non\u2011sunny (its slope is either \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or it is vertical).  \n\nWe shall construct a new line \\(s\\) satisfying three properties:\n\n1. \\(s\\) passes through a point of \\(\\ell\\) that also belongs to \\(T_n\\);\n2. \\(s\\) is sunny, i.e. its slope \\(\\alpha\\) satisfies \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(s\\) is not vertical;\n3. \\(\\alpha\\) is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) contains at least one point of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be useless), we may choose such a point.  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is vertical we take the point \\((1,\\,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\); if \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal we take \\((a,1)\\); if \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\) we take \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\). In every case the chosen point has the form \\((p,1)\\) or \\((1,p)\\) for some integer \\(p\\) with \\(1\\le p\\le n\\).\n\nLet \\((p,1)\\) be the chosen point (the case \\((1,p)\\) is completely analogous).  \nSelect a real number \\(\\alpha\\) that satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal F .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because only finitely many slopes are forbidden.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\ns:\\;y=\\alpha x+(1-\\alpha p).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3.2) guarantees that \\(s\\) passes through \\((p,1)\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, hence \\(s\\) is sunny.  \nCondition (3.1) guarantees that \\(s\\) does not coincide with any sunny line already present; it also cannot coincide with any non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) because every non\u2011sunny line has slope \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or is vertical.\n\nNow replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(s\\) and obtain a new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{s\\}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n*Preservation of the covering.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(\\ell\\) remain covered:\n\n* the point \\((p,1)\\) (or \\((1,p)\\)) lies on the new line \\(s\\);\n* any other point \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) satisfies \\(a,b\\ge 2\\); such a point also lies on the line \\(\\ell_{\\max\\{a,b\\}}\\) of the original basic covering (2.1), and that line is still present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) because we removed only \\(\\ell\\).\n\nAll points that were not on \\(\\ell\\) are untouched. Hence \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nThe number of sunny lines increases by exactly one, because \\(\\ell\\) was non\u2011sunny and \\(s\\) is sunny.\n\nThus the replacement operation converts a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one while preserving distinctness, the covering property, and increasing the sunny count by one.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Obtaining families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the basic covering \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) of \u00a72, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply the replacement described in \u00a73 to any non\u2011sunny line of the current family.  \nAfter the first replacement we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_n(1)\\) with exactly one sunny line; after the second replacement we obtain \\(\\mathcal F_n(2)\\), and so on.\n\nBecause the basic covering contains \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, we can perform at most \\(n\\) replacements, producing families \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines by construction.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. No other values of \\(k\\) are possible, because a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]"

