 sunny lines, we **replace** a diagonal \\(d_{c}\\) by a sunny line that also passes through a point of that diagonal.  \n   For a fixed \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) pick any integer \\(p\\) with \\(1\\le p\\le c-1\\) (so that \\((p,\\,c-p)\\in d_c\\)).  \n   Choose a slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) that is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present, and define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{p,\\alpha}:\\;y=\\alpha(x-p)+(c-p).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(s_{p,\\alpha}\\) passes through \\((p,c-p)\\in d_c\\); therefore after removing \\(d_c\\) and inserting \\(s_{p,\\alpha}\\) the point \\((p,c-p)\\) stays covered.  \n   Every other point of \\(d_c\\) has the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\). Such a point also lies on the diagonal \\(d_{a+1}\\) (because \\(a+1\\le c\\) and \\(a+1\\ge 2\\)) **or** on the diagonal \\(d_{b+1}\\); at least one of these two diagonals is different from \\(d_c\\) and remains in the family. Hence all points of the removed diagonal remain covered by the other diagonals.\n\n   Consequently the replacement operation preserves the covering property, keeps the lines distinct, and increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one.\n\n4. **Inductive construction** – start from the basic covering \\(\\mathcal D_n=\\{d_2,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines).  \n   Repeatedly apply the replacement described in step 3 to distinct diagonals. After \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and still covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Because there are \\(n\\) diagonals to begin with, we can perform at most \\(n\\) replacements; however the last replacement would have to remove the diagonal \\(d_2\\), whose only point is \\((1,1)\\). The point \\((1,1)\\) would then be uncovered (no other diagonal contains it), so the replacement is **not allowed**. Hence the maximal number of sunny lines attainable is \\(n-1\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)** – suppose a configuration with \\(n\\) sunny lines existed. Since a sunny line is never parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\), it cannot be of the form \\(x+y=c\\). Therefore none of the diagonals \\(d_c\\) would be present. But the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d_2\\) and on no other line of the form \\(x+y=c\\). Any sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\) would have slope different from \\(-1\\); however such a line would miss at least one other point of the set (for example \\((2,1)\\) or \\((1,2)\\)), because a single non‑parallel line cannot contain both \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) while also covering the point \\((1,2)\\) with the same slope. Hence a covering by only sunny lines is impossible, and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\nThus the attainable values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\text{ are attainable, and no other }k\\text{ is possible}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A basic covering by non‑sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_c\\) is parallel to the prohibited line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n:=\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\), hence \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d_c\\). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is a valid configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(d_c\\) be a diagonal belonging to \\(\\mathcal F\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) (the case \\(c=2\\) will be treated separately).  \n\nChoose an integer \\(p\\) with \\(1\\le p\\le c-1\\); then the point  \n\n\\[\nP:=(p,\\;c-p)\\in d_c\\cap T_n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nSelect a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal F .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\ns_{p,\\alpha}:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-p\\bigr)+(c-p).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3.3) guarantees that \\(s_{p,\\alpha}\\) passes through the point \\(P\\). Because \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\) the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); because \\(\\alpha\\neq0\\) it is not horizontal; and it is not vertical by construction. Hence \\(s_{p,\\alpha}\\) is **sunny**.\n\nNow form  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{d_c\\})\\cup\\{s_{p,\\alpha}\\}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Points that were on \\(d_c\\).*  \nThe distinguished point \\(P\\) remains covered because it lies on \\(s_{p,\\alpha}\\).  \nAny other point \\(Q=(a,b)\\in d_c\\cap T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq p\\) then \\(a\\le c-1\\) and the diagonal \\(d_{a+1}\\) (which equals \\(x+y=a+1\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) and is different from \\(d_c\\); moreover \\(Q\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\ge a+1\\), so \\(Q\\) also lies on \\(d_{a+1}\\) **iff** \\(b=1\\). In the complementary case \\(b\\neq 1\\) we use \\(d_{b+1}\\). In either case at least one of the two diagonals \\(d_{a+1}\\) or \\(d_{b+1}\\) is distinct from \\(d_c\\) and contains \\(Q\\). Since we removed only \\(d_c\\), that diagonal is still present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\). Hence every point of the removed diagonal remains covered.\n\n*Points that were not on \\(d_c\\).*  \nThey are untouched by the replacement, so they stay covered.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nCondition (3.2) ensures that the new line \\(s_{p,\\alpha}\\) does not coincide with any sunny line already present. It cannot coincide with any remaining diagonal because every diagonal has slope \\(-1\\) while \\(\\alpha\\neq-1\\). Hence all lines in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) are distinct.\n\nThus the replacement operation preserves the covering, keeps the number of lines equal to \\(n\\), and **increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one** (the removed diagonal was non‑sunny, the inserted line is sunny).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive construction of families with any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nStart from the basic covering \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines).  \nApply the replacement described in Section 3 to a diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\). After the first replacement we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_n(1)\\) with exactly one sunny line. Repeating the process on a different diagonal each time yields families \\(\\mathcal F_n(k)\\) for \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(d_2\\) consists solely of the point \\((1,1)\\). If we attempted to replace \\(d_2\\) by a sunny line, the point \\((1,1)\\) would remain covered (the new line passes through it), but **no other diagonal contains \\((1,1)\\)**, so after the removal of \\(d_2\\) the point \\((1,1)\\) would be covered only by that sunny line. This is permissible; however, we must also ensure that every other point of \\(d_2\\) (there are none) stays covered, which is vacuous. The subtlety is that after we have already replaced all diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\), the only remaining non‑sunny line is \\(d_2\\). Replacing it would give a configuration with **\\(n\\) sunny lines**. We now show that such a configuration cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal G\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nConsider the point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\). Since no line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is allowed, the line of \\(\\mathcal G\\) that contains \\((1,1)\\) must have a slope \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\).  \n\nNow look at the point \\((2,1)\\in T_n\\). If the same line of \\(\\mathcal G\\) also contained \\((2,1)\\), its slope would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{2-1}=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden (horizontal). Hence \\((2,1)\\) must lie on a **different** line of \\(\\mathcal G\\). That line cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so its slope is some \\(\\beta\\neq0\\).  \n\nSimilarly, the point \\((1,2)\\) must lie on a third line of \\(\\mathcal G\\) with slope \\(\\gamma\\neq0\\).  \n\nThus at least three distinct lines are needed to cover the three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,2)\\). Repeating the same argument for the points \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) forces two further distinct lines, and so on. In total we would need more than \\(n\\) lines to cover all points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. A more concise contradiction is obtained by observing that the diagonal \\(d_2\\) (the set \\(\\{(1,1)\\}\\)) is the only line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) that contains \\((1,1)\\); any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) fails to contain any other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=2\\) (there is none) but also fails to contain simultaneously both \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) because those two points have different slopes relative to \\((1,1)\\). Hence a covering by only sunny lines is impossible.\n\nConsequently the maximal number of sunny lines attainable is \\(n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) the construction described in Sections 2–4 yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. No configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists, as proved in Section 5."
