l \\(j\\le k\\le n\\)).  \n  Consequently each sunny line \\(M_j\\) covers at least the two points \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\).\n\n* **Step 3 (completing the covering).**  \n  After the \\(k\\) sunny lines have been introduced, the points of \\(S_n\\) that are not yet covered are exactly those lying on the vertical lines\n  \\[\n  x=i\\qquad\\text{with }i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n-k\\) such vertical lines, and they are all non‑sunny.  \n  Adding these \\(n-k\\) vertical lines to the already placed \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them (by construction).\n\n* **Step 4 (verification of distinctness).**  \n  The slopes of the sunny lines \\(M_j\\) are all different, and none of them coincides with a vertical line, so all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\nThus for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); geometrically it is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (non‑sunny covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and satisfy  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore it lies on the line \\(L_a\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (a family of sunny lines)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{j+1}\