l\\) would be \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nHence a sunny line meets each diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad 2\\le c\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nin **at most one** point.\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); all other diagonals contain fewer.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nMoreover, because a sunny line is not parallel to \\(D_{n+1}\\), it cannot contain **all** \\(n\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\); it misses at least one of them. Therefore a sunny line meets at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains }\\le n-1\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Points of a non‑sunny line inside \\(T_n\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n* If it is horizontal, say \\(y=b_0\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-b_0\\); thus at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on it.\n* The same bound holds for a vertical line.\n* If it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c_0\\), then \\(c_0\\) must be an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); the line contains exactly the points of the diagonal \\(D_{c_0}\\), whose cardinality is at most \\(n\\).\n\nHence every non‑sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A non‑sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the total number of covered points\n\nSuppose that among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(s\\) are sunny and \\(n-s\\) are non‑sunny.  \nUsing (2) and (3) we obtain the estimate\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_i\\cap T_n\\bigr|\n\\le s\\,(n-1)+(n-s)\\,n\n= n^2 - s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are required to cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\), the left–hand side of 