nstruction showing attainability of every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Take two non\u2011sunny lines*  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{h}: y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{v}: x=1 .\n   \\]\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the first row or first column.  \n   *The remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   *These points lie in the smaller triangle  \n     \\[\n     T_{n-2}=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le n-1\\}\n     \\]\n     after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\).  \n   *It is well\u2011known (and easy to verify) that the \\(n-2\\) lines  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\n     \\]\n     are all sunny (their slopes are distinct and none equals \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and together they pass through every point of \\(T_{n-2}\\).  \n   *Thus the family  \n     \\[\n     \\{\\ell_{h},\\ell_{v}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\n     \\]\n     contains exactly \\(k+2\\) lines, of which the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are sunny and the two fixed lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n   *If we need fewer than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, we simply omit some of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\); the uncovered points of \\(T_{n-2}\\) can then be covered by adding extra non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) without increasing the number of sunny lines.  \n   *Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we can produce a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) is also sufficient. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n