2k\\) points have been removed, leaving at least  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\ge n-k\n     \\]\n\n     points (the inequality follows from \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n   - Cover the remaining points with \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines as follows:  \n     for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) draw the line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n     These \\(n\\) lines together cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n     Delete any \\(k\\) of them; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines still cover every point that was not already on a sunny line, because each point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on exactly one line of the family \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\).  \n\n   Thus we obtain a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since a construction exists for every \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) and no value outside this interval can occur (there are only \\(n\\) lines), the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is the number of integer solutions of \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); a standard count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line be given by \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq 0,\\;m\\neq -1\\) and \\(m\\) finite.  \nSuppose three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) lie on it.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(a_i,b_i\\) are integers, the two fractions are rational numbers with integer numerator and denominator.  \nIf the common value \\(m\\) were neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), the three points would have to be collinear with a slope different from the three forbidden slopes.  \nBut the set \\(\\mathcal P\\) is contained in the union of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\{y=b\\},\\qquad \\{x=a\\},\\qquad \\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(b,a,c\\) are integers.  \nAny three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that are collinear must belong to one of those families, because the only possible slopes between points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nHence a line with a different slope cannot contain three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non\u2011sunny lines can contain arbitrarily many points of \\(\\mathcal P\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,b),(2,b),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n+1-b\\) points, which can be as large as \\(n\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains \\(n+1-a\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains \\(c-1\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Choosing sunny lines.**  \n   Because of (1) we have \\(|\\mathcal P|\\ge 2k\\).  \n   Select \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs of points from \\(\\mathcal P\\); this is possible since we may simply take the first \\(2k\\) points in any ordering of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   For each pair \\(\\{(a_i,b_i),(a'_i,b'_i)\\}\\) draw the unique line passing through them.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 this line is sunny (its slope cannot be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n   Denote these lines by \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n2. **Removing covered points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P'=\\mathcal P\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Each sunny