es on exactly one of the \\(M_c\\).  \n   Delete any \\(k\\) of these lines; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines still cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that has not already been covered by a sunny line, because each uncovered point belongs to a unique line of the family \\(\\{M_c\\}\\).\n\n   The total collection consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(n-k\\) remaining \\(M_c\\) lines – altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Since a configuration contains precisely \\(n\\) lines, no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible value of \\(c=a+b\\) (which ranges from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)), the number of integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) there are \\(c-1\\) such solutions, hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines intersect \\(\\mathcal P\\) in at most two points)\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0\\), \\(m\\neq\\infty\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\).  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nThe slope between any two of them is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_j-b_i}{a_j-a_i}\\in\\mathbb Q .\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points are collinear, the three slopes are equal; denote this common value by \\(m\\).  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0\\).  \nFrom \\(b_i = (p/q)a_i + q_i\\) (where