\); consequently \\(q\\mid q_i\\).  \nThus each point satisfies an equation of the form \\(b = (p/q)a + r\\) with integer \\(r\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the possible values of \\((a,b)\\) lie inside the triangle bounded by the lines \\(x=0\\), \\(y=0\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nA line of slope \\(p/q\\) intersecting this triangle in three lattice points would have to be parallel to one of its sides; otherwise the segment between the two extreme lattice points would contain a third lattice point by the integer‑step property of lattice lines.  \nThe three sides have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nSince \\(m\\) is assumed different from these three numbers, a contradiction arises.  \nHence a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\); it can contain at most two. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines can contain many points of \\(\\mathcal P\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,b),(2,b),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n+1-b\\) points, which can be as large as \\(n\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) contains \\(n+1-a\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains \\(c-1\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Choosing sunny lines.**  \n   Because of (1) we have \\(|\\mathcal P|\\ge 2k\\).  \n   Select \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs of points from \\(\\mathcal P\\); for instance, list the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) in any order and take the first \\(2k\\) of them, pairing them consecutively.  \n   For each pair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) draw the unique line \\(L_i\\) passing through the two points.  \n   By Lemma 1 each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and it covers exactly the two points that defined it.\n\n2. **Removing covered points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P'=\\mathcal P\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Since each sunny line covers precisely two points,  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P'|=|\\mathcal P|-2k\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\ge n-k,\n   \\]\n\n   the last inequality being equivalent to \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Covering the remainder with non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_c:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\{M_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for exactly one such \\(c\\); consequently each point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on exactly one line of this family.\n\n   Delete arbitrarily \\(k\\) of the lines \\(M_c\\); denote the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_{\\,n-k}\\).  \n   Because the points already covered by the sunny lines have been removed from \\(\\mathcal P\\), every point of \\(\\mathcal P'\\) still belongs to a unique line among the \\(N_j\\).\n\n4. **Verification of the whole configuration.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,N_1,\\dots ,N_{\\,n-k}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Exactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are of slope \\(-1\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \n   By construction each point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies either on one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) or on one of the non‑sunny lines \\(N_j\\).  \n   Hence the two conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore all these values are admissible.  \nConversely, a configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot 