            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise distinct and each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering requirement with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line can meet at most one point of \\(S_n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{If a line }\\ell\\text{ is sunny, then }\\ell\\cap S_n\\text{ contains at most one point.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) where the slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq 0,\\qquad m\\neq \\infty,\\qquad m\\neq -1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\).  \nFrom the equation of the line we have  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in S_n\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a,\\qquad b'\\le n+1-a'.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two inequalities in (3) yields  \n\n\\[\nb'-b\\le -(a'-a).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInterchanging the roles of the two points gives the reverse inequality  \n\n\\[\nb'-b\\ge -(a'-a).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb'-b=-(a'-a).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (6) into (2) gives \\(m=-1\\), contradicting (1).  \nHence a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_n\\); it can contain at most one. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Any non‑sunny line also meets at most one point of \\(S_n\\)\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates (they range from \\(1\\) up to \\(n\\)), so a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nAnalogously, the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(S_n\\) are distinct, so a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis also contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIf two points of \\(S_n\\) lay on the same line \\(x+y=c\\) they would have the same sum \\(a+b\\).  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one point of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\) (namely \\((1,s-1)\\) after ordering).  \nThus a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can meet at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nConsequently **every** line, sunny or not, can cover at most one element of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of a configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\)\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\).  \nIf we had \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\), each line could contribute at most one point (by the discussion in Sections 2–3).  \nHence at most \\(n\\) points could be covered, contradicting \\(|S_n|>n\\).  \nThe contradiction shows that **no** collection of \\(n\\) lines containing a sunny line can satisfy the covering requirement.  \nTherefore any admissible configuration must have \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nThe construction of Section 1 exhibits a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\); the impossibility proof of Sections 2–4 shows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) exists.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
