            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe are asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct, each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and\nhence not sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition with\nexactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – a line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\forall\\;\\text{line }\\ell,\\qquad |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a line in one of the four possible forms.\n\n* **Vertical line** \\(x=c\\).  \n  Because the \\(x\\)-coordinate of any point of \\(S_n\\) lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n  the only possible values of \\(c\\) are integers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n  For a fixed \\(c\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives\n  \\(b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the line.\n\n* **Horizontal line** \\(y=c\\).  \n  By symmetry the same bound \\(n\\) holds.\n\n* **Line of slope \\(-1\\)**, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  For a given integer \\(c\\) the solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are\n  \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\) of them.\n  The additional restriction \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\), so\n  \\(c-1\\le n\\).  Hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on such a line.\n\n* **All other lines** have an equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with slope\n  \\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq\\infty,\\;m\\neq-1\\).  \n  For each integer \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is at most one integer\n  \\(b\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation, because the slope is not\n  zero.  Thus the line can meet at most one point in each column, i.e. at most\n  \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIn every case the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on the line does not exceed \\(n\\).\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – if a non‑vertical line appears, more than \\(n\\) lines are needed\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If among the }n\\text{ lines at least one is not vertical,\n then the covering of }S_n\\text{ requires at least }n+1\\text{ lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the contrary: we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering\n\\(S_n\\) and at least one of them, say \\(\\ell_1\\), is **not** vertical.\n\nBecause \\(\\ell_1\\) is not vertical, it meets each vertical line\n\\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) in at most one point.  \nConsequently there exists at least one column, say \\(x=j\\), that contains **no**\npoint of \\(\\ell_1\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell_1\\) would contain \\(n\\) distinct points,\nwhich would force its slope to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=j\\) must therefore be covered by the remaining\n\\(n-1\\) lines.  There are exactly \\(n+1-j\\) such points (they are\n\\((j,1),(j,2),\\dots ,(j,n+1-j)\\)).  By Lemma 1 each of the remaining lines can\ncontain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); in particular a single line cannot\ncover all \\(n+1-j\\) points of the column unless \\(j=1\\).  If \\(j=1\\) the column\ncontains \\(n\\) points, and covering them would require at least one line that\nis **vertical** (otherwise the line would intersect the column in at most one\npoint).  Hence at least one of the remaining lines is vertical.\n\nNow consider the vertical line that covers the points of column \\(x=j\\).  It\ncovers exactly those \\(n+1-j\\) points and no others, because any other point of\n\\(S_n\\) has a different \\(x\\)-coordinate.  Remove this vertical line and the\ncolumn \\(x=j\\) from the problem; we are left with a set of points that is\nisomorphic to \\(S_{\\,n-1}\\) (shift the coordinates by \\((-1,0)\\)).  The number of\nremaining lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\nRepeating the same argument inductively we see that each time we encounter a\nnon‑vertical line we are forced to introduce an additional vertical line,\n and after \\(t\\) repetitions we would need \\(n+t\\) lines to cover the\ncorresponding reduced set.  Since we started with only \\(n\\) lines, the only\npossibility is that **no** non‑vertical line appears at all.\n\nThus the assumption that a non‑vertical line is present leads to a contradiction;\nhence all \\(n\\) covering lines must be vertical. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determination of the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we deduce that any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) must consist\nentirely of ver