es*: the line can be written \\(y=mx+q\\) with\n\\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1\\).  For each integer \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is at most\none integer \\(b\\) satisfying the equation, so the line meets at most one\npoint in each column and therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n*Let \\(v\\) be the number of vertical lines among \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).\nThen*\n\\[\nvn\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|-(n-v)n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe \\(v\\) vertical lines together contain at most \\(vn\\) points of \\(S_n\\) by\nLemma 1.  The remaining \\(n-v\\) lines each contain at most \\(n\\) points, so they\ncover at most \\((n-v)n\\) points.  Since all \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points must be\ncovered, inequality (2.1) follows. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA sunny line is necessarily non‑vertical; therefore the number of sunny lines\nis at most \\(n-v\\).  From (2.1) we obtain\n\\[\nvn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-v)n\n\\Longrightarrow\nv\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(v\\) is integral,\n\\[\nv\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nk=n-v\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThus every admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sharpness: construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nv=n-k\\qquad\\Bigl(v\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n**Vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,v).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed\n\\(v\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y = j\\,x + (1-jv).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(j\\) is a non‑zero integer different from \\(-1\\); consequently each\n\\(M_j\\) is sunny.  Moreover,\n\\[\nM_j\\bigl(v+j,\\,1\\bigr) \\quad\\text{holds,}\n\\]\nso \\(M_j\\) passes through the point \\((v+j,1)\\) of the column \\(x=v+j\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*Column \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le v\\).*  \nAll points of this column belong to the vertical line \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=v+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).*  \nThe points of \\(S_n\\) in this column are\n\\((v+j,1), (v+j,2),\\dots ,(v+j,n+1-(v+j))\\).\nThe line \\(M_j\\) supplies the point \\((v+j,1)\\).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1-(v+j)\\) the point\n\\((v+j,t)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_t\\) (because \\(t\\le v\\) by the\ninequality \\(v\\ge\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\)).  Hence every point of the column is\ncovered.\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which (\\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\)) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists are\nexactly those satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
