ed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we must exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the following configurations exist:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x=2\n  \\]\n  cover the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): replace \\(L_3\\) by the sunny line \\(L_3':\\;y=2x-2\\).  \n  The three lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2x-2\\) are distinct and still cover all points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}= \\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq S_n\\);\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nWe prove that for \\(n+1\\) and every  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k'\\le (n+1)-2=n-1,\n\\]\n\nsuch a family exists.\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\cup\\{(1,n+1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the only new point to be covered is \\((1,n+1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction\n\nFix \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-1\\).  \nStart from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) where \\(k\\) will be chosen later.\n\nWe add a new line \\(M\\) that passes through \\((1,n+1)\\). Three possibilities are available:\n\n1. **Vertical line** \\(M_V:\\;x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n2. **Diagonal line