 construction works for every \\(n\\ge3\\), proving that \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A family of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the following three families of lines, none of which is sunny:\n\n* horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) (\\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\));\n* vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (\\(i\\in\\mathbb Z\\));\n* diagonal lines \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).\n\nTheir slopes are respectively \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal N\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)+(1)+(1)=n-1\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, all non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Points of \\(S_n\\) covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\)\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies one of the following:\n\n* **Case A:** \\(a+b\\le n-2\\).  \n  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n* **Case B:** \\(a+b=n-1\\).  \n  If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);  \n  if \\(a=1\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n  (Both possibilities occur because \\(a,b\\ge1\\).)\n\n* **Case C:** \\(a+b=n\\).  \n  If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) the point is again covered by \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).  \n  The only points of this type not already covered are those with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); they form the set  \n\n  \\[\n  R_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n,\\;a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b=n-a\\ge2\\), we have \\(2\\le a\\le n-2\\).  \n  Hence \\(R_n\\) contains at most one point, namely \\((2,n-2)\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\); for \\(n=3\\) the set is empty.\n\n* **Case D:** \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n  Here either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\); consequently the point lies on \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\) except possibly the single point of \\(R_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Adding a sunny line\n\nSelect a line \\(\\ell\\) whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); such a line is sunny by definition.  \n\n*If \\(R_n=\\varnothing\\)*, choose any sunny line (for instance \\(y=2x\\)).  \n\n*If \\(R_n=\\{(2,n-2)\\}\\)*, choose the unique line of sunny slope passing through \\((2,n-2)\\); for example the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-(n-2)=m\\bigl(x-2\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) is any real number not equal to \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nBecause we are free to pick any such \\(m\\), a sunny line through the required point always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification of the construction\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal N\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, and we have added one sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Hence the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); only \\(\\ell\\) has a different slope. Therefore exactly one line is sunny, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Covering property.*  \nFrom the case analysis in Section\u202f2, every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) or, if it is the (possible) unique element of \\(R_n\\), to the sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Hence the covering condition required in the problem statement is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly one sunny line.\n\nTherefore the integer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=1}\n\\]\n\nis always admissible. The determination of whether other values of \\(k\\) can occur remains open."
