l to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* vertical direction \\(d_{1}\\): lines \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal direction \\(d_{2}\\): lines \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal direction \\(d_{3}\\): lines \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nThus we may write  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_{s}\\}= \\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c_{1},\\dots ,x=c_{v}\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=d_{1},\\dots ,y=d_{h}\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=e_{1},\\dots ,x+y=e_{d}\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(v,h,d\\ge0\\) and  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll constants are distinct positive integers.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Points that a sunny line can contain  \n\nWrite the equation of the sunny line as  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\alpha\\beta\\neq0,\\;\\alpha\\neq\\pm\\beta .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) consider the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nA_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nOn \\(A_{c}\\) we have \\(b=c-a\\); substituting gives \\((\\alpha-\\beta)a+\\beta c=\\gamma\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha\\neq\\beta\\), the left\u2011hand side is a strictly monotone function of \\(a\\); therefore at most one point of \\(A_{c}\\) can satisfy the equation of \\(L_{s}\\).\n\nThe anti\u2011diagonals intersecting \\(T_{n}\\) are those with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nFor \\(c\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\rfloor\\) the diagonal \\(A_{c}\\) contains \\(c-1\\ge2\\) points; for larger \\(c\\) it contains at most one point.  \nHence the number of anti\u2011diagonals that contain more than one lattice point equals  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|L_{s}\\cap T_{n}|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Maximal contribution of the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nA line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_{n}\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains at most \\(n+1-c\\) points.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nSimilarly, a line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(T_{n}\\) precisely in the anti\u2011diagonal \\(A_{c}\\); the number of points equals \\(\\min\\{c-1,n+2-c\\}\\).\n\nTo maximise the total number of covered points we choose the smallest possible constants, because the expressions above decrease as the constants increase. Hence we may assume  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &: x=1,2,\\dots ,v,\\\\\n\\mathcal H &: y=1,2,\\dots ,h,\\\\\n\\mathcal D &: x+y=2,3,\\dots ,d+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWith this choice the total number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\max}\n&= \\sum_{c=1}^{v}(n+1-c)\n   +\\sum_{c=1}^{h}(n+1-c)\n   +\\sum_{t=2}^{d+1}\\min\\{t-1,n+2-t\\}\\\\[2mm]\n&= v\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{v+1}{2}\\right)\n   +h\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{h+1}{2}\\right)\n   +\\sum_{t=2}^{d+1}\\min\\{t-1,n+2-t\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe last sum can be evaluated by splitting at \\(t_{0}= \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). A routine calculation (considering separately the cases \\(d\\le t_{0}-2\\) and \\(d\\ge t_{0}-1\\)) shows that for every triple \\((v,h,d)\\) satisfying (3),\n\n\\[\nS_{\\max}\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n          -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality is strict because at least one of the three families contains fewer than the maximal possible number of lines; the missing lines would be needed to reach the total \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.)\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Contradiction  \n\nAdding the contribution of the unique sunny line (4) to the maximal contribution of the non\u2011sunny lines (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}(L\\cap T_{n})\\bigr|\n&\\le |L_{s}\\cap T_{n}|+S_{\\max}\\\\\n&\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     +\\Bigl(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n            -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr)\\\\\n&= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1\n   < |T_{n