            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n| = 1+2+\\dots + n = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence there are exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) admissible points on that line.  \nIf the line is vertical, \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), an analogous argument gives at most \\(n\\) points.  \nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\), it can be written as \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\). The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\); for each integer \\(c\\) there are at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny lines)\n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nThe slopes between successive points are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\\qquad \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nSince all three points lie on the same line, these two slopes are equal.  \nBoth numerators and denominators are integers, so the common slope is a rational number whose denominator divides the difference of the \\(a\\)-coordinates.  \nBecause the three points are lattice points lying inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only possibilities for a line passing through three such points are slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (corresponding respectively to horizontal, vertical, or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) lines).  \nThus a line with any other slope can meet \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nSuppose we have a collection \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition of the problem, and let  \n\n\\[\nk = |\\{L\\in\\mathcal{L}\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines.  \nDenote by \\(n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) must lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).  \nCounting the points covered by the lines, Lemma 1 gives that each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, and Lemma 2 gives that each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k) \\;\\ge\\; |\\mathcal{P}_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying (2),\n\n\\[\n2k + n^{2} - nk \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; (2-n)k \\ge -\\frac{n}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; k \\le n-2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the last step uses \\(n\\ge 3\\) (so \\(2-n