ope \\(-1\\);\n     hence they are sunny.\n\n   - The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are taken from the horizontal family\n     \\(\\mathcal H\\):\n     \\[\n     H_j : y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n     \\]\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n   - If \\(b>n-k\\) then \\(a+b\\ge n+1-(n-k)+1 = k+2\\).  \n     Consequently \\((a,b)\\) is not on any of the chosen horizontals, but\n     because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n     In particular the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal\n     \\(x+y=a+b\\).  Since the set \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\) contains exactly the\n     points of the diagonal with sum \\(n+1\\), the line \\(L_{a+b-(n+1)}\\)\n     (which exists because \\(a+b-(n+1)\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)) passes through\n     \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the sunny lines.\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the\n   \\(n\\) constructed lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   For the special case \\(n=3\\) we may simply take the three sunny lines\n   \\(L_i\\) through the three points of \\(T_3\\) with distinct slopes; this\n   yields \\(k=3\\).  The same construction above works for any\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n   Hence every integer \\(k\\) satisfying the upper bound is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its equation as \\(y=\\alpha x+\\beta\\) with\n\\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  Suppose that \\(L\\) contains two distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\).  Then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\alpha .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha\\neq 0,-1\\), the difference \\(b_2-b_1\\) cannot be \\(0\\) nor\n\\(-(a_2-a_1)\\).  Consequently the integer points of \\(L\\) inside the\ntriangle \\(T_n\\) are spaced at least two units apart in the \\(x\\)-direction.\nHence at most one point of each column \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) can belong to\n\\(L\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf all \\(n\\) given lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would\nbe at most \\(n(n-1)\\).  For \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\nn(n-1)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|,\n\\]\n\nso the whole set \\(T_n\\) could not be covered.  Hence at least one line must\nbe non\u2011sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge 4).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have \\(n(n-1)=|T_3|=6\\); inequality (1) does not forbid the\npossibility that all three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) (or \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) when \\(n=3\\)).\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Sunny lines\n\nChoose the \\(k\\) points\n\n\\[\nP_i=\\bigl(i,\\;n+1-i\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a distinct slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) from the set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) and define the line\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-\\bigl(n+1-i\\bigr)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha_i\\neq 0,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the distinct slopes\nguarantee that the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise different.\n\n#### 2.2  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the horizontal lines\n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and\n\\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\{H_j\\}\\), altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) from (5).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b>n-k\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le k\\).  \nConsequently \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and the point \\(P_a\\) defined in (3) is\n\\((a,n+1-a)\\).  The line \\(L_a\\) from (4) passes through both \\(P_a\\) and\n\\((a,b)\\) because they share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\).  Hence\n\\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\nIn both cases the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\nTherefore the family of \\(n\\) lines covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\n- For every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) the inequality \\(k\\le n-1\\) is necessary,\n  and the construction above shows that each integer \\(k\\) with\n  \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n- For the exceptional case \\(n=3\\) the same construction works for\n  \\(k=0,1,2\\); additionally, taking the three sunny lines through the three\n  points of \\(T_3\\) (with distinct slopes) yields a configuration with\n  \\(k=3\\).  Hence all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are possible.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one described in\nthe summary. \u220e"
